      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELtili</p>
        <p>Flat brimmed hats are being worn to a con. eiderable extent just now, and it you are thinking of having a new chapeau I should advise you to have won with a brim of that description. With regard to trimming I have seen some lately covered with velvet. But this material, as you may observe, makes the hat heavy and some ladies of my acquaintance will not tolerate such a hat at any price. Flowers have not altogether faded from view on hats, but they are not so profuse as they were a month ago. Ribbon and velvet are the principal decorations with here and there a large autumnal bloom of the dainty variety. We shall soon be thinking about wearing felt hats, but meanwhile our attention must be directed to straw.</p>
        <p>Children's garments are-ever engaging our atten- tion for as with the poor they're always with us. At this time of the year the young ones require great care in the selection of their clothes bub geßerally mothers and guardians know how to look after the children, alth,..ugh they do not always use that taste which I should like to see exercised. I thick nothing is better for this time of the year than a costume fur a child. I saw a little friend in a very charming coat. It was square cut and was trimmed wi:,h rows of velvet which descended down the frcnt and either side. The collar and revers were of the same material, and I thought altogether the garment was as suitable aed sensible as any I had seen for many a long day. The collar turned up would have afforded ample pro- tection agßingt the cold, whilst down it looked neat and gond. Sumly two greater qualities need not be wined for.</p>
        <p>McDOTJ GAL US PATENT SELF-RAISING FLOU R PREVENTS INDIGESTION.</p>
        <p>- , 114'4'e7•11‘ 1 1 ilO 0 V:d tutic.'. ''..., •, i., /4,1,1,:t7C,..•:5:. ' " q //fr'/A • riyii: .: c4e ~/,/,,7i; I:ti6;:''• 14.'W• . •,';/,',,' ',,„..,,.,,,,,.!.,4 Bodices e.nencr</p>
        <p>(coiti...i,wer (.. .10 . • 9t.,,_„,,.... _40,,,„..., V. 7. i „.‘,!'•-,, --4 c, --7-4 g----- --wd celEz) 4 IS) /,p;\ 4,7 0..4, -------,Y/ . 0 it</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <p>seem to be de, /ding more on the shoulders than formerly, but I am inclined to think that the fashion will not survive much longer seeing that the colder weather will be Coming on soon when a covering for the shoulders would be absolutely necessary, I saw a bodice a friend was wearing the other day which I liked immensely. The waist was made to IF.; tightly, of course, and then gradually enlarged in a straight line to the shoulder. The neck and chest was covered with pleated silk, tucked widthways. The bodice was just slightly double-breasted. The edge of the garment was trimmed with braid about an inch in width, whilst on the bodice were three similar rows of braid &amp;seen:ling to the waist, which was encircled with velvet.</p>
        <p>New jackets seem very slow in putting in an appearance, and the coat which reaches to the waist and slopes below it in the front in s spade. shape, bears a high collar at the back and semi- fitting, is the only model deserving of considera- tion. This I have seen made up in dark red cheviot, faced with panne of the same shade. I am reminded by a friend that light mushroom cloth mantles can be made quite up-to-date if they are renovated with a few black velvet rosettes. For instance, on a mantle made with three cloth flounces the fronts may be outlined by a black velvet ribbon caught at intervals by the black rosettes, and the collar treated in the same way with a rosette back and front. Now that the Rammer is over, hats should be picked to pieoes instead of being allowed to lie and get dusty in their boxes until the ingredients they are composed of are quite out of date. if the flowers are shaken out and sorted, they will trim an evening boiice, or they can be worn ia the hair, as we shall be having regular heed-dretaes this winter—wreaths of rib- bons and flowers, such as can be seen in the old pictures. The bits of paste should also be utilised, as they would only tarnish if left lying by. Really it appear to me the bolero seems to have taken yet another new lease of life. It is seen on nearly every gown in the shops, with short, rounded ends or with pointed ones that fall below the waist and give an excellent finish, especially when the figure is short as compared with the limbs. This is regarded with disapproval by modern tailors and dressmakers, who correct it as far as. possible by the aid of the fashionable corsetiere and by nther means. The ideal figure from the Point of ..ew of a ladies' tailor, has tne waist long, out of all proportion with the lines. With these •• notes I will conclude. Your loving. Jonas.</p>
