      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <p>.. The present season is the wettest Her Majesty has ever experienced in Scotland, and she has been compelled upon many occesions to have tea carriage closed when taking her daily outings, it procseure exceedingly die- tasteful to our Sovereign. in [sanding the present month there are, according to an old saying, always twenty-one fine days in it. It this!state or affairs obtains,the Queen will have ample opportunity of reek.ng up for lost time, and enjoying the keen bright air she lo.vs so well. That interesting pereeoage, the Ducliess of Cieveland, was, as Lady Wilreltnina Steel-nee, oue of the bearers of Her Majesty's train at her ceronatien, and also one of tee Queen's tirideemaida in 1841. The weight of years presses so lightly upoe her that she may almest be counted amongst those entitusissts who we know as "gtcless trotters." Last year she was travelliog in India and cow she is in Russia, where she perposes visiting some of the most famous monasteries of the orthodox Greek Church. London, just now, has quite a festive appearance. This is due partly to the large numeer of weddings that have taken place,and are fixed to take place this month, awl partly to the cold snap whica has merle warmer garments an absolute necessity. All the fashionable oressmakers are besieged by clients AUX.l,illa to learn the latest ideas in the world modistie, end to place their orders in good time. Until about the middle of the eighteenth century,there , were, in England, three oistiuct method's of marrying. There was marriage by putilic cc It bration in the church, or in facie eccloioe, the clnditions ot widen' were the publication ot banns, er the grsnt of a licerne to die- , pense with them, the pertormsnce of the ceremony in the parish church between the hours of eight and ! twelve, and selisn the speuses, er either of them, ! happened to he under age, ti e consent of their lawful ! guardians. There was cianclestinommfage by a clergy- man episcopally ordained, hen al: these conditions ' were tie:hetes', and again there sess consensual mar- riage. Lord Hardwicke's Act of 1753 abolished clandes- tine and consensual marriage, and wade duo celebration in church, alter banns or license, and the consent of lawns' guardians in the case of minors, essential to the validity of a mai rine. This departure was not effected without much opposition, but it retrained in force for the next seventy yeara, and it was not until 1836 that the Reeis:ration Act was passed. aid purely Meit mar- riages became legal in England In Scotland, the lswef marriage differe in many par- titulars from teat of England. According to the law of Scotland, marriage is censtitin ed by consent, and con- sent only. The varieus forms ra In4rriage, which are recosnised are, proper's- epeakir g, the different nudes in which the law eilows, or ra vires suck consent to be proeed. In lr •land, the lea, wes fcnmsrly rho same as the old law of England, or the pr-sent law of Scotland. The provisions of Lead Hard wicke's Ace were not intra. duced there until 1818. One of• the seeekers at the Wnres sser Diocesan Confer- ence, offered the bold suegestion that the clergy of the Church of England should tint tre permitted to marry ! without the consent of the Bishop. and that permission should be refused unless the clergyman had a sufficient income either from bis effice, or private means. The Salvation Army has already this rule in force with re- gard to their officers who are recoired to obtain official consent before they engeee to marry. There is however, no likelihood that a simi'ar system will ever be intro- duced into the Eng'isle Church. It is quite true that tbe stipends of curates, as well as those of many of the beneficed clergy are inacn quate for the maintenance of a family, In reigeenabte comfort, bat there is something to be said fnr the view .that the remedy would be worse than the ill if the Chu•ch Englasid were to pay to her poorer clergy" You shall not marry unntsa you can find a wife who has private meats." At the same time there is an alternative although Churchmen are slow to apply it, that of greater liberality ori the part of the laity for the maintenance of their c orgy. Teem can be no dente: teat Itin Joseph Parker, whore autobisgranhy has just been published, has something of that genius which has been dr scribed as a spark from Heaven which is also Ain to that "fine madnees "of which Drayton wrots. That he is pepillar none can doubt, and probably the explanation of that popularity is, tbet he is not only a very eble and striking preacher, hut a foremost representative of that greater tolerance in religinus'matters which is happily one of the feaurea of our time. This disposition is exactly the reverse of that of the old lady who, after caneemning the doctrines of the Univerealists and their belief, that all men will be saved, at length remarked—n But we have been taught to here; for better things." The el silty rf this view by the way, scarcely exceeded that of the lady when " prayer " Mr. Glanetone showed to Dr. Parker. This petition sought not only the erest etatermeres removal from office, but his destruction " ere Willy in this world if not in the world to come." Mr. Gladstone had no idea who cou'd have written such a" prayer," but Dr. Parker guessed the author. She was an individual whose " hatred of Popery was timnly a fanaticism" and for that reason Dr. Parker tells, us, he ;a puninted her, at one of the Thursday morning breakfasts, to sit by the side ef a Jesuit priest. Unless the French authorities interfere. buli-figt tine promises to become ae general in France as it is in Scamn and ken far, there 'Tem, little nisensitisn to prohibit these revolting exhibition«, On the other hand, the French authorities appear to lead the way in that moral decadence into which France has fallen., toth as revards her concepton of justice, and of sport, It is often urged in excuse of the Drec fire strecitios, that !ter are the work ef the military, and the officials, hut this will not do, because in France ev, n more so than in this neutstry the people and the Srat e, and the State and the neeple, are one and the same. The sperting section of the French Press hails the orespeet of teill-baitien with delightosnd weie^tn" the Peepo;al to continue the attractien, now that ths Beinnene season is over, at Es gliien, which is a suburb of Paris!. The arranet ments are not intended to palm off on the euelic, an inoffensive imitation of the hell-fight, but enntemplate the eenuire Sneuish boll fiehting which certeinle melee rorture.4 animals, even if men do not serionele risk their lives.</p>
