      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>NEWS OF THT, GAME</head>
        <p>NEWS OF THT, GAME</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Ca,ro-street Draughts Club took place at the schoolroom on Monday last. There was a very fair attendance. Drummond, the Vice-President occupied the chair. In his open ing remarks the Vice-President comnaented on the highly succeseful season throngh which the club has passed. He was pleased to be connected with the club which had the right to call itself the champion club of the district, and he hoped the au:mess whice had attended their efforts last season, would still be with them in the season which was, just about to commence. lie then called upon the secretary, Mr. D. Plinston, to read his report which was to the effect that besides being able to report the success of the club as a playing organisation, he was pleased to report that the club was an undoubted success financially. The total number of matches played throughout the season was 19, of which 15 were won 3 lost, and 1 drawn Two of the matches that were low., were with the Liverpool North Bud D.C. (the champions of Lancashire) while the other defeat was by the Warrington D.C. The drawn match was played against Manchester Central D.C. The League record was, 10 matches played, 9 won, and 1 lost. In con- clusion he remarked that he must congratulate all the members on having won such a beautiful Cup and he hoped their efforts would b 3 even more ener- getic in the future in order to keep the Cup at Cairo-street. A handicap in connection with the second team having been in progress last season,the prizes were handed out to tie winners. Ist prize, B. Dunne ; 2nd, J Ratcliffe; 3rd, e; Plinston. The officers for the ensuing season were then elected : President, F Monks, Esq ; vice-presidente, Rev W H Drummond, and J S Hill, ltrq ; secretary and treasurer, Mr D Plinston ; captain let team, Mr S Tavlin; captain 2nd teana,Mr H.Rwitt; committee, Messrs Green, Hewitt, Tavlin, Plinston, and Tom- kinson. Club nights every Monday and Thursday at 7.30. The club opens on the 16th ie,t. A hearty vote of thanks having been accorde to the Chair- man the proceedings were brought to a close.</p>
        <p>The small diagram will enable our readers to understand the notation by which the roves of the game are recorded. Black le. uples the squares num- berlA 1 to 12 and white those numbered 21 to 32. The figures thus (11-15) indicate that a move is made from and to the squares so numbered. Black has the opening move and the pairs of figures apply alternately to Black and White to the 01Otle.</p>
