      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WATCH COMMITTER.</head>
        <p>WATCH COMMITTER.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the ail)tre c munictee were of a fortn4.l character. They were approval mi the m )tion of the Maroß, seconded by Councillor HELTON%</p>
        <p>THE ASSI-3TA.Nr OvERSEEMIIIP.,</p>
        <p>CON7ICAI,II-3 sti:)CEiSOR. THE COMPENSITLON WJEMON.—INFORAITION</p>
        <p>WANTED</p>
        <p>_ At a meeting of the Finance Committee it was r -solved that, pursuant t ) See.tion 123 ,f the Warringt in Corpora- tion Ant, 1899, toe powers aed duties of Mr. John Laveroek Tunstail, the assistant overseer and collector of poor rA,es for the townshp of Warrington, and the power of the Gaataiians of the Warrington Union to appoint collentore of poor rates in the townshio of War- rington shall case from and after the 29:h day of September, 1899 ; that Mr. Willine Henry Woodcock be appointed an asiistant overseer of the township a Warrington, and .11:st he execute and perform ail such duties at appertain to, and are ineident to, the ofEee of an overseer of the poor, and that he hold office, during the pleasure of the Council ; that all fees emoluments, and allowances payable to the aSsiStant overseer, by virtue of his offile, be p kid forthwith to the credit of the overseers. Other resolutions on the same subject were adentel. Councillor SHAW GRZEN, is moving the confirmation of the minote4, said th,-1, contained a number of resolu- tions in conoectioa with the Act which was rec3ntly obtained. Tne c onmittee deemed the half-year, the 29th September, the best time that could be fixed for deterinioing the appeintment of Mr. Tunstall, the fact being that during the half-year they would be better prepared to talc', over awl complete the whole of the duties of the offi..!e havez six months' work with the staff. (Hear, hear.) A special committee had been appointed to go into the while of the dusies p3rformei by Mr. Tunstal, and to arrange the work of the office and staf _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor FIEA.TON seconded. Councillor JOLLY: I.Ah)ulcl like to know if you are going to take over the clerks in Mr. Tuastall's This is a very important matter. Councillor TINNION said he had no personal kno ledge of the matter, but it seemed to him that the step that was bring takon was a very imp ircant one. a, had not seen any figures that would convince him thst the step proposed was the right one. Ho did not know whet was the amount of the sum to be paid to Mr. Tueetall for relinquishing his office, but the Council should have some information whether what it was proposed to do was wise and desirable Tees' were doing a goad thing either for themselves or for Mr. Tunstall. He ehauld like to know what the arrangement was, an I what were the advantages and disadvantages. If a man had a good position, and was making much money he would not hand it over hurriedly. H uuderstoof that it was not intended to bring the arrangement into force so soon, but that it had been brought forward no Mr. Tun- stall's application. There was a want of information, and he should like to know what the advent tees would be. He could see that the general principle was good, and welcomed the change, but ho would like to know the cost. The Mayon : Toe general principle of the whole matter has been thoroughly disoussed in the Council Chamber in connection witn the Bill. You seem to agree and vet to disagree. Everything has been made plain, but I will leave toe chairman to answer the questions you have mired. Councillor Tiamois: So far as lam coneerned I have not had any information about the matter. I don't dis- agree with the prineiple. but I want to know what are the terms, how chat' have hoe ) arrive I at, and the ad- vantage to the ratepayers (men a monetary paint of view.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: It is too late for that, sir. Councillor ['femme : Ir. is not too late to object. The MAYOR : If the Clairinan of the Ceineeittea likes to give Feu A reply I have no objection. Councillor rINNION I; is always too late when any- thing is important. The MAYOR : It is too late. It has passel the Conn- ell. Alderman SUTTON thou?ht the Council should be afforded as much information as possible in important matters of teis kind. He should like to ask whether they were to understand that Mr. Woodcock had to nerferm the duties of assi4ent overseer is addition to his present duties as collector of the improvement rater. Was there to be any addition made to the number of clerk's in the Boroueh Treasurer's department, and if so, to what extent ; what prevision or compensation had been made or was to he made to any clerk in the pre- sent overseers' office whose services might not be further required? He was compelled in some measure to pet, these questions on account of the numerous inqoiries which bid been made by various ratepayers. Reference had been made to Mr. Smith for instance. That gentle- man was in the overseers deoartment, and it was said ' that he was goide without any compensation or enniiier- ation being made in any way for him. He was a mar- ried man, and had a family to lying up. He had been a bag time in the service of the overseers, and he had been under the impression that when the question of compensation had to he settled, the case of the clerks would be taken inti consideration. That was the feeling of most people. In these eases he c ontended that they ought also to be generous with toe clerks if it Was sible. Councillor F. R. ROBERTS !Vie to a point of order. The resolution dealt only with taking over Mr. Tunstall'a duties from the 29th September. It had nothing to do with the agreement made with Mr. Tunstall. He sub- mitted that that could not be discussed, Was it a fact that the seal of the Cerporatioa had been attached to the agreement? Councillor SHAW GREEN: That is so. Councillor F. R. ROBERTS : It is not in order to dis- cuss the terms of the agreement. Councillor Sugentrase I submit that we are quite right in discussing the question. Councillor F. R.-ROBERTS : I asked for the ruling of the Mayor; not for Councillor Smethurst's. Coencillor SMETHURST : I can give you my opinion. Councillor F. R. ROBERTS: I don't want that. I want the rulieg of the Mayor. Councillor SMETHURST : I think we ought to known whether it is to be £3OO or £350 a year we are going to give to Mr. Tunstall. The Council ought to know, and they ought not to be kept, in the dark. We were Vold that this would be a grand thing for the Corporation. Will it be benefical to the Corporation to go further and oneage the other clerks, which means a rise of salary ? Councillor F. W. MONKS said he thought it would facilitate matters if he ventured to anticipate the chair- man's reply, on one or two points on which he thought he could give the information aoked for. In the first place Mr. Tuastall did not apply to be relieved from his duties. The whole matter came about at the initiation of the Finance Committee. The move rested entirely with that committee, and if they chose to put it off for two or three years it was in their power to do so, ht it was felt that now was the most suitable opportunity. A considerable portion of the work of the year had been done.and the Cornoration offici 113 who would take over the duties would have the lightest half-year awl the better opporunity to become acquainted with the de- tails before the reaily important matters had to be dealt with. Too whole object of tee arranzement was to con- centrate the work in one office, to make one demand note for all rates and taxes, and thus benefit the rate- payers. The re-arrangement would be a saving of ex- pense to the ratepayers. Councillor TINNION : But give us some figures. Councillor Moenes : It is not possible tie get at them. We had no means of compelling Mr. Tunst all to rive them or state the income he derived from the office. There is an Act of Parliament which regulates the com- pensation that ba3 to be paid. I admit to start with that the amount agrrel upon is in excess of what the Act of Parliament would have given Mr. Tunstall, for compulsory retirement, but there are reasons why we should make it more than the bare minimum which the Act would give. The amount it is proposed to give is two-thirds of his nttt income, and I believe it has been stated in the Council Chamber that it was to he two- thirds of the nett emoluments of the office. We had no means of getting at the nett emoluments, and it was arranged that Mr. Tunstall should make a statutory declaration, as to what the nett receipts have been for the last ,two or three years. He has made such a I declaration and two-thirds of the amount is between £332 and £333 per annum, which is the amount we shall have to pay him as long as lie lives. Notwith- standing that we will have to pay him that amount we estimate that the amalgamation will save the ratepayers from £2OO to £3OO per annum. When that ceases we shell save that much more. But taking over the staff has given more trouble than all else. With regard to Mr. Smith, he has himself to blame. For a long time he has been a stumbling block, and he said he would only come on such and such con- ditione, which the Finance Committee could not agree to for one moment ; his conditions were quite sufficient to prevent any further consideration of his claim. Now with regard to compensation to the atalf. No members of the staff are recognized by the Local Government Board as officials Cr overseers or asistant-wereeer ; and the idea of the Finance Committee is that Mr. Tunstall, out of the compensation paid to him can very well afford to compensate his staff. This is a proper and a fair light is which to look at the mattsr, and it is unfair to ex pect the Corptraeion to compenoate the staff as well asMr. Tunstall. (Hear, hear.) Alderman SUTTON replied that he did nct wish to he , misunderstood, he had made no reference to the corn. I pensatioa that Mr. Tuestall was getting, because that</p>
