        <p>TFIE HEXLTFI OF THE TOWN</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED ExrENstoN OF TFIE INFECTIOUS DISEASES FIOSVITAL. STRONLi OPPOSITION.</p>
        <p>At a meetiog of the Health Committee the Borough Surveyor was instructed to ob-ain tenders for the ex• tension of the hoipital, to ene, a qua,eity survryor, and to prepar-i new plans for a smatt-p-,x hosPital. The minutes contained the new reenlations with r ,apect to dairies, cowsheds and milkshops, and it was resolvrd that for the purpose of becom'ng the It,at airtinrity under the Small Dwellings Aevisition Ac", 1899, the Council be recommended to act uncle? tha Act.</p>
        <p>Alderman SurroN, in moving the adoptien of the minutes, alluded to the resolutina referrineto the teosion of the hospital, and said it was a queitlon which had remained dormant for a very lon% time, and the committee had felt bound to go on with it. If an epidene'e took Place in the town they would be to blame. The B)rough Surveyor had also heel instructed to pre- pare plans for a small-pox hospital. That was also a question which had been in abeyance for a hog time. They also thought that it Was ve-y necessary that somei steps shoull he taken there because if a anrtn-p•-x epidemic: occurred in the town it would be very eerieus for them. Referring to the resolution regarding dairies, cowsheds and milkshops he said a copy of the regut,- tions had been printed and supplied to each member. It might be interesting to the Council ro have some little informatien as to the general health of the town. (Hear, hear.) Hs! had it on the authority of the Medical Officer Dr. Gornall, that during • the week online; Sentember 30rh the general death rate was fairly I aw, 219 per 1,030. Fhe zymotic death rate continued high, viz.. 121, aryl incledcd 9 deaths frm diarrhce 1. 4 fro.n typhoid fever, 1 from diphtheria, and 1 from whooping cough. Is the same week it was satisfee.tory to note that the number of cases of zymetic disease fell from 40 in the previous week to 18. (ilear.hesr.) The hospital was still full, though by a little arra...seem "it more typhoid EIQE.a had been taken in. There were now in the hospital 9 cases of typhoid fever and 44 of scarlet fever, making a total of 53. The Lena! Government Board had sanctioned the plans for the alterationa in connection with their requirements in Aikin- tree% With regard to the last resolution " that for the perpese of becoming the local authority under the Smell Dwellings Acquisition Act, 1899, the Council be recommended to undertake to act under the Act," he would give the Towo Clerk's explanation. "The Sadl Dwellings Acquisition Act is now in force, an i the County Council are the authority within the borough under the Ac". The object of the resolutian is to make the Ton Council the local authority in the place of the County Coneeit." The TOWR Cl,,rk he might add, hal instruc- tions to prepare a Summary of the Art, erniee would he presented before the next meeting of the Council. He might also say that the object cf the Act enabled persons to obtain advances from local authorities towards the purchase of their dwellings. (Hear, her.) Alderman ROBERTS seconded the e mfirmation of the minutes. Coeneillnr PLIIMPTON said the Committee was about to a te an epnte of aheet £8 000 ie PrPrtin, a small.</p>
        <p>pox hospital at Pear Tree Firm, and a staff would have te be kept ttiere and the buillin; furnishel. It might be a great number of years—he hoped ir, would—before the place would be used as a sin hosoital, and if they were going to all this expense he th meht tne build- ing should be utirz .d for othee purposes than that of a small-pox homital. He had al mays held the opinion in committee that the I ifeethele Dis,teei ispital should also he at Pear Tve Farm. Property was being erect •d all araun I Akin-s•ret tp)ioitAl, and very snon it would ba in • the midst of a very thickly- populated district. It wculd be very mulls better and cbe Ter even now, instead of makieg tbe proposed extension of the fever hospital in Aikin-street for a building to be erected at Par Tree Farm. They had th,r-, 45 acri,s'of. land, a suffi iea area t isolate all cases. The feeling was that the Conned had made a great mistake in not accepting the offer of the Board of Guardians to take over the old ho.pital which they were prepared to do at the time at a full valuation. tear, he.ar.) The Health Committee had made a mistake in that respect. He felt sure that they were going to make still further mistake by extending the Aikin-street institution. Toe plans of the eveasioa had been at the end of the table, and he WAS sorry to say the chairman had n &gt;t; t-ken the opportunity to explain them. He d d not think that half a dozen members 'if the Cnuacil knew that the plans ever CSITP before the Coun- cil. Although he Was a member of the Health Committee he only saw the plans once in their full detail, It was proposed to make a second entrance in Goullen-street. • off LOvely-lane, and within a short distance of Lovely- lane there was going to be an administrative block. Lie . asked a question concerning that when the matter was discussed in committee and was told by the medical officer taat a future medical officer of healtia mi:tht re- silo there. (Lauebter.) ble objected to be put off in that way. (Laughter.) Alderman Roberts offered some little explanation, but the Medical Officer role rub- shad river the Commilee and over Aiderman Roberts—</p>
        <p>_ (Aid reran Rcusenie ,iss eatedifrem this: tmark ) —'lh s entrence,and a eew iode e in Goalilen-street, was sirneiy a fad of the Medical Officer of Health. There was no raecessity for two entrances. All the cases would go up Levelv-lane arid go in at teat (ado. Tnis lodge had been tacked on to the plans very recently. Alderman SUTTON : You are quite mistaken. The plans have been pe,sied with your knowledge. It 13 not to your credit as a member of the committee to say you know nothing at all about tnem. Tee :MAYOR took it that Coancillor Plumpton's chief objectoe was the proposal tha.. tne Barouge Surveyor be enpowered to engage a qaantitv surveyor. Councillor TINNION : He objects to Goulden-street Lodge. The MAYOR: The plans have been passed. Conncider PLIIMPT,N thouent it was not wise to spend i.'6,000 there. He believed iefectious diseas c ties should ee to the Pear Tree Farm. It was the greatest mistake to allow this extension. In addition t) that tw3 lodges were unnecessary. Alderman Sutton ought to have made a statement when the plans were brought before the Council, so that the members could have understood what they were asked t? pass. They were rushing this through. He Was reluctant to say it, but it was so. Ali. ratan SUTTON: lam son'? for you. (Laughter.) Councillor PLIMPTON then moved an am.ndment that the resolutions relating to the ex:onsion of the hospital and the plans for a smallpox hospital be referred back to the committee for further consideration. Councillor JAMES EvaNs seconded the amendment. He said he had no desire te hamper the commictee in their work of providing additional hoaoital aleornmolation. Be must confess that he did not understand the plans in all their fu'ness and clearness whea they were passed be the Coulcil. It was tile desire of a large number of people dying C)ntigaous to the present hospital that this mater seould receive furthar c3nsideration. The area taken up by the hospital was coasiiered to be quite sufficient without extension.</p>
        <p>Alderman Roeuars said the committee was quite cog- nisant of the fact that toera was A considerable amount of opposition to the proposal to have a second entrance at Gou!den-street. It was 'veil within the knowledge of Councillor Plumpton that a deputation should have met the Health Committee at the last meeting, but the com- mittee were largely absent, attending tie Health Con- gress, ani the d Tutation were mired to defer their visit until Weinesday night. The commqtee expected then to receive the deputation, and he would sty without hesitation that if this difficulty could be overcame without prejudicing the interest of t4s institution it would be overcome. (Hear, hoar.) Toe Committee had no desire to arouse any feeling of uneasiness, nervousness, or anxiety on the part of the residents. Tney would go out of their way to avoid doing anything of the kind. He was quite convincel that the risk WAS enCrely a myth. At the same tim he knew the feeling existed, and be was bound to respect it. He would do his best to overcome the difficulty. He asked the Council, therefore, not to vject the mirautPs. When the hospital was originally built the dis- trict was not populous, and they considered they were doing the right thing. If they had now to bogin de novo they would not dream of building within the boundary of the borough. They cauld not, however, give up this building wi,bout a serious financial los. It; was said that thy had made a mistak 3 in not accepting the offer of the Board of Guardians. The Guardians had built a very handsome block for the purposes of a hospital for infectious diseases.</p>
        <p>Ceerecillor PLIIMPTON : Four times larger than is necessary. AiderMln ROBSIITS observer] that that was the busi- ness of the Guardians, and if they had mtde a mistake they were responsible for it. But that Council never had en off .1. from the Guardians for the iufectious dis- eas,3 hospital in Aikin-street. If they had had they would have put it before the Council. What ocourrei ve.s that the Guardians approashed them and astred them if they were willlne to sell their premien. They :replied that they were very willine if they h kd a chance. The premises had cost from £l2 090 te £l6,ooo,aud they considered it reasonable to ask £lO,OOO for them. Thev acted in the interests of the ratepayers. Their price was 'isot favourably received by Vol Guardians and 89 the whole thing went off. If that Council was prepared to authorise the committee to run the risk of ineurrine the less—the present debt on the hulling was £6ooo—they would be delighted to try and find another site coatsida the town. They had been trying their level best for five or six years to secure a site for the StnAll pox hospital, and memheirs had no conception of the difficulties thr../ hid encountered. They stood te-day as they Sto34 five years ago, absolutely unprepared for an outbreak of small- pox, and after the experience of six years ago it did net reflect credit upon the Corporation. At Aikin-street they had not sufficient accommedeion for casss nor for their staff, With regard to the suppned risk, he might point out that there was no healthier place in tbe district than Whitecross Ward.</p>
        <p>Councillor Tirstsiols : "Question." Ilderman ROBBRTS (c)ntiouing) said therb was no question about it. Figures could be obtained which would prove that there was no healthier part in the dis- trict except the outskirts of Latchford. Still people were sensitive and property owners might consider their property would be j-opardiFel by the establishment of this see.,)ni lodge. riterefor:i they would give the matter serious attention. With regard to the discussion of plans already passed, if they were going to do that with re• spect to every plan they would never reaeh aridity in regard to their buiiness He hoped the Council would not refuse what they were asking for. Councillor TessuoN thought a good deal oi misunder- standing would have been avaidr-d if explanations had b ,en made when the plans were before the Council. He had not had an opportunity of examining the plans,and now catno this propoce I expenditure of £6,000.</p>
        <p>, Ald"rtliall ROBERTS : Th 9 anrsa has not been men- tioned. We don't know ours,lves. Councillor Trtimog : It is an estimate. PtilerMAß ROBSRTS :It iS not. The committee know noting about, it. It is not fair to maim such a state- ment.</p>
        <p>Caunoillcr Trionos : It is Or. opinion of one of the members of ths committee. The MAYOR : I don't El flr) how any member of the committee can know until the tenilers are rec-.ived. Councillor Tissiorr sticl it was, then, an approximate rstimate, and he thought the money might b spent in erecting a building in another part of the town. He thought no harm would be done if the resoluli on were re- ferral nsek. Councillor HEATON stated that a case came wider his notice on Saturday morning of a man who had been ill for four weeks and whose wife and two childrim had the fever. Toe Corporation was unable to accommoJate them in the hospital, and the man had only 10s a week to live on, and the doctor had ordered special food. He (Councillor Heaton), thought some provision should ha made fez' a man in such circumstances, when they were unable to find acominodation at the hospital. (Dear, hear.) Councillor BENNETT referred to the resolution recom- mending the Council to undertake to act under theSma.ll D ;ceilings Acquisition Act of 1899. He thought it would he better to have a synopsis of the Act before deciding upon any action. The Tows CLERK : This resolution doesn't bind you to anything. C)onciltrir BENNET r referred to the remarks of Alder- man Rob, es resoee'ing finality about ptan 4 and poin- ted out that the resolution was for the Survey ir to pre- pare new vans for a smallpox hospital. A long time ago they adopted plans for that hosp ti, and he was wondeting why they should now be altered.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : for a modified scheme suggested by the Local Givernment BoArd. rotin,;ll,, RP!sP.TPTT RA;(1 he had a go aeaf (If grrn-</p>
        <p>patky with the amendment. The C3uncil made a mis- takes in the first icstance in erecting an infectious dis- eases hospital in Aikin-s7reat. There seemed to have been for many years a disinclination to look into the ' future. If the Corporation had had the faculty of look- ing ahead they would not have planted the hospital in that locality 20 years ago. He thougiat they would be repeating the mistake by extending the present hospital. He was inclined to think they made a big mistake in not coming to terns with the Board of Guardians. (Hear. hear.) He thought it was worth the committee's while t 3 consider the coestion. C )uncillor MONKS thought Councillor Bennett's remarks were a reflection on the Council. It was all very well to be wis a after the event. What Cluncillor Bennett had complained of was net exceptional to this Council, bat to Councils generally and even private individuals as well. Lie had a good deal ot sympathy with those who had spoken on the amendment, but if they referred it back it might mean a delay of six or seven weeks. It did soem a pity to cause any delay ' when the Committee would, if it were possible, meet the views of the deputation which was to wait upon them. Councillor Burrros thought i,.. was the duty of the Health Committee to m,et the wishes of the residents if 1 it were passible to do so •</p>
        <p>Councillor SHAW GREEN ',bought the resolution might 1: referred baak ; it would only mean a delay of three weeks.</p>
        <p>Alderman ROBERTS said be bad had put before him the feelings of toe deputition which was to wait upon the Health Committee, an if the committee could see their way to abandm the proposed entrance in Gou!den- street be had been assured it would meet with the view of the deputation. The objection was not really to the proposed altera'ions. AldermanSUTTON, in replying, said they had so many inconsistent members of the Council %au° were ever- lastingly objecting to resolutions which they had helped to pass—(laughter)— Councillor Prarmrros : Give us a sample. Alderman SUTTON' There are toe many. (Laughter.) He really did not see the object of the amendment. It would only delay the wok. If they decided upon do- ing away with the eatrance in Gulden-.treet at the committee meeting on the following night the plans could be altered witheut any difficulty. It was im- possible to have an infectious diseases hospital without someone raising an objection. The amendment was put to the meeting, and only Councillors Plumpton, .hmls Evans and Tinnion voted for it. The minutes were th3a confirmed in their entirety.</p>
        <p>SANITA.RT WORKS COMMITTEE. APPOINTMENTS OF AGENTS.</p>
        <p>It was regolved, at a meeting of toe above Committee, that 3dr. William aiugoton be appointei agent for the sale ot mtnure in the Laticathire area, and Mr. John Bayly for the Cheshire area. The minutes were ad )pted on the motion of Coutnillor Homo, seconded by Councalor Jouti EvaNs.</p>
        <p>TdE LATE MR. J. E. WORSLEY. COMPLIMENr ro MR. C. M kDEuEY.</p>
        <p>In the minutes of the Museum, Library, Arts, and Technical Instruction Committee allusion was made to the loss sustained by the Conaraittee by the &amp;rack of Mr. Worslay, oae of the aon-members of the Council wh r was a member of the committee. It was also resolv,il Te i ton rt nitt has much pleasure in recording the fact that Mr. Msdeley has just completed the 25:h year of his service as librarian and curator of the Museum and Art Gallery. During this lengthy period, during the whole of which Mr. Madeley has bad the entire charge and responsibility of our in- stitution very important changes and developments have occurred, the result of which has been to increase very largely the popularity and uvfultiess of both tee Museum and the Library, and on all occasions Mr. Madeley has proved himself to be fully equal to the increasing de- mands made upon him, and has shown conspicuous ability in carrying out the varied and important duties with which he has been entrusted. The committee hope that Mr. Madeley m ay, in the enjoyment of good health, have still many years before him of increasing public usefulness."</p>
        <p>Alderman ROBERTS, in moving the adoption of the minutes, referred to the melancholy duty imposed on the members of the committee by the unexpected death ot Mr. Worsley. Be said Mr. Worsley's death cama as a shock to them, and he should not be doing justice to their feelings and his memory if is addition to the reso- lution on the minutes he did not also snake some refer- ence in the Council to it. Mr. Worsley had conducted the responsible duties of his high and honourable office for many years with great credit to himself, and his manner had won the esteem and confidence of the whole body of the general public. (Hear. bear.) As a mem- ber of the committee be was an extremely useful One, and an able contributor. (Hear, hear.) He had pleasure in calling attention to the resolution regarding Mr Made- ley. It was not often that a man completed 25 years of arduous labour either in private or public service, and the manner in which Mr. Niadeley had done his work re- flected great credit upon him, and had greatly increased trio usefalnese of an Institution Warrington had reason to be justly proud of. (Hear, hear.) Councillor BENNETT seconded. He said they all recog- nized Mr. Worsley'd worth in his public capacity, and as an antiquarian he had been a worthy follower of Mr. Beanoont and Mr. Kendrick. His services were very valuable, and they missed them very much. With re- gard ts Mr. Madeley the committee had not a :more zealous or faithful servant. Be referred to the fact that at the Librarians' Conference Mr. Madeley had contested the statement that Salford bad the first free public library. Be saw that the Salford people were going to erect a tablet setting that forth, and he thought War- rington should have a.tablet setting forth their claim to the distinction. The MAYOR endorsed what had boon said. Ha looked upon Mr. Worsley as a personal friend,. anl a conscien- tious and good citizen. Alderman Strrros said he could. sympathise with the serious loss the coinmitteelhad sustained in the death of Mr. Worsley, whose life was devoted to literary and antiquarian pursuits, and which made him one of the most valuable msnobers of the Museum arid Library Committee. The Alderman also concurred in the resolu- tion cencerniag Mr. Madeley. Alderman ROBERTS said that as reference had bean made to the claim of Warrington to be the first public library it was worth while knowing that in spite of Salford'd claim Warrington was the first town in England to establish a municipal free library. Salford established toe first free lending library. The Warrriegtoa Library was free, inasmuch as any person could use the books on the premises. It was not made a free lending library quite so early as was that at Salford. He agreed with Mr. Maieley's suggestion, and thought it should be put on record that Warrington pos- sessed the proud distinction of being the first Borough in the :Kingdom to establish a free municipal library. (Hear, hear.) The minutes were then passed. GAS CoairdiTTES. ANNUAL REPORT OF VIE ENGINEERS.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the aU9ve C)minittee contoin the annual report of the engineer which appeared ito Satur• day's " Examiner." Coancillor fIEo.ToN pt)p)Si. and Cmoncillor JOHN EVANS seconded too confirmation of the minutes. C ,uncillor SIZ A. .17 GREEN c3ngratulated the Committee upon the report of the gao engineer, and laid he loOced for ward with closiderable h3po and expectation to the profits to be derived from the deportment which could be hied as a it..!.t-off t 3 tile large expenditure of capital on public buildingB. (Rear, hear). Tne minutes were agreed to. TETE RIVER CfI&amp;NNEL.</p>
