        <head>MORE CONIPLIINf3 AG.IINir 'FITE SIII? CINA.L COAPINY.</head>
        <p>MORE CONIPLIINf3 AG.IINir 'FITE SIII? CINA.L COAPINY.</p>
        <p>"NOT GOING TO S ['AND ll' ANY LONGER,"</p>
        <p>Several complaints having b3ea received as to the un- satisfactory state of tPri channel in the river, and the Borough Surveyor having submitted the correspondence which had taken plici between himself and the engineer of the Manchester Snip Canal Company, it his been re- solved by the committee that the Town Clerk be in- structed to take proceedings as advised by counsel for enforcing the carrying out of the agreement between the Corporation and the c impany. Alderman HOBERTit in propoiing the confirmation of the minutes, said they had been inundated with com- plaints from the users of the river with regard to the condition into which the channel had been allowed to get, owing to insufficient dredging on the part of the Ship Canal Company. They were not going to stand it any louger,and they had tD:cided to ascertain their exact position with a view to seeing whether they coul im- prove the precont condition et things. (Ileac, hear.) Councillor HEATON seconded, and the motion was agreed to.</p>
        <p>WATIAR COMMITTEE.—PurtcaIse OF LAND.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Water C Kninittee, held on the 21st September, it was resolved that the Town Clerk be instructed to open negotiations for the purchase of sued land as may be required under the Corporation Act. 1899, for waterworks parpos-s. That the General Pur- poses Committee be re,q,i,sced te fix a uniform allow- ance to be allowed to all workmen in the employ of the Corporation on the occasion of their annua trip, and that the tender of E. Timmins and Sens, Limited, to sink trial horings at alien-lane, Houghton-Green, at the sum of £132 be accepted. In answer to Councillor SMETHURST, Alderman FRAN- Ciniß said they required more land a Winwick, but they were not going to purchase it uatil they knew the result of the borings for water.</p>
        <p>The mir.utes w‘qe clatirrnel. ELECTRIC LIGaTING CORIMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Committee met on the 11th September when it was resolved that Mr. T. Fletcher bs appointcid clerk or works for the electric lighting station and central sanitary depo at the sslary of £3 103. per week, and that the works be connected with the te'ephonl system. Os the motion of Conncillor MONKS, th 3 minutes were passed without comment. THE WARRINGTON AND KNUTSFORD LIGHT RAILWAY.</p>
        <p>At a mooting of thm General Parposes Committee the 14,1lowihg letter was raad from the Ormskirk and South- port Electrle Light Railway Company. —To the Town Clerk, Warrington.—Doar sir,—Morring to or intir- view this clay upon the ab3vc subject, I shall be oblivd if you will, at an early date, kubnait t 3 your C3mmittea</p>
