      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>TRANSVAAL CRISIS. REFERENCES FF 1,6-0.4.1, MINISTERS.</head>
        <p>THE TRANSVAAL CRISIS. REFERENCES FF 1,6-0.4.1, MINISTERS.</p>
        <p>At many of the churches and chapels in Warrington on Sunday, prayers were offered for a p.aceful settle- ment of the Transvaal quetion. At Cairc-street Uni- tarian Chapel the Rev. W. EL Drummond offered special prayer. At Wycliffe Congrezational Cimrch the national memorial protesting against., war and pleading for arbitration was placed in the vestibule for signature,ancl many members of tha congregation attached their name;.</p>
        <p>At the close of the sermon at the morning service the pastor, the Rev. John Yonge, said he could not forebear suggesting one question that was pertinent to our national crisis atthis time. Our nation was.unhappilv, in a rapidly developing excitement which seemed to be hurrying :it into the horrors of war and bloodshed. The• noise and turmoil of warlike preparations I sent their echoes throughout the land. Respon- sible statesmen who were in metrol of affairs seemed to be yielding to what they might deem the dire necessity of conflict. The war flame had been I kindled, and was being sedulously fanned by men whose interest of one kind or another is in strife. Yet to all appearance never was war less reasonable, leis justified, 1 less necessary. The question he wield ask was this— Would not this country which profeseed to be Christian. ' and the Transvaal Republic. which male the same pro- fession, be saved from this terrible danger, delivered in this hour of crisis, if both countries, rulers and people alike, would stay their dip'omaeiei, stay their arm meets, stay their spreadine animoiitiei, and listen to God ? Surely it was for His voiceth et even melon.; should be ready to listen. Instead of taking counsel with pres- tige and pride, with greed and gain, with revenge and retaliation, nations and people that c'aimed to bee Chris-, non should take counsel with God, with, Christian prin- ciple, with the spirit and the methoie of the Kingdom of Christ. Very speoially should tee British nation, that had been so conspicuous an advocate of arbitration as the preventive, or at least the precuser, of war, stay its band until every peaceful method hid been tried to settle the matter in dispute. If only the twe peeples,so uneqnelly set in array, would hush the rising clamour and listen for the voice of the King of Miles and Prince of Peace this danger would be averted. Was not this, therefore, a day in which the churches should speak? Could they expect Cabinets and armies to be slow to anger when churches bad no word to speak in favour of peace? It was not a war of stern noceesity, nor was it a controversy in which as yet all pessible peaceful methods had been exhausted. The crisis was dark with danger. The call to Christian prayer and the expression of Christian opinion was imperative. Let every tru• patriot pray:for the prevention of this national crime and for the establishment of reasoneble rind righteees p.ace. And let him also, in one way or other. see*. Let every lover of peabe and righteousness among them that day sign his or her name in protest against needles war; in advocacy of pacific policies and patient reason. On Sunday morning the Rev. H. G. Roberts preached at the Bold-etreot Wesleyan Chapel on " Citizenship " The preacher referred to the religious sch e 1 of Im- perialists, one of whose prephets and p eats was Rul yard Kipling, whom, he said, they were glad Gel hai spared to them, for they acknowledged him to be the poet laureate of the AnglceSaxon race. Rudyard Kspling had voiced an imperialism with which he could agree, an imperialism based on service r ether thin on glory, an Imperialism which presented as its c trdinal obligatinns and credentials the hearing of hardens rather than the extorting of tritute. They must make a distinction between this imperialism and the beaetful and bastard jingoism of the music ball which revelled in the splendours and ignored the duties and oblieetions of empire. The embitter' of the hour was for emiYr t and and for conquest. 'reeve were those who aaid that theee combinations might beco us their destruction. Let them pray that their empire might eontinee to be true to her highest and best traditions. The questien prescntel it- self to him, bow might the love of empire be trans- formed from a selfish ambitiou into a blessed one? There wee one answer which promised a solution of the problem, and it was to turn the ambitions of the empire into an ambition to win an empire for Christ. Referring to the Outlanders, the preacher said he hoped that they felt that they had the sympathy, power and majesty of the British Empire behind them. He prayed that the government at Pretoria, a government charac- terised by venality, corruption, and tyranny, a govern- ment led by the obstinate,wily, crarty, narrow-minded, canting old Dutchman who is being thrust by the force of circumstances int) a eosition he had no right to occupy, might at the last hour respond favoursbly to the reasonab'e demands of their fellow-citizens, for they consider them to be reasonable. To eey that there was no ground far interference or action wee an insult to the cornmcn sense of the British public. They were in duty beamed to stand by their fellow citizens, who they were told paid nine-tenths of taxation and yet were denied the privilege of having their children taught their mother toegue in the public schools nf the country. The conduct of the Transvaal Republic was inconsistent w.te the convention be which they hal restored to them their independence. The generosity and patience shown by England to the Transvaal Republin was most creditable and he thought unparallelei in the hietnrr of diplomacy. They prayed that %vitt@ (11,17 ca m'.,tr, p and that the decision of the Cabinet. this we .k might be worthy of the teaditiena 2f their rteentre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
