      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>ALL NIGHT AT SEA. GALLANT RESCUE BY A WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>ALL NIGHT AT SEA. GALLANT RESCUE BY A WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>STEAMER.</p>
        <p>During the heavy gale which swept over the Lan- cashire coast on Sunday last the Pioneer, a passen- ger steamer owned in Preston, had a narrow escape from destruction. On the day mentioned she left that port fora cruise to Lytham and Southport, having on board a mixed company of excursionists and fishermen. After landing the foi mar the vessel put out to sea to convey the fishermen to the fishing grounds beyond Southport. As she did not return some anxiety was felt as the wind had in- creased in violence, and the sea rolled landward in tremendous waves. On Monday, however, news was received that the storm had smashed the Pioneer's rudder and left her helpless at the mercy of the sea. Most fortunately,at this trying and criti- cal juncture, the steamer Gertrude hove in sight and gallantly bore down to the rescue. The task ,was a dangerous and arduous one, but the brave captain and crew of the Gertrude were equal to the emergency. After one or two failures they succeeded in attaching a hawser to the disabled steamer, and towed her safely into Birkenhead to the joy and gratitude of all on board. It will interest our readers to know that the steamer which was thus instrumental its saving life and property was the Gertrude belonging to The Joseph Monks and Company, line of Warrington and was on her way to Preston with a cargo of 4,000 sacks of wheat when she sighted the distressed Pioneer. The Gertrude is a very powerful steel screw steamer of 500 tons with a steaming power of twelve knots. She was built by Messrs. Scott and Sons, the eminent shipbuilders of Bowling,near Glasgow, to the highest class at Lloyds, and is com- manded by Captain John S. Monks, an experienced and reliable seaman. We may also mention that the Company are just now having three more steamers built on the Clyde, and when these are delis, !red the carrying capacity of the line will be 2000 tons.</p>
        <p>11 Some of the passengers and crew of the disabled I 'Pioneer" have been interviewed and their narra- tives are of a thrilling description and show how near they all were to a watery grave. Mr. William Wilson,fish dealer,Wellfield-road. Preston I uave a lucid and detailed account of the adventures of toe Pioneer from the time she left Preston on Sunday until she was taken in tow by the Gertrude on Monday morning. He said :—" After disembarking our passengers at Southport, we made due west, and when outside the Jumbo buoy, six milei eut,dragged for aboist an hour, and bawled, taking a quantity of fluke and haddock. We then discovered that something had gone wrong with our steering gear, and finline ourselves unable to manage the Iroise' immediately dopped anchor. A.n endeavour was made to take down the mait,a,nd use it as a temporary rudder, but in vain. In toe heavy sea which was running, such a contrivance refused to act. Young Will Monk dived overheard, and endeavoured to repair the damage, but was utterly unable to do so, and I would not have undertaken such a task. We drifted about at anchor until daybreak on Monday, when we showed signals of distress, hoisting a flag and blowing the steamer's whistle vigorously. Between six and seven o'clock, a schooner bore down upon us. but was in dis- tress and unable to assist us when we hailed, her sails !King blown to atoms. About eight o'clock the s.s. Gertrude came within hailing distance.and we asked her captain to tow us to Preston whither as it happen-d, he was bound. With difficulty we got a line on board, and were towed in the direction of Livsrpool for half an hour, when the rope broke opposite the Liverpool light. ship. We drifted round the Zebra buoy. nut towards the lightship, the ere wof which hoisted the black flag as a signal to the lifeboat. The captain of the Gertrude, however, brought his vessel round, and at extreme risk came near enough to throw a second rope on board Owing to the heavy sea the Gertrude erashei into our bow, and stove in several planks. When sae saw her towering above us on the crest of a bug)) wave we gave ourselves up for lost. I shouted to the men stand- ing on the fore part of the vessel. "Every man for him- self! Jump if the wor:t Comes." The damage, how- ever, was net so serious as was anticipated, and we thrust our oilskins into the breach to stop th leak, and we retowed up the Meraey:to Egremont in the presence of thousands of spectators. At the mouth of the river we were met by the lifeboat and a tug which were putting out to our rescue, and when the lifeboat men saw we were safe, they lifted their caps and gave us a rousing cheer which was taken up by the crowds lining the river bank. We responded heartily. I can assure you, and must have presented a pretty spectac,e, for the :majority ot us were wet to the skin. I never r sdc is a better boat than the Pioneer. She behaved grandly in a terrible sea, riding to snckor like A sea gull in the water, now rising on the crest of a big wave, and then being hid from the sight in the trone,h. She stood the gale magnificently, and it was a pure accident that she was damaged in any way. With two exceptions, our party suffered acutely from s'cknees as car: only be expected, for the boat was tossed up and 11 down, the sensation equalling that of a swing-boat. She righted herself time after time. Throughout the 1 night we shipped very little water, being swept by only a couple of seas :Ind it was a dangerous feat to walk on the deck, even though holding on with both hands, for one moment one side would be raised almost perpendi- cular in the air, whilst the next it was buried in the troqi2h of a wave. Toe wind increased in force after midnight,and some of the party cried, "We are doomed" bet I told them we were never doomed till the boat went down. The night seemed interminable, but with the approach of daybreak, we knew we had a chance, for we were in the direct line of vessels making to-Lim pool. So rough war it, than even a big boat like the Gertrude was toseed about like a cork. and after she had taken us in tow she was frequently lifted so high that we. build se s tier propeller whizzing round. striking the empty air, while the next moment we could see nothing but her masts. The captain of the Gertrude told us he would not have ridden at anchor with 15 fathoms of chain and a cadge anchor Omagh such a night for £l,OOO. Neither would I again." _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomis Kirkham, of 33, Ecroyd-ztreet, Ashton, told an eqaally interesting clery, though slightly differ- ent in detail. "Young Will Munk," he statei, "made a plucky attempt to fir a temporary, steering gear, strip- ping and diving into a raging sea. He endeavoured to place a shackle in the hole from which the ruider was tern, but without avail, the waves washing him back time after time, and after being in the water 20 minutes we had to haul him back by the rape attaelasd to his waist. Many a ship would not have lived an hour in such a sea. We had the lifebelts ready for ernergeney, but she was never in danger of being lost until run into by th. Gertrude, the captain of which is worthy of cre lit for the efforts he put forth on our behalf, for he risked his ehip and crew to gave us. He is a gentleman wherev he is. One of our party, Richard Halsalt, better k lawn to the bowling kept 'watch on the forecastle, fraternity es Culd Dick,' without a break, for 12 hours, keeping a, keen lookout for the lights of passing vessels and seeing that the Pioneer did not dritt from her moorings. At intervals during the nizbt he would cry out.in .fitentorian tones All's well,' and his voice was so solemn that it tended to frighti-n realer than rea mire his comrades." Mr. William Monk, jun., the mate of the Pioneer, said, " We would have been able to fir a timporary steering gear had not the wind increased, in force. We intend bringing the Pioneer home to-morrow (Tuesday) and expect corlinuing our daily sailines to Blackpool on Thursday. Her grOSA tonnage is 50 tons. We have built and run five excurtiion vessels, and this is our first accident and first occasion upon which wa nave failed to land cur passengers at the specified tune."</p>
        <p>T.. the—Wit:ld: Pure Darjeeliqg Tea. No Blends or D-letprioos Admix- tures. 1/5,1/9,2/2, 3/- &amp; 4/- per lb. In 1,5,10 &amp; 20 ib boxes or cheAl A TRIAL of this PURE TEA will sa•isfy anyone of its perfo,',ion. The Darjeeling Pure Tea Co., Pure Darjeeling, Coleman-st., London. B.C.</p>
      </div>
