      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>GUARANTEED THE FINEST SAUSAGES</head>
        <p>GUARANTEED THE FINEST SAUSAGES</p>
        <p>PROCURABLE. W INDSOR OXFORD CAMBRIDGE VD. „ SLICING 9D. „ TOMATO SD. t,</p>
        <p>FRESH SUPPLIFS EVERY MORNING.</p>
        <p>LOCAL BRANCH. 4, BUTTERILIRKET STREET,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE argument for justice to Justice to tho the Outlander has been met Outlander.</p>
        <p>by the contention that he is not worth the trouble we are making about him. There is a touch of meanness in thus meeting a political argument, and we should hope it will not be made much of by the persons who are intent upon a peaceful settlement. First of all, it is said of him that ho is an invading adventurer, who would not have entered the Transvaal if he had not been drawn thither by commercial greed. This is a singular argument to come from the descend- ants of men who went to the Cape with like motives, and ousted the Dutch, who were there before us, and who hold India to-day because our fore-fathers exploited the country for commercial purposes. But we may let that aspect of the matter pass, to point out that the Outlanders are there by the assent, if not by the invitation of the Boers, and that the Boers could have worked the gold industry themselves if they had chosen so to do, instead of allowing foreigners to take it up. That the Boers have made a good thing out of the brains, money, and work of the Outlanders cannot be denied. That they have denied justice to them is equally undeniable. Some of the Outlanders of Johannes- burg are occasionally described as "the scum of the earth." Possibly many of them are rough, Islangy dare-devils. Mr. BARTLEY, M.P., writing Ifrom Johannesburg early last month, describes the lOutlanders as consisting of two classes—a small number of capitalists, constantly increasing, and highly-paid labourers. The latter are less political than the former, and less teuchod, at present, by the high taxation. The former are hampered all round, and feel that lack of security which comes from the want of a just, incorruptible Government. Statements as to the political desires or indiffer- ence of the Outlanders are sure to be coloured by the observer's point of view, because the two classes do not as yet perfectly agree in their politi- cal opinions. It is further said that whilst we are right in gently agitating for the political enfran- chisement of the Outlander, we must never put his case strongly because we have no right of forcible interference with the internal affairs of the Transvaal. The argument wants re-stating in another form. If we have absolutely no right of interference whatever, we may at once abandon the idea of getting any justice for the Outlander, and cease either arguing the matter or writing despatches. Few persons are so absurd as to take up this logic4l position. They want jus- tice done to the Outlander, they are all for argu- ment, patience and diplomatic methods; but they think there is no difference between administra- tive direction and electoral power. We have never sought to :tell the Transvaal how it shall conduct its administration; but we have constantly re- minded the Transvaal Government that it inot keeping its promise to award equal rights to the Outlanders. There is still another point to make. When we gave the Convention of 1884, is it con- ceivable that we intended that British subjects should be destitute of political power, and that we handed them over to the tender mercies of the Boers because we were beaten at liajuba Hill ? Such a contention would be an invincible argu- ment for avenging ..Majuba Hill, and we should deeply regret to see the question argued in that way. No; if we still desire to do justice to the Outlanders we shall be unfair to regard them as unworthy of it, or as undeserving of all the efforts we can make in their behalf. If the Boers arm to refuse justice to the Outlanders the whole case is transformed.</p>
        <p>Fon a second time in half a Eagland's Example. century, this country has</p>
        <p>submitted to an arbitration in a dispute in which the United States was in- terested. The Alabama award went against us, and we paid the money; and now we are asked to accept a slight modification of the claim to a boundary of British Guiana which we advanced in 1841. There has been a growing feeling in favour of international arbitration, and we have always desired to resort to it, wherever we could, in the settlement of disputes. It is no reflection upon our consistency to say that we have declined the kind of arbitration that would have satisfied President KRUGER as to some of the points in dis- pute between us. The position of the Transvaal,and the question at issue, and the temper of the Boers make us unable to accept an arbitration tribunal. Yet there was a time when, if President KRuaErt had been more amiable and less obstinate,possibly the whole series of questions might have been dis- posed of in some other manner. What we say now is that we have resorted to arbitration more</p>
        <p>than any other Power in the world, and that this is proof of our belief in a civilised principle. The Behring Sea award has not been accepted by the United States, and the Delagoa Bay award is not yet made known. Our example may be cited as a reply to some of the observations which are being made about us on the Continent. Frenchmen, who forget how we settled the Niger question, have no right to throw stones at us as a blood- thirsty and truculent people, ever ready to bully the weak and to embarrass the strong. We have always done our best to avoid war whenever 3 settlement could be obtained in any other agree- able way. We joined the Hague Conference, and though it was manifest that some of the Powers were more anxious to cripple our offensive and defensive forces than promote disarmament and establish international peace on permanent lines, it cannot be said that our behaviour was inconsistant with our past, or with the terms [upon which we took part in the Conference. The long dispute with Venezuela is at an end, and she will receive some slight conces- sions, to which no real objection will be raised in this country. If other countries would follow our example, instead of criticising our conduct, there would be more hope of a reduction in the number of wars for mere greed and lust of power. Our Imperial possessions are so extensive, and they touch so many races and interests, that we are rarely in a state of profound peace and calm. The fact is that our Empire has grown so much, and of late so rapidly, that instead of the" Little Englander" being an object of cheap and noisy derision the time is coming when "Big Englander" will be the nickname of politicians of the " swelkd head" variety, who have parted with common sense, and are determined to copy the fatal ex- ample of the Roman Empire. Patriotism, how- ever, is not of one kind only, and the man who ostentatiously proclaims love of coutitry to mean taking more territory at some remote point—half China, for instance—or adding to our black Empire, as the late LORD DERBY called it, is not to be regarded with respect. He is a type of the politician who would make his love afar to be s ite at home</p>
        <p>Revelations, sure?y the most revolting ever heard even in a police court, were made in the evidence concerning two cases of child neglect which came before the magistrates at the Run- corn Petty Sessions on Monday. The homes, as described by the officers, were in a condition which the imagination absolutely fails to conjure up. Filth of every kind, and insects of almost every verminous variety, were here, there, and everywhere. The children whose lot it was to live in such surroundings naturally bore evidences of the suffering and injury to health they had sus- tained. In each case the husband was a hard- working man cursed with a wife addicted to drink and absolutely indifferent to the condition f her children and her home. The magistrates marked their sense of disgust at the conduct of these de, graded females—we cannot call them women--. by sending one to gaol for six months and the other for one month, both to undergo hard labour. It is nothing to the credit of the health authorities of Runcorn that such cases can be found in their midst, and if they themselves are unable to look after the interests and safety of the community in this respect they had better petition the N.S.P.C.C. for the appointment of a local officer. The Warrington representative of the Society will probably have his hands more than full if he has to undertake the purification of Runcorn slums in addition to those of his own town.</p>
        <p>Many of the Warrington ministers indulged in references, on Sunday, to the war cloud looming over the Transvaal. The most striking of any of the remarks we have seen reported fell from the lips of the Rev. H. G. R3berts, who has already earned a reputation in the town for,at least, calling a spade a spade. Presumably,the rev. gentleman's intention was to forward the peace movement, but we fear his utterances would have rather a contrary effect. He prayed (P)" that the government at Pretoria—a government characterised by venality corruption, and tyranny, a government led by the obstinate, wily, crafty, narrow-minded, canting old Dutchman who is being thrust by the force of circumstances into a position he has no right to occupy—may at the last hour respond favourably to the reasonable demands of their fellow citizens." The intention of the " prayer " may have been admirable but the wording of it is somewhat out of the ordinary run of sup- plications to the Almighty, and tends to destroy the peaceful spirit it might otherwise have engen- de red. Even" the man in the street," with his distorted view of the Transvaal crisis and his ple- thora of vituperative adjectives, would have a diffi- culty in slanging Paul Kruger with greater vigour and effectiveness than the Rev. H. G. Roberts has done from the pulpit. It is just this kind of com- ment on the Boer Government and its head which does so much to foster that spirit of blatant mili- tarism among the people which inspires them to clamour for war, and the avenging of Majuba Hill. We credit the Rev. H. G. Roberts with the best of intentions, but we cannot commend his choice of language in advocating the cause of "Peace on earth and goodwill to men."</p>
        <p>It is probable that the victims of the plague in Europe have to thank for their tribulations an Austrian hospital official, who sat down to his meals without first washing his hands. The de- tails of the outbreak in Vienna are within public memory,but very few people in England are aware of the circumstances which led up to it. Hospitals in Vienna, and hospital nurses, have been not in- aptly compared by Miss O'Connor Eccles to that period when Sarah Gamp represented all that was best and brightest in British sick nursing. The Viennese nurses are recruited from the lowest and most ignorant class of domestic servants, "women who, because of incapacity, increasing years, or doubtful character, find it difficult to get a situa- tion elsewhere." The Roman Catholic religion, which is all-powerful, has not yet induced any of its well-bred or educated lay followers to tend the sick or to take any part in hospital management. The Austrian hospitals are regarded by the poor with the utmost horror. Decency le outraged for the gratification of the merest visitor; no woman can go in as a patient, it would seem, unless she is prepared to sacrifice her self-respect. The nurses are allowed to blackmail the patients. If the latter will not pay they get no attention, and no complaints to higher officials can secure re- dress. Patients who are dying of thirst in the night can seldom secure a drink till nine in the morning. The sanitary arrangements are vile. The surgeons and physicians appear to be eager scientists, but they make no attempt to alleviate the sufferings of the patients, who might, perhaps be equally easeful in the hands of Spanish Inquisitors of the old times. On the whole, the slffering stranger might well prefer to be taken ill in London rather than in Vienna.</p>
        <p>The retirement of Mr. D. W. Finney from the headmastership of Heathside Schools is an event worthy of a place—if only a minor place—in the history of Warrington. When a man has spent thirty-five years of his life in one service, and that service, too, the education and upbringing of the younger generations, the termination of his task is an occurrence of more than passing impor- tance. During the period in which Mr. Finney has laboured at Heathside the population of the town has doubled, and its prosperity and influence have increased in corresponding ratio. These developments, doubtless,are to some extent the outcome of Mr. Finney's work, for it cannot be questioned that the knowledge he has instilled into the minds of so many of the inhabi- tants must have resulted, as the acquisition of knowledge always does, in the expansion of power. That power, obtained individually, has been used</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <p>collectively for the benefit of the borough of War- rington, in the growth of which, therefore, Mr. Finney has played no unimportant part. He has not been a public man in the sense that the phrase usually implies, but he has nevertheless obtained a wide popularity by his unvarying courtesy and geniality, added to a sincerity in and devotedness to his responsible work which has been a matter of common knowle;lge. The substantial present of which he was the recipient on Wednesday evening was a recompense for unflagging service which was never better earned, and, we believe, never more readily given.</p>
        <p>We cannot say much in favour of the Runcorn Rural District Council's attempt to deal with the sewage of Helsby. They have bestirred themselves in the matter, and gone so far as to cause a Local Government Board Inquiry to be held with re- gard to their borrowing £5,200 for the necessary bewage works, but this is about all that, in our opinion, they deserve credit for. The scheme it- self, as unfolded before the Inspector, appears to us to be a preposterous one in many respects. The main idea is to pump the sewage into a stream with the euphonious name of Hol- pool Gutter, and it will then flow into the Snip Canal entirely untreated,or as the Runcorn autho- rities put it, in a " crude "state. No wonder the Ship Canal Company offer some objection to the scheme, and little wonder, too, that there is objec- tion from other parties more or less directly con- cerned. There is one point distinctly in favour of the scheme and that is its simplicity. How many authorities would be delighted beyond measure to find so easy a solution of their sewage problems as the pumping of the refuse directly into the Ship Canal ? There is no doubt that some townships do adopt this plan surreptitiously and the result, as far as the Canal is concerned,has been painfully evident t,4 the olfactory nerves of those whose lot it is to reside in the vicinity of the great waterway. Really, if authorities are going to come before the Local Government Board with cool requests to turn their filth directly into the Canal they will cause something in the nature of a revolution. The idea is an outrage on decency and public health, and we hope the Canal Com- pany's protest will be persisted in and sustained by the Board. What the Canal would be like if such things were to be allowed beggars description.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>ESTIMATES FOR THE HALF YEAR.</p>
        <p>The ordinary monthly mesting of the above Board was held at the Council Chamber,in the Town Hall, Runcorn, on Wednesday evening. There were present :—The Rev Canon Maitland Wood (chair- man), E Imison, J Littler, W J Cunningham, W Handley, F J Norman, J W Lightburn (clerk), and A Lightburn (assistant clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>SCHOOL MANAGEMENT.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL MANAGEMENT.</p>
        <p>The School Attendance Committee held pr:or to the Board meeting took into consideration the question of the precepts.—Tho resignation of Miss A. M Kdburn (headmistress of the evening continuation school) was received, and Miss Jane Shaw, of St. Helens, appointed in her stead.—The minutes were adopted on the motion of Mr. Imison, seconded by Mr. John Littler. TEIE PRECEPTS :NO CHANGE.. _ _</p>
        <p>The Chairrnam moved the confirmation of the finance committee's minutes,which recommended that a precept for £1,050 be served upon the Overseers of the Poor for Runcorn, for the half-year ended March 25th. It was proposed to call the money up in two instalments : the first on the Ist of December, 1899, for £550 ; and the second on the Ist of March, 1900, for £soo.—Mr. John Littler seconded, and the minutes were unanimously confirmed. THE ESTIMATES SATISFACTORY.</p>
        <p>The Chairman also submitted the estimates for the half year ending March 25-,h, 1903, which were as follows :</p>
        <p>ESTIMATED RFCEIPTS, HALF YEAR ENDED MARCH 25, 1900.</p>
        <p>s. d. Balance at Bank, Sep. 29th, 1899 Free grants Sales to children</p>
        <p>--- Contributions from blind and deaf children 8 0 0 _ _ _</p>
        <p>Hire of rooms 7 0 0 _ _ .</p>
        <p>Grants under Agricultural Rates Act ....—.... ..... 8 9 6 Rent from cottages Miscellaneous , Overseers (say) School fees</p>
        <p>2074 12 9 ESTIMATED PAYMENTS ELLF YEAR ENDING MARCH 25TH, 100. £ e. d,</p>
        <p>Election expenses Salaries of Officers of the Board Legal expenses Other expenses sof administration Salaries remuneration of tetchers Books, apparatus and stationery 6O 0 Fuel. light, cleaning, repairs and renewal of furniture Rent, rates, taxes, and insurance 53 3 0 Cther expenses of maintenance Repayment of loans and interest 351 0 0 Repayment of Fee Grant overpaid on Granvi'le- street Schools lB 3 0 Maintenance of blind and deaf 4O 0 0 Enlargement of Vistoria-read School Furnishing of Victoria-road School Balance at Bank March 25th</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>2074 12 9</head>
        <p>2074 12 9</p>
        <p>In doing so, Canon Wood said they all hio-cl before them a very complete statement of the financial pros- pect, and he thought they eueht to thank their elerk for the very clear and cernpact way in which it had been drawn up and laid before them. They had,in two columns, side by side, the estimated receipts and the actual receipts for the corresponding period ; and like- wise with the expenditure for half years ending respec- tively, March 25th. 1899, and 1900. and they were able to see how each item compared with the other at a glance. It was the first occasion on which it had been put before them in that shape, and be thought they were indebted to Mr. Liglatburn (the clerk) for the very clear and satisfactory manner in which it had been done. With regard to the items of expenditure of the Board there were perhaps certain things that needed explanation. 'flee first was that there were no election expenses required for this year, an item of £67 last year. Dealing with the items separately (the Chairman) explained that there was an increase from £4l to £6O for books, apparatus, etc. This was due to a demand by H.M: Inspector for extra things for Granville-st'eel schools. He believed that Granville-street schools had not been fitted out for lectures and object lee:ons, and the extra money was for the purchase of specimens which were necessary for such lessons. Of course it was an expenditure they could not, neither desired, to avoid. In the £lBO for fuel, light, etc., there was an item of about £2O for renewal of furniture for Mill Brow Schools, due to the fact that the old stele of gallery in the infant school had been condemned as inadequate, and it was neces- sary to purchase desks to bring the school up to the mark. Rent, rates and taxes were ine.reased from £l7 to £53; of that amount £3l wag to be taken for the accommodation of the scholars from the Granville-street school at the Bethrda schoolroom, during the period the other buildings were undergoing improvements. They would also observe that loans and interest, Asc., had gone up some £4O, due to the fact that they had to borrow money for the improvement of Granville-street schools. Then came a very curious item of £lB 34 for re- payment of fee grantoverpaid on Granville-street schools. This be explained was due to the fact that the fee grant being paid on the average attendance of the previoue year, which was much higher than the present time, owing to the scholars migrating tolfictoria-road Schools, the Government by adhering to their principle had over. paid them that amount for Grsnville.street. They re- funded the money ; but there would be no less to the Board for the money had accrue to the Victoria- road Schools by the increased attendance there. There was no other point he thought which required explanation, but it was a matter for congratulation that they did not find it necessary to make a greater call on the Overseers than it was last for the corresponding half year, aid also to notice they had a better balance in the bank than there was before. Mr. John Littler seconded, remarking the Chairman having gone through the accounts so carefully, he did not think it necessary to add anything. It was a matter upon which they could congratulate themselves that there was no additional call to make. The motion was then put and carred nem, con.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>FRODSHAIi.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAIi.</p>
        <p>THE PARISH CHURCH.—The harvest festival was cop- tinned on Sunday, when the morning sermon was Preached by the Rev. R. Colston, senior curate, the Vicar, the Rev. H. B. Blogg, M.A., preaching in the evening, when there was a very large congregation. Special hymns and the anthem, "Sing to the Lord of Harvest," were rendered by the choir. At the conclu- sion of the service Mr. C. H. Hibbert gave an organ re- cital. The offertones amounted to £lB 193 111, and this sum will be divided between the Chester Infirmary and the Frodsharn Parochial Nurses' Fund. METHODIST FRES CHURCH.—The annual services took place on Sunday, the pastor (the Rev. E. C. Percival) preaching morning and evening. A special service was conducted in the afternoon, when Mr. J. B. Isaac pre- sided, the address being given by the Rev. T. H. Bailey. The interior of the church had been very tastefully decorated for the occasion. The main feature was a charmingly arranged table covered with choice fruits and flowers.</p>
        <p>UNDER THE OLOCK FOR GOOD BOOTS.—Advt. BEST BOOTS FOR ALL, UNDER TICE CLOCK.--Advt•</p>
      </div>
