      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>THE FLOWER QUEEN .-21 CORRECTION.</p>
        <p>In our report of the above last week we inadventently mis-printed the namei of Miss Cameron (violet) and Mis 3 Carter (tulip). JUNIOR FOITBALL MATCH.</p>
        <p>i - Playing n a match on the Canal•street ground,Bruns wick 'Guild Association, succeeded in defeating the Co- operative Stores omployes by 6 goals to 2 goals. THE RUNCORN HEATH HARRIERS.</p>
        <p>This club had its opening run last Saturday, when about 14 members started from the headquarters Halton-road for a seven mile spin over heavy country. A good finish was witnessed W Ray ; 2nd, W Thomas; 3rd R Hadfield ; 4h 8 Hopkins.</p>
        <p>A NEW RATE FOR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The rate for the ensuing half year was sanctioned before Messrs. W Timmrns and A. R. Norman, J.P.'s, on Wednesday, at the rate of is 31 in the £ on buildings, and Thd on lands, which is estimated to produce £3,480. The rate is ld less in the £ than for the last half year. CAR ACCIDENT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Oct Sunday, about noon, a cab driven by Mr. Henry Booth, Bridge-street,was proceeding along Church-street, when opposite Mr. Arthur Hough's one of the wheels came off, and the cab fell, damaging its axle. Luckily there was no one inside at the time. The driver also escaped injury. Help was obtained and in a short time the wheel was " fettled up "sufficiently for the removal of the vehicle to the Coach-houses in Bridge.street to await thorough repair. A" HUE AND CRY" AT RUNCORN:</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoon last, a " hue and cry " was raised by Mrs: Johnson of Norfolk-street, over the loss of her little child, and for the greatest portion of the after- noon the parents and neighbours, assisted by Police Sergeant Brewster were engaged in looking for the lost one. Then it was deemed advisable to call in the ser- vices of Mr. Thomas Saunders, bellman, and for the great space of an hour and a half he dinned the intelli- gence of the said loss into the ears of an awe struck public! All the time this hubbub was going on the little child was fast asleep upstairs in one of the rooms atl home, and was eventually found there by the almost distracted parents. DISORDERLY SCENE IN A WIDNES-STREET. Before Alderman Sadler and Major Lewis. on Monday, at the Widnes Police Court, John Coney was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Victoria-road, on Saturday night. P.C. Falkingham proved the case, atd , said prisoner behaved violently, and threw him in the mud, making a mess of his clothes. It was his 20th ap- pearance,—Peter Hogan was charged with being drunk and disorderly on Saturday night in New road. P.C. Jones proved the case, and said great difficulty was ex- perienced icconveying him to the stAtion.—George Wil- kinson, of the tramp class, was charged with being drunk in Lowerhousc-lane, on Sunday night. P.C. Hall said he found prisoner about 11.30 fast asleep on the ground. It was raining .very hard at the time.—Fines of 103 and costs were imposed in each case, or 14 days hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE,</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE,</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the above society was held last Saturday evening' in the Greenway-road School, under the presidency of Mr. Thomas Cooke. There was a fair attendance of members, considering the unfavour- able weather. Mr. Samuel Tomlinson spoke of the im- portance of members attending regularly. Mr. D. Eis- brown supported the remarks of the previous speaker. He said they met as temperance men week after week to encourage each other. There was undoubtedly an inspiration in numbers. As" iron shsirpeneth iron, so a man sharpeneth the countenance of his friend." Their object was to have their minds informed on the temper- ance question, so that they might be able at all times to give an intelligent reason for the cause they had em• braced. The Chairman then stated that during the quarter they had paid 33 sick members the sum of £6B 9s. Of this amount £25 4s. 4-d. had been paid for acci- dents, and the rest for ordinary sickness. It was de- cided to pay 10s 61 to the District Nurse Fund, and also 103 6i for the " Cadets." After the usual business had been transacted the meeting closed. HARVEST HOME AT THE PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest home festival, at the Parish Church,was concluded on Sunday last,w hen the preacher both morn- ing and evening was the Rev. A. H. Fish, 8.A., B.Sc., head master of Arnold House School, Chester. The sub- ject of the morning discourse was "The joy of harvest." In the afternoon there was a special;service for children, but owing to the unfavourable weather the attendance was limited. The church was well filled at evensong, when the singing of the choir formed an important feature of the service. They appeared at their best in Smart's anthem "Praise the Lord, 0 nay soul." The singing was of a very spirited character. and proved as usual to be a great help in the service. Mr. Johnson at the organ fulfilled his duties with his customary tact and ability.—Sir David Gramble, of St. Helens, has accepted an invitation to perform the opening ceremony on the first day of the bazaar to be held at the end of the current month, in aid of the renovation of the Parish Church and for other purposes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>LATE MR. W. WHITTAKER.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. W. WHITTAKER.</p>
        <p>The following is an extract from the " Parish Magazine" for October and is from the pen of the Rev. Canon Wood :—" In my letter for September I referred to the importance of the annual meeting of the subscri- bers te the National Schools, to be held on Monday, September 25th, but I had little idea that when that meeting took place the best friend, perhaps our schools ever had,would have passed from this world to a better. Yet so it has been ordered, and in this life we shall never again look upon the face of our dear kind hearted treasurer. Mr. William Whittaker, as you all know. slept away peacefully on Monday afternoon, September 11th, about four o'clock, and a very great loss we have all sustained. He was elected treasurer of the schools on the resignation of Mr. R. C. Whiteway, some time towards the end of 1865, and held the office till the end. I shall not attempt to say more of his good life and god work. Personally I owe him much, he was the very first to meet us and welcome us to Runcorn in 1886,and from that time until now he proved himself to be a faithfuliand unwavering friend.'A middle aged parishoner said to me :—" I shall never forget Mr. Whittaker, he was the first person who, when I was a hey, gave me sixpence, and I have not forgotten it to this day." A simple story, but quite characteristic of thelman."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUNCORN COUNTY COVRT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN COUNTY COVRT.</p>
        <p>The business transacted at this Court on Tuesday, before His Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes and Mr. D. J. Jolliffe registrar was of a routine nature. There were 27 original plaigts ; one default plaint, and twQ adjourned plaints, and 13 judgment summonses dealt with.—Rob.rt Wallace, draper. Hoole-road, Chester, claimed 6s 3d for goods sold and delivered from James Lockett, of Helsby. Defendint's wife who appearel denied the claim.—Daniel Dewar, in the employ of Mr. Wallace, said that the goods werestspplied to the defend- ants wife, who four years ago paid the last installment, of a running account of is,leaving the 6s 34 now claimed still due. He had particulars of the transaction in an- other book at home.—Defendant denied any knowledge of the transaction; she had, however, dealt %Ma a Mr. Beck, of Chester, but had paid him all. Mr. Beck was now dead and those people who were supposed to have bouzlit his place were claiming from her.—His Honour decided that plaintiff should pay the costs for that day, and the ease would be adjourned to the next court.</p>
        <p>A RUNCORN BOATMAN COMMITTED TO THE QUARFER</p>
        <p>SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police-C,On Wednesday morning, before Mr. Francis Wiswall, Enoch Robinson, boatman, of Runcorn, was charged with having on the 3rd of October, stolen two pairs of boats, the goods of Anthony Bradley.—Thomas James Walker said its was manager of Mr. Bradley's shop in Bridge-street,Runcorn. About ten o'clock on Tuesday morning he was in the shop and noticed the prisoner looking at the boots bang- ing outside the front door. A few minutes later he saw the prisoner snatch two or three pairs off a hook. He went outside and found two or three odd boots on the pavement, and saw the prisoner going up High-street with the others under his arm. He crossed over to the Police Station and gave information of the theft.—P.C. George Carrington, stated that in consequence of what the last witness told him he went in search of prisoner. He found him in High-street with the boats (produced) in his possession. He took him to the Police Office and charged him with the offence. Prisoner replied " I want to get away for the winter." As prisoner was drunk at that time, he (witness) again charged him with the theft of the boots about 7 o'clock when he made no reply.—The usual warning having been de- livered by the Magistrates' Clerk, pridoner stated I was drunk when I did the job or 2188 I should never have done it."—The magistrates decided to cornmitt prisoner to the Quarter Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>SUDDEN DEATH AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Dobson, coroner, held an inquest on Saturday afternoon last, at the Runcorn Police Court, on the body of Mrs. Elizabeth Acton, (widow of the late Mr. Joseph Acton, mariner), of 5, Lyon-street, Runcorn, who died in e. neighbour's arms murmuring " Lord have mercy on my soul." when about to go out shopping, on Friday night last. Ellen Acton, said she was a daughter of the deceased. About quarter past 7, on Friday night, witness and her mother were getting ready to go out on business. Deceased complained of " laavinig the wind." She asked witness to get her some ginger and water. Witness did so. Later dereased asked witness to loose her clothing, which she did : and also rubbed her back for her. Deceased then said "Lord hav3 mercy on my soul " and leaned her head back and died. Dr. McLennan was sent for and came. Her mother had suffered from rheumatic fever, which was said to have weakened her heart. She did not remember her mother being attended by Dr. McLennan. Dr. Robinson attended her for the rheumatic fever.—The Foreman: Had your mother ever complained ef her heart f—Witness : No.—Maria Fletcher, wife of Mr. Thomas Fletcher,l9, Briscoe-street, said she was present at tbe death. Deceased died in her arms murmuring "Lord have Enemy on my soul" in the manner described by the previous witness.—The Coroner observed it was evidently a simple case of sudden death, but the inquiry had been necessary to ascertain the cause.—The jury re- turned a verdict of "death from natural causes, to wit, heart disease."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <p>• . •• • •• . .• . ••• • .7.4: - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY OCTOBER 6, 1899</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY OCTOBER 6, 1899</p>
        <p>INTERESTING PRESENTATION TO MR. JOSEPH Fox. There was quite a congenial gathering of the friends of Mr. Joseph Fox, at Brunswick Schools, on Wednes- day night, asemblei for the purpose of presenting him with a token of their high appreciation of his services as a teacher at that admirable school. Mr. T. Simister took the chair and spoke highly of Mr. Fox's value as a worker in the cause ot the Master. Mr. Fox had enter.: d the school in 1138 and became a teaciier in 184'3, and since that time had held a similar position up to the present day. Mr. Fox's work was a patt3ro to their young people which should be emulated. He presented a beautifully illuminated address and a purse of god to Mr. Fox as a tangible sign of their appreciation of his value. The address was signed by the superintendents of Branswick-street School : Messrs T Simister and E Tayloreand also by the secretaries, Messrs J Unswotth, J Birkett and J Porah on behalf. of the school, all of whom spoke highly of Mr. Fox and his work.—Mr. Fox feelingly replied and thanked his kind friemds for their seasonable gift ; and also expressed the thankfulness with which he was filled at being allowed to participate for so long in the work of God.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PARISH CHURCH. In connection with the Yuung Men's stall at the forth- coming bazaar, a recital was given in the National Schools nu Wednesday evening, by Mr. Barnish Barns- dale, the eminent elocutionist. There was an excellent audience,and they listened to an admirable exposition of the art of elocution, of which Mr. Barusdale is un- doubtedly a past-master. The programme consisted of selections from the writings of Dickens, Shakespeare and other well-known authors, and the audience demon- strated in no uncertain manner their appreciatiun of Mr Barnsdale's histrionic abilities, by means of which he had his hearers alternately in tears and laughter. His recitals of " Ebenezar Scrooge," " Tne game of life," " Hamlet," (part of Act V.,) and "Bill Adams at Water- loo" were especially fine. The Rev Canon Wood, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. Barnsdala, hoped that the excellent audience was only a foretaste of the suc- cess they hoped to have at the end of the month:</p>
        <p>'house and children in September. He went there. The children were away, and he did not see them. The house was in a filthy condition ; and he could not only corroborate the Inspector, but would go further and say that it was " worse than the 72 stinks of Cologne." In justice to the owner he might say that he mentioned the matter to bins, and learnt that the people were some £7O or £lO in debt, and virtually lived rent free. Constable. Fry statedi be also visited the place, and could corroborate as to its filthiness. If their worships ' were to take a brush and sweep the walls they could "get a bucket full of bugs off "—(laughter)--they were so thick ; and the stink was something horrible. Constable J. Wilding, also corroborated, and added on the occasion of his visit there was a bucket full of stuff upstairs he should not care to describe, and he could not se the ceiling tor bugs. Ho never in all his life saw so many ; they were hanging down. (sensa- tion.) The male defendant, on oath, said he had his meals sent out to him to his work at the Runcorn Dock yard. He had done his "endeavour," and everything in his power with regard to keeping his home as it should be. He had for some time past spent the money himself and seen that food was procured for the children.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne said be did not doubt the min the least, and as in the other case he would withdraw the case against him on the same conditions. The promise was made and the husband discharged. The female defendant said she had nothing to say, and did not think she would require any witnesses. The Chairman observed, it was a very sad case they had been obliged to listen to. They were sorry to see the woman there under those circumstances, but she would be committed to one month's imprisonment with hard labour, in the hope that it would bring some re- form.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL REPORT FOR 1898-9.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Urban District Council meeting, held at the Town Hall, Runcarn,on Thursday morn- ing, Mr. J. Wilson (chairman of the Technical In- struction Committee) submitted the minutes of that body :—" That a further sum of .ClO be ex- panded in the purchase of science apparatus for the day school ; also that casts be purchased for the art school, at a cost of £5, and that the annual report be adopted."glnldoing so he abservedfthey were asking for the £l5 in order to keep pace with the educatioa department requirements. From the reports,in the hands of all the members, they could see that the result of the last session had been satisfactory, and she wed a marked improyement on the previous year. The Committee was pleased te be I able to say the staff was an efficient I one; a fact which was to be seen in the success of their efforts. Referring to the new wing, Mr. Wilson wished to put on record their indebtedness to Mr. James Wilding, the surveyor, for the admirable way in which he had planned the arrangement of that building, which had been put up for less than the estimated cost, and there were no extras. (Laughter.)—Mr. Lightburn : Is that Mr Wilding's payment ?—(lncreased laughter.. —s4r. Wilson : The cost has been .C 540 and Mr Wilding has kept it within the limit, Mr. Wilding simply receiving an expression of thanks. (Hear, hear.) The Committee also wished to thank Sir John Brunner for the donation of £3OO. Mr. Samuel Taylor seconded. The report, a summary of which is appended,was then approved and adopted unanimously :</p>
        <p>ANNUAL REPORT FaR -SESSION 1893-9.-LTLIE DAY</p>
        <p>SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>At the commencement of the year there were 101 pupils in attendance, 86 being in the Secondary School and 15 in the Preparatory School, as against 95 pupils for last year. Of these fifteen held scholarships, the fees being paid by local donors. Six held county scholarships. EVENING SCIENCE AND ART SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Science Classes.-16 classes have been held in 11 subjects. 179 students were on the register, the number being the same as last year. There were 141 individual students. It is satisfactory to note that 16 students took applied mechanics, and six took steam.</p>
        <p>Technology J.-..c.—Five subjects were taught, viz: —Theoretical carpentry and joinery, cookery,dress- making, French, and Shorthand. There were 85 students as against 119 for the previous year. There were 82 individual students.</p>
        <p>School of Art.—The number on the registers was 123, compared with 44, and there were 67 in- dividual students. Great interest has been dis- played in one of the new subjects, that of Clay Modelling and the results have been very good. The total number on the registers of the Institute was 387 as compared with 342 last year, and 290 individual students as compared with 296. The Grants.—The grant received from the County Council was £325 with a further grant of £l9 13s 8d in respect of the advanced stages of Science subjects. The grants received from the department were £62 12s 6d for Science, and £29 lOs lid for Art, total £92 3s sd. The grants for the past session are not yet known. Students fees amounted to £63 9s as against £54 14s V,.</p>
        <p>Sick Nursing Lectures.—Six lectures were given in the third course by a lecturer provided by the Cheshire County Council. Nine satisfied the ex- aminer and gained certificates. A table of results and examinations and state- ment of accounts was eppended showing the actual receipts to be £1,338 15s 10d from all sources, and the actual expenditure to be £1,268 13s 4d; £lO 17s 101 was the adverse balance April Ist, 1898, and £59 4s 8d the balance in hand, for the present year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES:</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES:</p>
        <p>[By A CORRESPONDENT.' What causes our milk to " turn " so very soon? is a question often asked by the Runcorn house- wife; and to this question a very ingenious answer has been put forward showing that to a great ex- tent the blame lies with the farmer who supplies the article. As is well-known, when the milk is drawn from the cow its temperature is decidedly high, it is then in the majority of cases measured off into the floats and at once driven off to the door of the consumer. The result of this is, that the warm milk, whilst in transit, goes through n semi- churning process, and thus is rendered more liable to "turn '' than would be the case if the tena- peratura of the new milk was reduced by the use of a refrigarator before being subjected to what the Chairman of the District Council the other day aptly termed" The riding and driving."</p>
        <p>Serious complaints come to hand of the water at Halton, which Mr. Thomas Wright (the Rural Dis- trict Councillor for that place), asked should be analysed by the County Analyst. I understand that the result of the analysis shows the water to be of " doubtful "character. As the larger portion of the water supplied to Halton is derived from the Run- c?rn mains the conclusion seems to point to Run- corn's own supply baing none of the best. In face of this, and in face of other facts that are ominously cropping up, I ask, is it wise to sink any more money in the Runcorn Waterworks—or had we better seek for another supply elsewhere, before it is too late?</p>
        <p>Before Runcorn can take its place amongst well- governed towns, several changes must be made. At present it is something like a hundred years' be- hind the times. An altered state of affairs must and shall come te pass The Urban District Coun- cil has control of the Waterworks, and o the burial ground, and the control of its own lighting should be obtained as soon as possible, either by the pro- vi3ion of the electric light; or by the purchase of the Gas Works undertaking: Indeed had the Gas Com- pany been bought out about twenty years ago the Runcorn ratepayers might to-day have been enjoy- ing the advantages of cheaper gas—a la Widnes— as well as have effected a saving equivalent to the amount pocketed by the shareholders of the Com- pany—a by no means small sum. Thera are many other quarters in which an economist coull point out defects in the government of our town.</p>
        <p>Another matter worth considering is, a campaign for the obtaining of reductions in the tolls for craft entering the port of Runcorn. This might be asked of the Ship Canal Company and if refused—like it no doubt will be—it can be persisted in, for there is no way so sure in breaking "hard stones"as to keep knocking them, and if the persistency of such a body as the Runcorn U.D.C. once succeeded in get- ting the reduction, Runcorn would to a great measure be "made," and Manchester would by no means be the sufferer.</p>
        <p>At the School Board meeting, on Wednesday, it was decided to issue a precept on the overseers for £1,050 for the ensuing half year in two calls ot £550 and £5OO. As compared witlethe cor:esponding half year of the last twelve months the figures are exactly the same. The "budget" of the Board does not exbibit any tendency to stride forward in the cause of education, but is simply based upon care- ful management of what already has been under- taken. It is a matter for congratulation (perhaps?) that the Board only intend spending what they must. The vacancy caused by the retirement of the Rev. Father Gore has not yet been filled, but is down for consideration on Thursday the 19th inst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>SAILOR DROWNED AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>A SAILOR DROWNED AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>About a quarter-past two, on Wednesday afternoon, as Mr. James Smith, of Norfolk-street, was proceeding down the old line of locks on the Bridgewater canal, he saw the body of a man floatina at the bottom of one of the locks. He at once gave information to Henry Moores canal constable, who caused the lock to be "lifted "and with asistance the body was taken out. A few minutes afterwards Police-Constable Wilding arrived with the wheel ambulance and the body was removed to the mortuary where it awaits identification. The general description of the deceased corresponds to that of James Bond, seaman, about 37 years of age, who was reported missing from the s.s. "Olaf" at Rua- corn on Sunday the 24th of September, which is as fol- lows :—"Height about five feet, conaplezion dark, eyes blue, light moustache, hair turning grey and thin on top of bead, bracelets tatooed on wrists, single breasted coat and waistcoat, dark coloured trousers, low shoes, and a peak cap."—The peak cap is, however missing, and only one of the wrists appears tatooed. There is a wound on the side of the head, and everything seems to point to the conclusion that the man fell into the lock on his way to his ship at night time. Two shillings and 511 in copper, a pipe and a handkerchief wera found on the deceased. THE INQUEST.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. E. White (deputy coroner) held an inquest on the body at the Runcorn Police Court on Thursday after- noon. Mr. James Lea was foreman of the jury. Mary Bond, Boston Terrace, Port Dinorwic, Carnar- von, identified the bely as that of her brother, James Bond, 37 years of age. He was an able seaman. Her brother was a single man and she last saw him alive in February last when he left home by train to go to Gars- ton. ShF, knew nothing more of him. He used to write home every week. She received a letter from him on Sunday morning the 24th of Septemt?r add,esaed from Runcorn. It had evidently been posted on the Saturday night. She believed the boat her brother worked on was the s.s. " Olaf."— Matthew James Smith, boatman. 27, Norfolk-street, Runcorn, said on Wednegday the 4th ef October about 2.15 p.m. he was following his boat up the locks, and saw a man's head in the fourth lock from the bottom of the line of locks on the Bridgewater canal He went and shut the locks and called the attention of the lock- keeper to the fact. The lock-keep.r providcd him with a rope which he hitched round the body, and with assistance the body was taken out of the water. The deceased was completely unknown to him. The body was handed over to Police-Constable 'Wilding. —Conetable John Wilding said he received the body as stated from the last witness. He removed the body to the mortuary and searched it. He found two shillings, and 51191 in copper, a pipe, and a handker- chief. There was "LB." on his singlet marked in cotton. On examining the body he feund the face was dis- coloured and bruised under the left temple. The back of the b3dy was also discoloured. There was a bracelet tateoed en his lift wrist. He would judge the man to be from 35 to 40 —By the Foreman : How long did he appear to have been in the water ?—Witness : Ten or eleven days.—Flcrenee Roebettosn, single woman, said she was a domestic servant at: the Mariner's Hotel Percival'e-lane, Runcorn. She had seen the body and recognised it as a gentleman who bad been in their place on the Sunday bight about 7 o'clock There were two more sailors with him belong- ing to the " Vaynol " steamship. The 3 men stayed in the house until 9.45. Witness did not know what they were served with for the landlord Mr. Edwards served them. The three seemed quite right and on friendly terms with each other. They were sober and shook hands with her on leaving.—Other evidence was heard. The Jury retired for a brief space, and on their return the foreman announced: They were agreed that the ' eased was "Found drowned" on the 4th of otober. Lee</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RUNCORN HOMES. REVOLTING REVELA.TIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN HOMES. REVOLTING REVELA.TIONS.</p>
        <p>A MOTHER SENTENCED TO SIX MONTHS. "WORSE THIN 72 STINKS OF COLOGNE 1"</p>
        <p>Before Me3srs. James Handley (chairman), Samuel Taylor, and Francis Wiswall, at the Runcorn Petty Ses- sions, on Monday, two shocking cases of neglected homes and children came on for hearing, the disclosures of which were of a most revolting character. The N.S.P.C.C. was represented by Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, Warringtoo, and Inspector Walter Howard Hackett of Warring:on. Michael Corcoran andlAnn Cercoran, man and wife,of Massey's Court Brunswick-street,Runcorn were charged, at the instance of Inspector Hackett, of the N.S.P.C.C., with the wilful neglect of their two children,aged 11 and 5 years respectively, on the 18th day of September and other dates, in a manner likely to cause suffering and unnecessary danger to their health.—Both pleaded not guilty. Mr. A. Browne, in his opening statement, submitted the case as one rather out of the ordinary. Tne Society found that nothing could be done with such people, as the defendants, unless very strong measures were taken. Then were two children in respect of which they were proceeded against, aged 11 years, and 5 years,re3pectively. These children had been so severely illtreated, that on one occasion Mr. Massey a neiehbour had found the defendants, in an entry with the children, and out of charity to the latter had allowed them to go into one of his empty houses. Since then defendants had taken advent ige of Mr. Massey's charity, and taken possession of the house, and would not quit it ; so that they were practically living rent free. In consequence of what he heard, ihepecter Hackett visited the house in company with Detective Bowyer, on the 24th Septem • her last, and the place had also been visited on previous dates. The Inspector on one of his various visits had seen the woman at the end of the entry. She appeared to be under the influence of dr;nk. Mrs. Corcoran, evaded the Inspector by entering a neighbour's house and going into another street. Subsequently Inspector Hackett again visited the house, when he found the woman and children in the house. He examined the children and found them fairly well nourished, but their bodies were dirty ; heads nitted and verminous. Everything in the house was filthy, and the whole place almost destitute of furniture, and though it was a miserably wet day there was no fire in the grate. With regard to food there wae nothing but a piece of bread and butter upon a dirty shelf. The kitchen was extremely dirty, and all the dirt had simply been shoved under the tables and other places. The bedrooms were disgraceful, there being nothing but an old iron crib, with an old flock mattress there, and as for bed-clothing the place was destitute of any. The Inspector had asked the female defendant where they slept, and she told him in lodgings. He had enquired about that statement and found it wee not so. "The Inspector bad also visited them when they lived in Mersey-street, sometime previous and on that occasion the state of the house was such that on the matter being reported to the Nuisance Inspector the people were turned out by him. Dr. F. McDougall had examined the chil- dren, and would give his report about them. It was not a case in which the people could say they had been given no chance, for the society had given them chance after chance. William Massey, shopkeeper, residing near the bottom of Brunswick-street, Runcorn, said he was the owner of some property its that locality. Some weeks ago, he should say about six, he saw the two children standing in the entry as described by the solicitor, and taking pity on them be- cause they said they had no where to go he gave them some sticks and coal to make a fire with, and gave them permission to go into one of his houses, which, was then empty. The defendants had come there and stayed without any further consent from him. He lived close by and heard the man go out regularly every morning to his work at Weston Point. He believed the man to be in regular work there. He had often seen the woman in a drunken condition. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Inspector Hackett, deposed to having the defendants under observation for two or three months. He cor- roborated all that had been said by Mr. Browne, and submitted a detailed account of the filthy conditions under which the children were compelled to live. Detective Bowyer, of Runcorn, corroborated the In- spector in every detail. He visited the home at night time on one occasion and found that the male defendant slept in his clothes in a chair. The children slept on an old iron crib of very small dimensions. They also slept in their clothes and had no covering whatever. . .</p>
        <p>_ Dr. F. lAcDougall said be examined the children or rather one of them, the other decamped with the mother before he could get hold of her. He found her to be fairly well nourished. Her hair was nitted and lull of small" nitts," and also some lice. fie considered that the house they were in was not fit for human habitation and there was not a vestige of anything the house likely to make the children comfortable. The male defendant, on oath said he had lived in Brunswick-street for about five or six weeks. They had two children aged 10 years and 4 years. He went over to Weston Point, where he was a dock labourer, every morning, but had no reguiar job. Some days he would earn 39., 45., or Sc., but other times he would have to go without a job for a day or two. lie had not had the opportunity or the money to furnish the house properly, and it was impossible to get things to provide the chil- dren with.—The Chairman: Water is cheap enough?— Defendant: Yes. That is right enough sir. But I cannot be at work and attending to the children.—Con- tinning defendant said he had never lost a day's work when there was any to be had. Mr. Brown?, : Is it a fact that the parish nurse has given your wife some sheets P—Defendant : Not to my knowlelge. I have no heard of them.—The female defendant also denied this. Mr. Browne said that he considered that the female was most to blame in this ease. The Chairman : We are quite of that opinion too. Mr. Browne : I propose, therefore, to withdraw the case against the man with your permission, if he gives an undertaking to look after the children, and do all he can to. put,the home straight. The only object the society have, in bringing them here is for them to realise their responsibility for the woman's omissions. We must have no empty promises thoueh. The undertaking was given by Corcoran, and the magistrates requested him to give the Inspector of the N.S.P.C.C. every facility for looking him up from time to time, as they were doing it all for his own good. The Sergeant of Police submitted the record books against the female Corcoran which disclosed 15 cases of drunkenness, 7 of obscene language and 2 comitt sic for 4 months and 2 for 2 months for neglect previously. The Chairman observed, with regard to the woman, it was a case in which punishment should be inflicted, and she would be sent to finutsford for 6 months with hard labour. THE WORST CASE YET. ALLEGATION AGAINST THE SANITARY INSPECTOR.</p>
        <p>Bernard Flannigan and Ann Flannigan, man and wife, of 39, Cooper-street, Runcorn, were then similarity charged with the wilful neglect of tlaeir four children on the 18th of September and other days.—Both pleaded not guilty. Mr. A Browne, said this was the first case that had ever been preferred against those defendants,but it was one of the worst within his experience. In this case the defendants, occupied a four-roomed house at a weekly rent of 3s. The male defendant was a labourer, at the Runcorn Docks, and very attentive to his work, and could not reasonably be ignorant of the state of his house. They were proceeded against in respect of the four children : Ellen (11), Joseph (9) Ann (7) and Mary (4). The wages of the man were 303 a week, a son earned 10s, and there was another son who had earned money, but who was at present out of work. Pienty of opportunity of reforming had been given, and the In- spector had the place under observation from the 20th of July to the 18th of September, and he would now say that instead of improvement, the last state of the house was worse than ever. On Mr. Hackett pointing out the horrible state of the house to the woman, she had admitted it was herself that was te blame, as she drank ; and also had pawned things. The female had also been repeatedly warned by Constable Fry. The pro- perty belonged to Mr. Imison, though he could net say what bearing that might have upon it, but he was going to say, for the information of the public, that Inspector Hackett went to see the Inspector of Nuisances about visiting the house ; but for some reason, he (Mr. Browne could not tell, the Nuisance Inspector refused to go and visit the house, although he was told of it. He (Mr. Browne) did not know what other people might think, , but he was of the opinion that it was a gross neglect of duty on the part of the Nuisance Inspector not to visit the house. _ . _</p>
        <p>Inspector Hackett said he had been watching the house for something like three months before the 20th of July. On that date he ascertained something of the condition of the place and of the four children. He visited the house in company with Constable Wilding. They then saw the mother and the youngest child. The kitchen, he noticed, was dirty, and the dirt, old bones, and the other rubbish had simply been swept or pushed under the table. The walls and ceiling were simply one mass of filth. There was a little bread and butter in the house; but as regarded furniture the Inspector described the place as almost destitute. On going upstairs he found the girl Mary lying on a dirty bed with no covering save an old shirt which was filthy dirty. Her body was fairly clean though badly bitten by vermin. The bed she was lying:on'consisted of a delapidated iron bed-stead old flock mattress with the flocks flying all over the room, and no covering whatever. The other details given by the Inspector showed that the place was teaming with filth and vermin, and the stench arising therefrom must have been detrimental to the health of the children. He corroborated his solicitor's statement, and spoke of Lhz great many visits be paid to the place and of the opportunities the woman had had of improving things. She never took any notice. Referring to the conversa- tion with the Nuiastr..,ae Inspector, Inpector Hackett said that in refusing to visit the house the Nuisance In- spector had assigned his reason for not visiting the place to be that if he was required to give evidence he thought he would have to consult his committee about it.</p>
        <p>Dr. F. McDougal!, said he wss asked to examine the</p>
