      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. -- WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. -- WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The heavy rain on Sunday afternoon militated against the attendance at the meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. Mr. Kent presided and founded a brief address on the words "Go work to-day in my vineyard," He stated that evidences of progress were on every hand, and we were apt to forgot the benefits we enjoyed. In every department of human life there had been a change for the better. Christianity itself had made wonderful progress but there was still much to be done, and it was by individual effort that greater results would be obtained.—Mr. George J. Brown, of Warrington, delivered a very thoughtful address in 1 which he showed how the claims lof Christ superceded those of others who had lived on earth. Not only did He teach brotherbsod to men but he lived and practised it in his life. No movement designed for the betterment and uplifting of mankind could be Wasting which had not as its root princinle the religion of Jesus Christ. Referring to the book " In His Steps " the speaker said they knew that in some quarters it had been severely criticised and by risen who might have found better employment. They told us the ideal was too high, but the standard could not be lowered to suit men's tastes and fancies. We needed to bring our- selves nearer the standard and follow mom closely the precepts and example of Jesus Christ. Christianity had founded and maintained the philanthropic institutions which were happily now so many. On Saturday, a street collection was taken in the town for the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission This was a Christlike work and deserved the support of all lovers of their fellows. He hoped the promoters realised a sum to carry on their work; and that those pre- sent took the opportunity of doing something to help their unfortunate brethren and sisters. When he saw the great social gulf which separated sections of our own people the question often arose why should these things be. There were hundreds and thousands of honest, temper- ate, hard-working men, who after having struggled through life and brought up their familiee, had had little opportunity to prepare for a rainy day, and when they bad committed what appeared to be the greatest of all sins—become old—they bad to depend upon the help of their children, or be compelled to live with those who through intemperate habits and reckless living had brought thenvelves to poverty. He knew it was a great qnestion,and one that had troubled the wisest men,but he hoped something might be done to save the honest poor from the degradation of the Workhouse. (Hear, hear.) In conclusion, the speaker referred to the Transvaal crisis, and said they could only regret that England seemed likely to close her history of the nineteenth cen- tury with such a dreadful strife.—Mr. Farrar called attention to the National Memorial against war which was open for signature in the vestibule. Let them have arbitration first, and fighting afterwards. (Hear, hear.) At the close of the service a large number of men signed the petition. The service vs as also taken part in by Mr. Robins.</p>
      </div>
