      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>WARRINGTON POLYG LOT SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON POLYG LOT SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of the second season of this institution was held on Wednesday evening at the Old Warrington Academy when a very profitable evening was spent. The President (Mr. H. M. Ash- ton) made very clear the object of the Society, which is to provide facilities for verbal intercourse in foreign languages or affording such muttkal help as cannot be attained except by regular Con Act with foreigners. The members are for the most part interested in foreign languages from a com- mercial point of view, and the importance of keep- ing well in touch with the chief languages of com- merce cannot be overestimated, particularly in the case of Englishmen who have been so much ousted by the foreigner who has had opportunities of ob- taining sound knowledge of most of our important trades and markets and has then gone into severe competition with us. Now, however Englishmen are alive to the fact that a good knowledge of foreign languages is essential in the great com- mercial world and the work of the Polyglot Society in Warrington is to assist in maintaining that in- terest and enthusiasm in foreign languages which Icommercial interests demand. During the evening loccasion was taken to make such modifications in I the rules as were deemed desirable after the ex- perience of the first season. The title of the society will be in future the Warrington Polyglot Society, instead of the Warrington Polyglot Club as hereto- fore! It was also resolved that the following be sent on to the newly formed Birmingham Cosmo- politant Club :—Resolution: " That this, the first meeting of the second session of the Warrington Polyglot Society hears with great pleasure of the formation of a similar society in, Birmingham and hereby sends its heartiest greet- ings to fellow workers in the cause of commercial education. This society earnestly hopes that if a' any time mutual assistance can be rendered it will be rendered in that friendly spirit in which this es gratul!tien is sent and in which we trnrusstaigte of eil will be received," It is encouraging to know that new societies are now forming on the lines of the Warrington Polyglot Society and it is hoped that much service may be done by them. The officers and committee of the Warrington Polyglot Society for the seasion 1899 to 1900 are President,Mr II M Ashton,secretary,Mr E J Gerrard, treasurer, Mr E W Kirk ; committee, Messrs J W Bouchier, F G Sertland, J J Nield, J R W Comber.</p>
      </div>
