      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>ST. PETER'S CHURCH, NEWTON. Services of thanksgiving for the harvest were held in the above church on Sunday. The edifice bare evidence of the occasion, by reason of the decoratious consisting of fruit, flowers, and corn. The morning service was conducted by the Rev. Morley SPevenson, M.A., of War- rington, and in the evening by the Vicar, Rev. James Ryder. In the afternoon a musical service was given by Mr. P. Fairclough, organist, and the choir, which was slightly augmented. The following programme was rendered :—Organ," Marche Sacree ;" solo, " The Good Shepherd," Mr. W. Hindley Sinlth ; anthems. " 0 for a closer walk with God," soloist, Mr. .1; Mather ; organ, " Berceu.e in D;" solo, "The dream of Paradise," Miss A. Molyneux ; solo, "The Everlasting Day, Mr. W. Weleh ; organ, " Symphony and chorus." There were large congregations at each of the services and the offer- tories were in aid of the parochial funds. PARISH CHURCH. The harvest festival services were cmtinued on Sun- day at the Parish Church, when there were large con- gregations. The pulpit at the morning service was occupied by the Rev R T Brockman, vicar of St. John's Baptist, Tue Brook, Liverpool. At the evening Le rvi es, the Rev J F Howson, vicar of Christ Church, Chester, preached. The choir rendered the anthem, "Ye ehall go out with joy," Mr Woods presiding at the organ. Collections were taken in aid of the clergy fund.</p>
        <p>EMMANUEL CHURCH.- The harvest thanksgiving services were h,_qd on Sun- day, when the Rsv. E. T. Reed preached at both servioes to good congregations, appropriate references being made to the safe ingathering of the harvest- A chil- dren's service was held in the afternoon. At the evening service the choir rendered the anthem " Serve the Lord with gladness" in a very creditable manner. Collections were taken on behalf of the Nursing Association, and good sum was realised.</p>
        <p>ST. PETER'S. The harvest thanksgiving services were continued in the above church on Sunday. In the morning the ser- mon was preached by the Rev W Bracecamp. The Rev W A Knight, curate of St. Ann's, was the special preacher in the afternoon, when a children and young people's service was held. Two solos were creditably sung by Mr Alfred Pembertcn. At the evening service there was a crowded congregation. The service was in- toned by the Rev W Bracecamp (curate), whilst the pulpit was occupied by the Rev H S B3lton, vicar of Ravenhead. who preached from the word., " The fitches are not threshed with a threshing instrument, neither i. a cart wheel turned upon the cummin, but the fitches are beaten out with a staff, and the cummin bread and corn is bruised, because he will not be threshing it nor break it with the wheel of his cart, nor bruise it with his horsemen." Special music was renderel.the anthem at the morning service being " Thou visitest the earth," whilst in the evening the choir sang the anthem com- mencing " All Thy works praise Mee, 0 Lord," the tenor solo being taken by Mr Robert Knight. The offer- tories were in aid of the church expenses fund.</p>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY MISSION, FACTORY YARD. The harvest festival at Holy Trinity Mission was celebrated on Sunday evening. The room had been beautifully decorated with fruit, flowers, &amp;c., by Mrs. Carleton and Miss White. Mr. John White, of Moore Ball, conducted the service, and preached an excellent sermon from Matthew xiii. 39. The lessons were read by Mr. H. Taylor, P.C.M. The offertory, both then and in the afternoon, was given to the Police Court Mission, by the Rev. E.C.E. Carleton. A good sum was realized.</p>
