      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>BEFORE the ' IVIAGISTRATES. THEFT OF A WATCH AND CHAIN.-1 DROYLSDEN DUN'S</head>
        <p>BEFORE the ' IVIAGISTRATES. THEFT OF A WATCH AND CHAIN.-1 DROYLSDEN DUN'S</p>
        <p>INGRATITUDE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Monday, before Mr. Monks (presiding) and other magistrates, William Daley, 89, William-street, Droylsden, was charged with stealing Timm a bedroom in the dwelliue-hone of George Blundell, 49, Chorley-street, between eight and half-past ten on Saturday morning.a lady's watch and chain valued at £B.—Superintendent Monaghan asked for a remand till the following day. He said that the prisoner went to the prosecutor's house at eleven o'cleck on Friday night. Prosecutor knew him, and allowed him to sleep on the sofa during the night. When he came down the following morning be told prisoner he could go upstairs and sleep on tile bed. Prisoner went upstairs, and Came down about ten o'clock and disappeared mysteriously. A lady's watch and chain were missed from the bed- rocin. and prisoner sesmed to have gone to Manchester and pledged them. When arrested at Droyliden a pawn- ticket for a watch and chain was found upon him.— Prisoner was remanded.—When brought before the magistrates on Tuesday,prisoner pleaded guilty.—Super- Intendent Monaghan arrain stated tbe facts of the ease and added that £1 ESA 4d was found on the man when he was apprehended. He said he had spent the rest ot the money he got for the watch and chain in drink. In ans wer to the charge he said " Yes, I took it ; that's the ticket for the watch." Prisoner said he was very sorry for what had occurred. He did not remember taking the watch ; trilled been drinkiog for two days, and it most haveeffected him. He had a wife and three cbildren dependent upon him, and if the Bench weuld deal leni- ently with him be would promise never to take a drop of intoxicating drink Again. He could not have been in his right senses when he took the watch and chain, as it would have been the farthest thaight in his head to do a thing like that.—Samuel Moores, pawnhr.;ker, London-road. Manchester. was called, and said that when the prisoner pawned the wateh and chain at his shop at 11 o'clock on Saturday morning he was quite sober.—The Chairman (Mr. Birchall) said it was cue of the meanest cases he remembered ever having dealt with. It was a douhle crime, after the man treating, him so kindly and acting as egood Samaritan for him to go and steal his property and run away from the house. It was a very bad case indeeci.—The nr4gistrates decided to send prisoner to gaol for two months with hard labour, and or3ered the money found in his pos• aeveion to be returned to the pawnbroker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT, FRlDAY.—Before tha Mayor (in the chair), Mr. Birchall</p>
        <p>and other magistrates. TH7Fr F BOOTS. —Harold Sharpe, Liverpool, was charged with stealing a trir of boots, value 3s lid, from the shoo of Oliver end ro. Bridge Foot, the previous night.—The Chief Constable said the man was seen by the manager to take the boots from the clo+rway and walk off with them. He fol- lowed him and brought him back to the shop and gave him Into custody.—Prisoner admitted havink been convinted of felony three times at Liverpool. He was committed to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>MaNDlT.—Before Mr. F. Monks (in thA chair). Council!erg Shaw Green, and imethurst, and Messrs. S. Wright and</p>
        <p>Birchall DRIPTICENNESS.—Betsy Charnley, 22. Knntsforcl-road was charged with being drunk in St. James's-street on the 27th September. This being her first appearance, the case was withdrawn on defendant putting ts ed in'the poor box. Is FIGHT IN LIME-STREET.—WILLIAM CoNNOR AGAIN.—W;I- - Connor, 2. Hardy-street, and Bernard 0. Rourke 17, Dolman'elene,were summoned for committing is breach of the peace by fighting in Lime-street on September 23rd•—ronnor was not present when the case was heard.—P.V. Smith said that at 5 p.m. on the date in question he was proceeding through the Market Place when he heard a great deal of shouting and bad langutee in the neighbourood of Lime street. On procPeding there he saw the defendants fighting. Conner was bleeding on the left side of his face and Rourke had blood on the right side of his face Hs separated them. and ordered there away. but they retnrned. He had great difficulty in ascertaining the name of Rourke, who gave two false names and addresses.—Rourke said that two other men were fighting and he se-arated them and the officer reported him.—The Clerk: Why did you give a wrong name twice.—Rourke: I was too much excited. (Laughter.)—P.S. Smith said that the third address was also a wrong one —lt was decided to bind defendant over to keep the peace and order him to pay the costs (75.). and grant a Warrant for the apprehension of Connor--Before the business of the court was over, however. Connor made his appear- ance. He denied the offence and said that he had been dress- ing some geese and that was the reason he had blood on him--Mr. Birchen remarked that he had given a similar excuse before, but last time it was a rabbit he had been skin- ning. (Laughter.) The magistrates dismissed the case, oa defendant promising not to give so much trouble to the police in future —Councillor Shaw Green said he thought they should have been more lenient with the other man.—Mr. Birchen : He gave two false addresses.—lt was ultimately decided to dismiss the ease against Rourke on pay- ment et the costs of the summons (3sed.) OisscEarg LANGTTAGE —Thomas Kneekten. 1, Dial Court, who made his 23rd appearance, was on the evidence of P.O. Smith tined 5s and costs for mal.ing use of obscene language In Dial Court on September 26th. A DANGRROTTS PRACTICE.— Matthew Knowles, 14, New- road, a boy was summoned for using a catapult in Lord- street, on Thureday. -P.C. Clough said he saw defendant in Lord-street shooting in the direction of St. James's schools, and at railway carriages. He ran after him down several streets, and could not catch him for half an hoer. He was shooting little stones.—Sunerintendent Monaghan ettirl.the police had received eom plaint, from that district abont boys shooting with catapults. It was a very dangerous practice in- deed, particularly when they shot at railway carriages.— Defendant was fined 2s 61. SELLI,G FIRyWoR/CS TO CRILDREN.--gIITOPICEEPER FINED. —Jane Shaw. 85 Haydock-street. was semmoned for selling fireworks to James Taylor. a boy under 13 years of age. on September 24th.—P.C. Clark said that at 12.30 tn the morn- ing in question (Sunday) he was on duty in Havdock-street in company with P.C. Fish when he heard a discharge of fireworks, He sew the boy Taylor with some fireworks in his hand, and he told them that he had bought them from defendant's shop.—P.S. Cheney acid when defendant was granted a license she was warned about selling fireworks to children under thirteen years of age. He had had to cention her for having fireworks in her shop window which was against the Act - The Clerk said that defendant had no right to sell on Sunday.—Defendant said she did most sf her business on Saturday and Sunday.—A fine of 5s was imposed the Chairman remarkinx that if defendant was brought no again she would be more severely dealt with.—The Clerk said the maximum penalty was £5. DOG TTINMITzZLE.D —F. Percival, Rose Lea, Latchford, was fined Is for allowing his dog to be at large unmuzzled in Bridge-street on September 24th. CHIMNEY ON FIRE.- Robert Andersor, Croats Keys Inn, was fined Is for having his chimney on fire on the 21st September.</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
        <p>•• p. 65CaPo 9•• ' 4 ZsDi. ikt</p>
        <p>GRATE FU" -CO iVi FCTI N G.</p>
        <p>rk* itzkrh.,?</p>
        <p>tiR:IAKFA3T Ai'4D SUPPE;</p>
        <p>TUE3DLY.—Before Mr. Birchall, (In'the :chair) and Councillor Smethurst. _ _</p>
        <p>DISTURRANCF: AT rue PoL!CE STATION.—A.nnie av•-. 5 char4.ll with being drunk and disordelly at the Police Station at 7.45 the previous nig:A —P.O. Barnett said that at the time named priser was brought to the Police Station by .oetective qop-.r with refer-ince to another charge. She was drunk and m :de use of filthy language. Inspector Phillips tried to p trsuade her to eonduet her,elf in a prope, manner int': eh would not and he ordered her to be lo?ked up.—Pi and 53, ane C sts AY seven 61578. THURSDA.Y.—Before Mr. Chandley (in the chair), Alderman SUG on, and Messrs. John Fairclough and J. tiepherd. Tune.a.resuceo TO " FINISH " A POLICEMAN.—Brian Flaherty, Percival's-fold, and Patrick McCue, 17, Naylor-fit., were cusargel with being drunk and disorderly in Butter- market-street at 4.30 the previous afternoort.—P.C. Cooper (20) said that at 420 on Wednesday afternoon he was on duty in B ittermarkw-street. ne Li* the " King's Arms " Hotel, when the prisoners, who were drunk, came Uci to him. McCue asked if it woeld matter if h- took a barrel oft a lurry that was standing there. (Laughter.) He told them to go away, and Flaherty said it was a long time since he had a go" at him (witness), but the next go would be a finisher." Knowing the character of the man he tok him Into custody and with the assistance of a civilian he took him to the Pelice Station. Melue followed, and would not leave the Police Station so he was locked up.—The Chief Constable said McCue had been before the Court once, and Flaherty 15 times previously.—Flahurty was committed to gaol for 21 days. and McCue for 14 days. SEPARATION ORDER AGAINST AN ACTOR.—In the case of John Matthews, an actor, against whom a separation from his wife was granted, defendant to pay 15s a week towards his wife's maintenance, it was stated by Mr. Browne, who represented the complainant that he had not paid that amount. On the day the case was heard he sent a tel gram saying he would pay 15s and the order was made.—Defend- ant said he could not aff srd to pay 153 as his salary was only about 32. a w,ek.—rhe magistrates made an order for 10e. ,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SE3SION3</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY. Berore Captain Withing,,on (in the chair), and</p>
        <p>ML John White.</p>
        <p>A SEetoUS OFFENCE—Adam Robertson. Rixton. was summoned for having on the 16th September caused 99 sheep to be moved from Manchester to Rixton ar.d not having them dipped twice in a fortnight, contrary to regulation 1 of the Infectious Diseases (Animals) Act and Sheep Scab Order of 1898.-I'.C. Clarke proved the case. —Defendant pleaded ignorance.—The Magistrates' 0 ark said the full p-naltv was £5 tor each shsep.—The magistrates decided to line defendant 2 •s and costs. ()Lim Povey claimed £1 123 6d wages due to hir from David Taylor. laundry man, Winwick. —lt was stated that an order we made six weeks ago for Payment of the am unt. but defendant had not paid any- thing.—Defendant asked for a fortnight in which to pay the money.—An ord r wt.s made for defendant to pay the amount in a fortnight or go to gaol for a month. MINOR OFFENCES.—Thomas Daintith was fined 2s. 6d. and costs for being drunk at Poulton on the 16th (3' Septemher ; Philip Milburn. Warrington, ad Harry Milburn, Orford, were each fined 3s. 6d. for allowing their cogs to be at large unmuzzled ; Percy Wolfendea. Warrington, was fined 2s. 61. and costs for driving a horse and cart at Winwick on the night of the 24th September and not having a lighted lamp attached to the cart ; William Birch was fined ss. and costs for being drunk and disorderly at Burtonwood oa the 25th September ; May Longton was Seed Is. and costs for riding a bicycle on the footpath ac Sankey on the 23rd Septem- ber.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>?doltnaY.—Before Messrs. James Handley (presiding) Samuel Taylor. and Francis Viriew TRANSFER BUSINESS—The following license transfers were granted" Wheat Sheaf Inn," Bridge-street from rtarry Farmer, to Alfred Taylor; " Sharnrook " from Henry Barnes to Aliee Barnes; " Oddfellows Arms" from John Marsh to Alexander Carruthers. OBSCENE LANGUAGE ON SUNDLY.—Ann Bostook, Alcock- street, Runcorn, was summoned for using obscene language. in Alcock-street, on the 24th of September.—She pleaded not guilty.—P.C. Fry. said on Sunday, the 24th ult.. he went to Aloock-street, where he found the woman in the act of throwing a basin at another woman She used very bad language.—Defendant denied using bad language in Alcook- street. She had used it at her back door.—The Chairman said they considered the case proved ; but as the costs of the case were 9s 6d. and they were not desirous of sending her to to orison, a fine of 6d and 9s 6d costs would be inflicted. The money was paid. STONE THROWING PI THE STREEIS.—John Collins (15), Hughes' Court. Cooper-street, was summoned for throwing stones in the street. to the danger and annoyance of pas- sengers, on the 24th of September —The boy pleaded guilty. —P.O Fry stated on account of numerous complaints of the danger of stone throwing, he was on day in Cooper-street. he saw the boy throwing 'stones there.—Defendant was dis- charged, on promising never to do snagain, on payment of the costs 4s 6d. LoITRRING ON FooTPlTne.—John Ellison, John T "hillips, James Cunliffe, and John Nield, youths all of Runcern. were summoned for loitering on the footpath in King- et., on the 24th of September, to the annoyance of passengers —Defendants, with the exception of iPhillips,pleaded guilty; Phillips stated he was not on the footpath.—Constab!e Fry said. about 4 45, on the 24th of September, being Sunday, he was on duty in Cooper-street and King-street. in con- sequence of complaints of obstruction being received. He there saw the four defendant&amp; with about 15 or 20 ',nailer children loitering on the footpath at the corner of the street. They moved away on seeing him. He to!d them that he would report them as they were the big.iest. He wished to add that the door, shutters. and the woodwork of the shop near to where the defendants were loitering had been very bully cut and mutilated. He did net say that the defend- ants had done it ; but it was evidently the result of persir.ent loitering there. The property belonged to the late Mr. Speakman, He was positive Phillips was there.—The Chair- man pointed ont that the defendants were acting contrary to the law; but if they promised not to do ems again they would be dischargel on payment of 4s 6d costs. &amp;moot ATTENDANCE OVIES.—The following parents were summoned at the instance of the Runcorn Union School Attendance Committee and fined 6d and 4s 6.1 costs for neglecting the education of their respective children :—Ann Stanley, and John Radicorn, both of Weston. -.Evidence was given by School Attendanee Officer W. B. Brimelow, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>PERISFIED FROM COLD IN SUMMER. This awful fate seems imposaible, but yet is nevettbe leas a fact. How many people cnautract a c7ld in summer, and then neglect it, finally being stricken down %WI congeE— tion of the lungs or bronchitis, to be no doubt followed 13; an attack of pneumonia, that destroyer of rich and poor alike. If they had taken one dose of Lava.ns Lung Linctisv, dit.ectly the symptoms were felt, they would certainly b., hying to—lay. Everybody, especially he.da of families, should remember that they will be a long time dead, try. to live as long as possible, if not for your own sake, tor those that are dependent upon you. If you Noire a throat tonic. or if your chest feels tight, buy a bottla at once from the nearest Chemist or Cc-Dperative Stores, and it will prove the best investment you ever made.— Green and Co., Bonne, will send a bottle, prett free, la 1., 2s 9d., and 4s 63.</p>
        <p>LIC R NEM TRANSFERS.</p>
        <p>The following licenses were transferred at the Borough Police Ceart, on Monday :—Ship Inn, from Agnes Jenks deceased, to Walter Bestow Walker ; White Bull, from Ann Bal4haw to Thomas Edward Brumbell ; off-license, 47, Walker-street. from James Wilson to Wilmot Davies ; off-license, 19, Gaokell- street, from Thomas Atherton, deceased. to Annie Gertrude Atherton ; off-license, Leicester-street, Elizabeth Griffin to Fred John Binks. FATAL ACCIDENT AT MESSRS. CROSFIELD'S WORKS. Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquiry into the circurostences attending the death of William Thomas Percival, 18, Lall-street, who died in the Infirmary on Wednesday from injuries re- ceived whilst following his employment at Messrs Oro-field and Sons' soap works.—Mr. J. 11. Rogers, H.M.'s Inspector of Factories, watched the case on behalf of the Home Office,and Mr. Newall, engineer, represented Messrs. Urosfield and Sons. Mr. D. Fdnshaw was foreoun of the jury. —Henry Harman said deceased was his brother-in-law and was a general labourer for Messrs. Crasfield and SOII9 He saw him in the Infirmary after the accident happened but he made no mention of it.—Robert Brown Marshall said he worked for Messrs. erns- field and Sons as a general labourer. He confirmed the police report of the accident which was read by the Coroner and which stated that at 11 o'clock on Wednesday morning, August 23rd, deceased with a number of other men sc as engazed in hoisting the funnel of a soap roastinz pot and whilst in the act of going to fasten a guide rope, which necessitated him walking across a wall three feet wide and thirty feet high, he fell to the ground. No one witnessed the accident, but deceased was shortly afterwards missed, and on lo3kiog round the work- men found him lying insensible at the bottom of the wall. Witness said that deceased was not doing anything wrong in walking on the wall. He thought it was a safe width. Deceased had light boots on. He (Marshall) was about the first to get to him after he had fallen. He was unconscious, and be was removed to the Infirmary.—Crnss-examined by Inspector Rogers.—He told deceased to cross the well. There was a plank on the top of the pot. The pan wan about a foot from the wall, and to get to it would mean a drop of about four feet. He could not say whether he had fallen from the wall or from the top of the pan.— In answer to the foreman of the jury.witness said he would have had no hesitation in cres4itig the wall himself.—Nnve Dobson said deceased was admit- ted to the Infirmary on the 23rd August, suffering from a compound fracture of the skull, and a frac- turea thigh. He died last Wednesday at 3-30 in the afternoon. He was unconscious the whole of the time he was in the Institution.—The jury returned • a verdict of "Accidental death."</p>
        <p>TO THE DRAF.—A gentleman who cured himself of Deafne,s and Noises In the Head after fourteen Tears fluff log will gladly send full particulars of the remedy, on receipt of stamped addressed envelope.—F. CLIFTON. Amberley House. htrAnd. London, NV C.—Advt.</p>
