      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. CIIMBERLNND v. CHESHIRF.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. CIIMBERLNND v. CHESHIRF.</p>
        <p>The a;,nu .l encounter between fifteens culle from the Northern Union clubs of these counties was decided at Whitehaven on Saturday. If anything the home fgr- wards were the smarter, but the defence on either side was good, and Cumbtirland only won by a try—sc.ired by Lomas—to nil.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>DERBY DAY AT WARRING FON. WIDNES WHACKED.</head>
        <p>A DERBY DAY AT WARRING FON. WIDNES WHACKED.</p>
        <p>riaere i 3 tal match of the season rouses more excite- moot and rivalry at Warrington than that with Widnes, though Oldham and Runcorn run it close. 01 late the Widne3ians have had the better of matters, but the local lads were determined to avenge themselves.this time, and by sheer determination, they did it. The day foittinately torned out fine,and in consequrnee_there</p>
        <p>w-js a huge crciwd Nuinbe ring, between 7,000 and 8,000, of which kure Widnes contributed a tow hundn,ls. Both Varna were well represented, neither side having to bem )an absentees from the selected fifteen. A moder- ate bree,ze blew from the railway goal which had the effect of drying the turf niaely. Mr. McCutcteon (0!d- -ham) officiatid as referee, and the players turnei out as follows :</p>
        <p>_ Warriegton : Ashurst, full back ; Wood, Jones, Isherweod and Harris, three-quarters ; Bate and Duek- worth, half backa ; Taylor, Naylor, Boardman, Fair- hurst, Sac Low. Morrison, Eden, and Scholtze, forwards. Widras : Batey,full back; Brennan,Neeitt,Rispan and Hughes, three quarters; Bown and Murphy, half backs; Hampson, Aspey, Dannelly, Redmond. Handley, Travis, Heghes, and Golding, forwards. Warrington, as usuel, won the toss and elected to play with the; wind and Aspey started the game with a poor kick. Exchanges between the tacks followed, Widnes obtaining an advantage. The visitors forwares were the first to waim to their work, and they gained possrs-ion from the packs. Bown early showed his mettle by neatly handing to Hampson who led a danger- Mai rush which was checked plochily by Jones. Boyer), Rspan and Hughes passed prettily but with no result as the wing man was well tacklee by Harris. Widnes maintaioed the pressure on the home line which had another narrow escape, Duckworth going down for the ball in admirable style. The same player relieved with a spanking run and punt which Batey failed to respond to. Taylor gained further ground with a rush and Werrineten began to liven up. The forwards got into their strile atel Nevin mulling, the ball was rushed over the Widnes line in a twinkling Bate appearing to touch down, but in the scramble a clear view was an impossl- bility and the referee disallowed the claim for a try. The inci lent redoubled the excitement however, and appeared to act as a tonic to the homesters fer thev fairly swarmed round the Chemicals' goal. Duckworth was conspicuous with dodgy play, Harris making a good run. Jones was next to the fore with a lovely punt under difficulties and Batey being again at fault the line was crossed again. Brennan kicking deed in the nick of time. Jones next hal a shot at goal from cen- tre, bat his ziele wait charged down. Off-side by Hughes gave him another shot and be had hard luck with the kick. A centre from Duckworth landed the ball on the line where Aspey mulled and Warrineton failed to score an ems. try. They scrimmaeed cei the line desperately and the ball coming out on the Warrington side, Bate and Duck worth whipped it across to Harris who dashed over at the corner. Frantic cheering greeted Warrington's success. Jones was again unsuccessful at goal. Widnes now trial to get c'ear by punting but a mark from Wood checksd them. Morrison put in a dashing run in midfield, finally ternw- ing ta Wood who made a powerful effort to break through. Warrineton's attack looked like being pro- ductive when Bate threw all away by making an awful dron at goal and registering only a dead ball. Morrison and J mei; between them compelled Batev to concede further gr nond, and Duckworth breaking away Peauti- fully a try seemed certain until the hall was sent for- ward to Harris. Play was toreeedingly keen and Brennan was citation&amp; as the result of a tussle with the home captain. Warrington maintained the attack Wood ani Harris both doing good work on the wines. Aspey had to kick dead and a five yards scrimmage led to great excitement. Warrineton were penalised however and Widnes relieved the pressure. Play went. back to centre where Bown came out with a wonderful sun through the home backs. Near the line he sent to E. Hughes and the latter passing to Donnelly Widnes were placed on level terms jnst before half-'ime. With the wind in their favour Widnes new had the best of matterseend on resumption quickly foend their way into the Warringtnn half. Bown and :Sturphy got away the later trying a drop at goal which was not far out. Widnes sesme i to quite overplay Warrineton for a few minutes and Bown'ran over from a close scrim- mage, only to be broneht back for sonlf? informality. The narrow squeak woke Warrington up again and Jones reel wen to centre. Harris secured and dashing down touch punted past Ratey who was left on his back. J. Hughes and Isherweed went for the ball and the latter netnaeing the Widnasian secured the oval and romped over the line with a grand try. Warrington peonle cheered themselves hosra' .1 meg again failed at goal, tbotigh only by the merest trills. Beth tsams now went at it like &amp;moral, and there were some hard knocks given and taken. Ashurst misfielled and let Widnes a dangerous rush being stopped by Merrison who kicked solondidly from the goal-mouth. Warring- ton were nenalieed hut Taylor char:zed Bator's kick down and Widnes had to falllback. Bate got clear in brilliant stele and then -had to throw the try away for lack of speed and support. It was a lovely overine which he made and the home skipper might have done much better had he gone on. Achurst let Warrington down again and J. Hughes led the way to the home quarter, where some deadly tackling was witnessed. Bate ansrainine a naety•wrsnch of the shoulder. Harris arid Wool eheerral the venue the latter putting in a dash to the Widnes 25. The fortunes of the easne chaneed rapidly, first one side and then the other gaining the upper hand. Morri- son WA9 off the field for some minutes with a dam- aged head as the reset of a collision with Baton'. Bren- nan received after nice passing and ran atronelv. hut Ashurst was found (elite safe as a t ickler. Wirlees strained every nerve to obtain the lead and got near the home goal ; a bed pass by Bown spelling an (Tirade- nity. Bate kicked cut and Batey marked, finally dr sp- ping a lOVeir goal. Only one point now separated the teams and blare was deenerete. Isherwood made useful mark. And kicks which Irspt Widnes out, but jot on time Bitsy had A kick at goal. He placed the hell with great care hes feUed to send it over ties bar, tn the great relief of Warineten. The whistle then sounded for the close of hoatilities leaving Warrington victoriees by 2 tries (6 points) to Widnes one goal °se try (five points) TYLDESLEY v. ROCFIDALE HORNETS.</p>
        <p>Very few irop'o witocssld thia match at TrldFstoy. The Horne'q won a clots* same by one drop goat and on. try to one try (J. Borry). W. Ri drorped the goal, and 11,othroyl sained the try for Ronhdale, BROUGHTON RANGERS v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A one-sideid r!ame a Bronohtnn reignited in favnur of the " Saint: " tic one goal and fnur tr;es to nil. Liver- sage, Rennin. Whiteley, and Bries each scornd tries, Traynor kicking I•hn LEIGH v. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>Salford were the visitors in this match, and returned home the winner; hv three .735115 and two trios to two goa's and mil try. Griffi'lls. iladwen (for Salford), Davis, Aillyneur, and Galtlev (for Leigh), were the scorers.</p>
