        <head>E A MINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER 6 18q9'</head>
        <p>THE E A MINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER 6 18q9'</p>
        <p>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>HALTFAX v. LIVERSEDGE.</p>
        <p>Slinrtly after the ,itart Qf th's match at i3alifax, Ric= gcored a try f;r the hnt.n2 teitn. Subsequently tries were registerJd by Young (two) and Rigg,Stoylo placing one. Halifax thus woo by on, groat and four trics to nil.</p>
        <p>LEEDS v.. CAS I LEFORD.</p>
        <p>A brilliant win awaltel tho vidlors at br.e.ls.the score at the close hPin:!, in their favour by fotir oil and three tries to nit Silmony, Hentmstali, and Tosvuend com- piled the score. BRADFORD v, BRIGHOUSE.</p>
        <p>Bridf )rd %I'm toe vertu:: of this awn,th visi!:ors i•-tu.t 'Hug reverse by two goak and three tries to 3ooper, CAiverz.., And Doosou obtained the tries, the first- named 'iuckisi:: 111, NAIR.</p>
        <p>BITLEY v. LIOLBECK</p>
        <p>lie,vy skloring eiltincterised this mitch at Batley, the home si:le whining by twa goals and four tries to one goal (Hein4ey). Dagins (two), Curley, and GArner crossed the line for Batley. BRAMLEY v. LEP:O'3 PARISIi CHURCH.</p>
        <p>A level f.tam- was witnesied at Brarv, ay, home side winning by two triqa to one try. Harnbrecht s3ored the winners trios, I.o;wii doing a like service for the Church team, HUNSLET v GULL KINGSTON ROVERS.</p>
        <p>The home Ki.ie had the best of this match at Hunslet, anti won by two goals and one try to nil. The scoring was performed by Robinson, who gaine i the try. and W. Goldthorpe, who dropped one goal and placed the other.</p>
        <p>HULL v. AIItNNINGHANI.</p>
        <p>A large crowd viewed this match, at Hull, where the local team won by two grills and three tries to one-goal and one try. Gorman, Jacques, Driscoll, Proctor, and Rho2.ei sc•-,r,rl Ulr their respective sidss. WAKEFIELD THINIIT V. HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>A. ha, diome victory awaited Waktfiz•ld, on their own ground, the resuit bling three goals nd threo tries to nit in their la,voilr. Price. Flow-11. and Lockwood gained the tries. Whi:aker and iVeteatf kicking the goals.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE "A" TEAMS COMPETITION. WIDNES "A" v. WARRINGTON "A"</p>
        <p>Th3s match was played at Widnes, on Saturday before a fair at endence. The visitors won the toss,and played with the wind. Immediately on starting Warrington were penalised underneath their own posts, and Jones trio,ti to place a goal but failed. Merry weather tackled O'Brien in a manner whieh did not please the referee, and was cautioned. On several occasions the home team were in troub:e, but Percival was ever on the watch ,and effecte I several good saves. Widnes were penalized, and Mason made an attempt to place a goal, but the la,ll went wide. Ratcliffe got possession on the hal-way line, and after a magnificent run got ov-r near the peet3. llellem had no difficulty in landing a goal. Inspired by this success, Warrington were soon again on the attack, and Alien crossed the meek rut. Mason made a good effort to hick a goal, the ball hitting oce of the uprights and rebounding into the field of play. The vieiters con tinued to have the best of the game, and at half-time the score was—Warrington, oise goal ts 3 o tries (eight points) ; Widnes, nil. On restarting Widnes at once became dangerous, and some good defensive work was done by Ratcliffe. Widnes played with a lot more dash than they had dine in the first half, and on two occasions Peacock was within an ace of scoring, but pasiing te Lomax, that player failed to catch the b and both efforts were lost. The ball was carried over the visitors'lines, and from the ensuing five yards scrim- mate, LOMAX scored at the cornr.r. Percival failed to place a goal. There was no more scoring, and Warrineton won by one vat and two tries (eight points), to Widnes one try (three points).</p>
        <p>JUNIOR LEkGITIE.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V. WIDNES RANGERS.—&amp;t Warrington. Widnes pressed. but Corless relieved with a mark. Then St. Mary's were penalised, Brennan fail- ine at goal. Hughes geined a try for Widnes. Half- time score :—Widnes, tone try ; St. Mary's, nothing* Nei@ scored two tlies immediately on restarting. neither being converted. R,sult :—Widnes Rangers, 23 points: Warringten S. Mary's, nil. FLETCHER RUSSELL'S ENGINEERS Y. LATCHFORD RANGERS.—PIayed on the ground of the latter on Satur- day. The home team kicked off, and play went into the Latchford half, where smart work was exhibited by Sutton and Foden. From a scrimmage, Sutton got pos- sesgion, and transferred to Warburton,who made a dam)] for the line, but was pulled up. Shaw cleared, and T. Knight was called upon, but was not caught napping. Nelson took play to mid-field, where he was floored, and a series of scrimmages ensued,in which Fletcbers' gained considerable ground, Latchford eventually saving. Frem the drop-out, Letcbford went away with a rush, but were pulled up by Foster. Foden cleared and Pieston took a drop: at goal, which want outside. Fletchers' were now having miters much their own way, and the passing of the back 3 was splendid. T. Knight rln to the line where he was tackled, and from the scrimmage which followed Foden made an unsuccessful attempt to drop a goal. Lawton gathered and took play to midfield. Sutton obtained, but Wee tackled before he could get away. Foden rased to Foster. who transferred play close to the- home team's line. Fletcher's kept up a vigorous pres sure, and Latchford defended well. At half time there was no score. Resta.rtiug, F letcher's again went away with a dash, and alnust scored in the first minute, but the full back effected a fine clearance. For a brief period play was taken to centre, where T Knight got pees-ssion, and after a passing movement Spencer was grassed at the quarter flag. From the scrimmage which ensue), Knieht scored the first try. The goal kick was unsuecessful. The kick off saw the visitors again on the attack and in a very few minutes Wilson was enabled to ade the second try, the goal kick this time being success- ful. The restart saw Latchford make a desperate effort to cross the line, but were checked by Knight, and once again th, ball travelled to the other end where Holbrook scored again under the posts, a goal resulting. Final: Fletcher Russells Engineers, two pals, three tries (13 points) ; Litchford Rangers, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION MEE lING. CLUBS FINED.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION MEE lING. CLUBS FINED.</p>
        <p>_ An important meeting ot the Northern Union was held at llvid.rsfield on Tuesday night. An interesting. point was r tie ,c 1 by Millom with respect to Moore and Young, who were registered with Halifax in 1896 when Milk= were et II a Rugby Union organis stion, but had never played with the Yorkshire club. These me°, had there- fore, always been memb,ra of the Millorn club, but as they could not be registered with two senior conc. DP.tizions Millom now asked that they should be permitted to play with thefts. The committee decided against the application, ruling that the two men had bean properly registered with Halifax. C Strickland and J o,Ats were suspended until the end of tho seasoo for having signed tvo professional forms.the former with Minoru and Barrow, and the latter with Kingston Rovers and Featherstone. For playing with Normanton witeout the c masent of Batley,with whom he sws r4gistered, E Plinomer was suspended until Novem- ber 30. For playing with Altinclaam after having signed Fr Bradford, Findlow was suspended until the end of October. Tueßarrow club were fined £lO for playing Joshua Platt,who had not been in bona-fide employment, but Platt was reinstated from date.Shipley's appeal to be adniitted to the Yorkshire Nn. 2 competition was upheld. The decision of the Competition Sub-Committue was, therefore, overrul.d. The application of C. C. Lemnriere for reinstatement as an amateur under Northern Union rules was granted. H. Woodhead, of Elland, and F. Harry, Torcerav A'hletic, who had been suspended by the English Rugby U.sion, were given permission m play with Northern Union clubs. For neglecting to register nr.-fifessional players, Broughton Rangers and Rochdale Hornots were each find £lO. The Whitworth and Altrinctsam clubs were also fined £1 for breaches of Vase professional laws. THE COUNTY TRIAL MATCH.</p>
        <p>_ The fortni.zlitly meeting of the Lancashire County Uai)n wag held in Mane.hester en Monday night, Mr J H Smith (Wimsa) prosiding. The fhihwinz clubs were eleo•ed to membership :—St. Helens White Rose and Levan Valley. The temps to play in the trial match at salford, on Wednesday, o.3tober 11, were selected as folio w 5: - Pt b +Meg R J Hate' (Wiihr's), back • R Thomas and T William; (Salford), D Traynor Helena), and S winia,ma (OlTham), thrse-quarters ; J Molyneux and J H Dunblvin (Leigh), hall backs ; J Williams (Salford),G WhiLhea (Broughton Rang Its). P Taylor (Leigh), W Briers (St. Helens), J Harrison (Rochdale Hor- nets), G Fr tter (0 dham), F Tunney (Sal- Err I), and G Aapey (Widnes), forwards. Poe- sihles R L 'Thomas (o'dliam), balk ; T D Davies and T Fletcher (011hain), T Jackson (Broui,•hton Ratwers).and J T Liversage (t. Helens), three quarters; J R Lawton (01.iharri).a.nd 11 Kruger (Rochiale Hornets) hall-')ockg F fUmPlorl (Widn'4). J Blacklysrri (Wigan) D Morrison (Warrington). T Cleminson (Broughton Rangers). J Teller (Oldham), E Vigors (Swinton), I Roberts (Lich), and G Jackson (Lancaster) forwards Reserves : D Smith (Salford), back ; A Field (Rochdale Hornets). A Pearson and H Hadwen (Salford), three- quarter; B Griffit'is (Salford). and D James (Broughton Rangersl,hal'-hack-; R Berry (Tyldesley),J W nardicker (Roondale Hornets). J Lamb (Broughton Rangers), and .5 Thompson (S:. Fl-lens), forwards. Refert-T : J H Smith (Widnes) ; totiola judiivas. T El Houghton (St. Helena), and J Underwood (Wigan). The Probables will play in white and the Possibles in colouvd jerseys</p>
        <p>BRASS AND IRON BEDSTEADS, Belding, Wr:ng- ing Machines Lamps. awl Coal Vases. Shefll9t4 Cutlery and Pate—J. S. GARLAND. Ironmonger, Bridge-street, Warrington—Advt. JUST RECRIVED 1 a very choice assortment of the new THLOM-QUA Tre RR C 0 AT ; Imperial Waterproof (Cloaks and Coats). Mackintoshes. Capes. Jackets. &amp;c., in the Me t shapes and tints.—W. T. WILLIAMS. 74. Sank ey-street , Warrington.</p>
