      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS v. ELLESMERE PORT.</p>
        <p>Ellesmere Port were the visitors at Nursery Park on Saturday. Owing to the Cheshire Club arriving late a start was not made until 4-15. There was a strong wind blowing fr:nn goal to goal, and of this the Newt.-,ners had the advantage in the first half. The visitors won the toss, and Ellis set the ball in motion on behalf of the homesters before a fair muster of specta- tors. The opening stages went all in favour of Newton, who were soon swarming round the Port goal, but Harri- son rais,d the siege ny kicking the ball behind; A sudden break away by the visitors right trainferred play, and a splendid shot from the foot of Griffiths was saved in masterly style by Horrocks. The homesters still kept up the attack and at Vngth, Butler getting posse3siou in a grand position he bar4ed the leather into the ;set, this point coming as the result of 20aminutes play. No more .acering was doe before the interval which arrived with the score Newton 1, Elesmere Port 0. On resuming the hornesters on assumed the aggressive, and Thompson with no one to beat but the custodian shot yards wide. Still the Newtoners kept up the pressure and from a pass by Butler. Thompson increased their lead with a fast low ahot which gave the goal-keeper no chance. The viAtors made strenuous efforts to reduce the Newtoners lead and in tnis they were at length successful as the ball was put past Horrrcks after an Exciting scrimmage in the goal mouth. All their efforts to equalise were futile however, and time was calle 1 with Newtoa winners of a hard fought gun° by two goals to one.</p>
        <p>ENGLISH CUP.</p>
        <p>QUALIFYING ROUND. WARRINGTON v. MIDDLEWICH.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon the first qualifying round in this cempetition was played off at Middlewich between Middlewich and Warrington, in the presence of very few spectators. The visitors set the ball in play, arid it was taken directly into the vicinity of their goal, their custodian Aspinall beim; called upon to save. The b ill' then travelled towards the home citadel, but the home backs proved too vigilant, and after a time their for- werds broke away, and commented pressing their opponents, the custodian haying again to save, and Conner, the homesters left back, sent an excellent shot, which proved just a Ii tie too high to effect anything. After another well-directed shat being rendered tutile by the visitors custodian the Warringtonians broke away. The ball got in dangeroue proximity to the home goal-posts, but Jones failed to take advantage of a good opportunity of scoring. Connor, Vacs visitors' outside left forward, dieplayed gcod form. The judici- ous combination of the whole of the forwards was a noticeable feature. Cooper proved one too many for them, however, on several occasions, his strong kicking powers being put forth te their best. Hulse, the botnesters' centre half back, called upon Aspinall to save, and subsequently the ball travelled to and fro for some minutes. Hough nici ly passed the ball to Bei ey, who neatly pw.s.d it on in turn to Thompson, who in a very effec- tive manner headed it through the visitors' goal posts, the first goal being scored after about 20 minutes excel- lent play. Middlewich pressed strongly, but their op- penents' forwards at last broke away, and a foul tieing allowed against the home team the Warringtonians had an excellent chance of equalising, but the bill went through the goal posts before stetting in play. Bailey exhibited some sharp play on the • homesters' side, and once or twice jeopardised the visitors stronghold. and on one occasion imme- diately before half-time however, Riley cleverly eluded the homesters backs and equalised. Midele wich then pressed until half-time, but nothing eventful hap- pened. Oa resuming Evans made an excellent shot and forced a c )rner, and Gailimore narrowly missed scoring. B sins then ran the ball to the home citadel, and Riley proved too much for its custodian by scaring the second coal for Warrington. The visitors then pressed and the Middlewich backs were kept engaged on the defensive, and Bains finally managed to • add another goal to the score. The homesters played spiritedly for a time, but their opponents' backs proved al- most invulnerable. Hodgkinson showed good form in several rufeaes which were made on the Warring on stronghold,and it was at this point of the game that As- pinall's high stature proved a decided benefit to his team. After getting away the homesters forced a corner, but failed to de anything, and this was subsequently repeated. The Warringtonians then put forth au effort, and Cooper made an unfortunate miss which prove i disastrous, the visiters increasing their score to four goals. It then appeared pretty evident that the strong wind which the homesters had to contend with was an important factor in the game, and directly after scoring the 4th goal the visitors added another. The hamesters, notwithstanding hard lines, continue I to show spirit, and made several plucky bat unavailing attempts to reduee the majority of their oppanents, but towards the latter part of the game their play was necessarily mainly on the defensive, time being announded without any alteraticn ia the score, the game standing—Warrington five goals to one. The teams were as follows :—Warrington : Forwards, E k Connor. J Merray. U (Seine, Colour-Sergeant Jonas, and L Riley ; half-backs. McGrey, Hackett, and Graham ; backs, Dowie and Glover ; goal, G Aspinall. Middle- wich Forwards, G flougn, J Bailey, D Moss, S Thofnp- sen, and E Evans ; half-backs, G Egerton, W Hulse and F Gellimore ; backs, J Elodgkinson and D Cooper; goal. J Wilkinson. Linesmen, D Jones, Warrington ; and T Hough, Middlewich ; Mr R T Johns (Henley) proved a most impartial and popular referee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>ALLEGED EMBEZZLEMENT BY A COMMERCIAL TRAVELLER,</head>
        <p>THE ALLEGED EMBEZZLEMENT BY A COMMERCIAL TRAVELLER,</p>
        <p>PRISONER COMNIITTED FOR TRIAL.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Saturday be- fore Mr. Birchall (in the chair), and Mr. It. T. Fair- dough, Frederick Thomas Poole, 7, St. Nicholas- street, Ipswich, was charged on remand with hav- ing on the 10th November, 1898, feloniously and fraudulently embezzled the sum of £5 83 61 received by him on behalf of his employers, Messrs. J. Cros- field and Sons.—Mr. A. Browne prosecuted and prisoner was dePended by Mr. T. S. Steel (Messrs. Jenkins and Co.)—Mr. Browne said it would be necessary for tti6'dfici to go to the Quarter Sessions for trial, because the Bench had no power to deal with it unless the prisoner pleaded guilty. The prisoner was a traveller for Messrs. Crosfield and Sons in the Ipswioh and Norwich district. It was hisduty to remit the moneys received by him, if paid by check to forward the cheque and if by cash to bank it and transmit it to their bankers, the Manchester and Liverpool District Banking Com- pany, Limited. On the 10th November, 1898, he sent an account, which he should produce, showing that he had paid in £ll 83 61, but he did not say where. It was received by Messes. CrOifielri, and was put to his credit. In December it was found that the money had never been paid in. They had assumed that the money would be paid into some bank at Ips- wich, Norwich or I arcenuth, and would be remitt3d to the District Bank at Warrington. He was credited with the £ll 83 6d., but when inquiries were msde it was found that it hed not been paid. The gentleman who had 'ha matter in kand was taken ill and had to go away. He was away a considerable time, but in the first week in Feoruary when he inquired ab)ut the matter it was discovered that the man had " bolted " and a warrant was ifsued against him for embezzle- ment. Mr. Alfrel Dane, accountant at the Manchester and Liverpool District Bank at Warrington, said he had gone through Messrs Croefield'e account for November and December but there was no payment for £ll 8s 6,4 or £5 Bi 6i. Other accouate had lees paid in by a mui named Poole.—By Mr. Steel : Messri. Crosfield had more than one account, but they were identified. He was alluding to the current account. He baieved the bank had agents at Norwich. Robert Bruce Murray, chief accountant for Messrs. Crosfleld and Sons, produced the agreement between prisoner and the firm dated August 30th, 1897. The total amount unaccounted for was £36 78. 3d;—Cro3s- examined by Mr. Stnel : The alleged defalcations were first discovered between Christmas and New Year's Day, but the warrant was not issued till February 7tn, 1899. The firm was insured against any loss in a Guarantee Society. Part of the money had been paid by the Society. The prosecution was really compulsory. —Mr. Browne : The pros%cution will ccst us £3O or £4o.—Mr. Steel : The case was laid before the Society, and they have instructed you to prosecute.—Witness : They have not specifically said prosecute Poole.—Mr. Browne: lam not objecting to this, but Mr. Stewart of Liverpool, will not allow anything about guarantee societies to be taken down in embezz'ement Cases. Witness added the firm had re- ceived the following letter from Mr. Wiseman i• January 14th,—Mr. Poole has left Ipswich and the depot in a dilapidated state, no address. Claim on the Guerantea Society, if he does not communicate with you snon." James Wiliam Daniels, grocer,of Cricket Ground-road, War winh, paid he knew the prisoner. The receipt was written out by the priseeer' and the sum was paid in cash after the discount was deducted. He had paid the prisoner other moneys, and he had known him the greater part of the time he had been in Norwich. Superintendent Monaghan stated that he received the warrant for the apprehension of the prisoner on the 7th February last:. He had been in communication since with the police at London, Ilevrich aloe Norwich, and received prisoner into custody on the 19th September. When the warrant WA3 read over, prismer said " If I had it I have paid it in.'! Prisoner reserved his defence, and was committed to the Quirter Sessions on °eel) 'r 31st for trial, bail being allowed, in two sureties of £25, and himself in £5O.</p>
        <p>When StSking for Olcoa, Insist on haying C&amp;onunr.s.---sold only In Packets and This as other Cocoas are often subset- tuted fa the sake of extra profit.—Advt (1)</p>
        <p>and</p>
        <p>one of her lungs</p>
        <p>was affected."</p>
        <p>IRENE F0M77:37.</p>
