        <p>It is about the same after influenza as it is after any other exhaustive disease, and in fact, after disease is much like before disease. To explain more clearly, either when recovering from disease or when contracting it, the system is weak, without its natural resistive power, and it h because ths system is in this exhauFtN.l condition that disease is easily taken or convalesence is slow. The com- plications following influerizi. are the results of exposure when the body cm n offer no resistance. Will you kindly read the following letter about a little girl that had a terrible cough after in- fluenza : " 61, Brockman Road, Folkestone " June 27th, 1898.</p>
        <p>"Dear Sirs.—From the speedy relief which I have seen Scott's Emuls:on give to my little niece, aged eight and a half years, who lives with me and is entirely under my care, I cmn indeed speak for the preparation. Afcer having a very sharp attack of influenza, she was loft with a terrib,e cough, which was most obstinate about leaving her, and as one of her lungs was affected through. it, I was rather anxious about her, but am thankful to say that after taking Scott's Emulsion for a very short time tier ceuglo has complAnly left her, and her cheeks— naturally thin, but very much more so lately—are so fat that different friends have asked quite ser-</p>
        <p>iously if her face was not swollen. lam e-xtremely ''Pgrateful for all that Pcott's Emulsion has done for</p>
