      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL. [BY "OyamsY."] Has ta heard met P—N-wm What is it P—VV/K. Warriton whacked Wideui. It tuk a bit o' doin' but they done it, and rest well too.—Yo' s'uri3e me. Ah shouldn't a thowt it, but awm m tin c.,,lad al'. Come and hey a drink on t' strenith !—(And they did, several times).</p>
        <p>Tbe conversation between these two gentlemen.whom I must leave to the delights of "half and half," was short but it conveyed a lot. They also cin vi;sed a lot (of liquor) later on—but that is a matter for the police and not ourselves. All we are concerned wi,h is the fact that the match between Warrington and Widne4 on the Wilderspool ground resulted in favour of Warring- ton. Such a result had been hoped for, perhaps prayed for, by the enthusiasts who shout for the "wirepullers." The "Chemicals" have been altogether too strong of late years and generally asphyxiate Warrington, but on Saturday the intended victims tried a new preventive against the "chemicals" which worked the oracle. or miracle to be more correct. It is said that some rather powerful "chemicals," taken neat, were required to re- vive the vanquished ones, and the rumour smacks of truth and possibility.</p>
        <p>Saturday might have been improved on as far as weather is concerned, but it was really not at all bad. Rain kept off very consPerately and the fast-drying qualities of the Wilderspool turf were son en evidence, so that by the time for the k;clc-,ff everything was favourable for a fine game. the Widnesians came in large numbers,rnany wearing the. Widnes colours, and all wore an expansive, happy-an-futuro sort of smile. A good deal of this wore off tater on however. riaving trounced the league champions the week before they felt quite confid,mt of making Warrington bite the dust, even if there was none. There wasn't either, and the dust-biters were also a little Ibit " oph " that sort of thing. They had tired of it awl experimented with a new " chemical" compound which, 'tis said, proved very palatable. When 3.30 arrived there would be about 7,000 spectators round the enclosure, which had ben banked at the pavilion end. The club officials looked extaticilly happy for the receipts had again topped the £lOO.</p>
        <p>Referee M'Cutcheon, who is very popular at War- rington, and everywhere acktuswledged to be one of the most capable whistle-blowers in the Uoion, showed the teams that he bad them well in hand from the commencement. It was well be did so for the rivalry between the representatives ef Widnes and Warrington is so keen that the men are apt to confuse the football field with the battle-field—a mistake which is calculated to cause trouble. Tin qe were only one or two slight initances of this forg,tfuinesi and they were promptly dealt with. Altogeth.r, the game was con- ducted in good spirit and good football was plentiful. The first half saur Warrington in pas,eqsion of the wind and mindful of the experiences at St. Helens and Ronh: dale, they set to with a will. Widnes hid them for a while but were s .rely driven back. The home tea</p>
        <p>played lika furies and Harris burst through he defence' after a quarter of an hour's play. Jones did his best to get* goal, but fate was against him and it look ,d odds on Warrington having to be content with a 3 point lead at half time. Just blfor3 the interval however there was an unexpected reverse. Bown. the tricky Widnes half, dodged his way through the homr, backs and gave Donnelly an easy journey to the line. Widnei were thus placed on a even footing, for Rato7 missed the goal. It was hard luck an Warrington, for they had ben unfor- tunate in not obtaining a more substantial lead.</p>
        <p>Nothing daunted, though the odd; vnre in favour of Widnes after changing ends, Warrington kept up the attack. Widnes responded with renal energy and Bown had hard luck with a try which was disallowed. The play went rapidly from one end of the field to the other, and while at the Warrington end Widnes sustained a second reverse. Harris ran and puntr•cl past Batey, Isher- wood and Jim Hughes went for the hall, and the former by a clever piece of robbery secured the hall and scored a fine try. The tension which had beew experienced was relieved by a huge cheer. Warrington were again a try ahead, and superhuman exertions by both sides f l. lowed. Not since the cup tie with H %lifax have I seen Warrington FO desperately in earnest. They had a big task to) for Widnes did not reish the defeat loom'ng be- fore them. Batev managed to bring them within a point of drawing level, with a dropped gal, and juit on time he had a place kick. Everythin,g depended on it and there was an oppressive, thundery, silence while the hall was planed. Then it touched, Batey ran and kicked, the Warrington forwaris carzL nl the ball</p>
        <p>my niece. I was very troubli,d to knowlwhat give her, as I was ir,reed to drip the ordinary cod- liver oil. It po-i-ively ;th rrP retch even ti measure it out for her. In one way I also have found personal relief from Scott's Emulsion, as I now have restful nights, whereas I was feelinz quite worn out with the dreadfully disturbed onei before on scounr, of her incessant coughing, and I shall take every opportunity of spolkinz for Scott's Emulsion.—Yours very truely, " (Signtd) Frances A. Forrest' " Scott's Emulsion does much more than nine's, cure cough or cold. It is very effectual in relieving local inflammation, but its best work is in no ir- ishing the system and restorieg vigour and strength to the vital organs. Scott's Emulsion makes con- valescence rapid and it av,v,s iliseise. For children who do not Olive there is no remedy so effectual. To overcome or prevent all the wasting tendencies • of the system, Scott's Emulsion is without an equal. It is the most palatable and mast effectual form of cod-liver oil, combined with hypopbosphites and glycerine. Any doubt about the advantages of Scott's Emulsion cvrr all other forms of cad-liver oil will be dispelled by trying it. You can obt tin a sample of Seated Emulsion by sending threepence to cover postage to Scatt awl Bowne, Limited, Manufacturing. Chemists, 95, Great Saffron Hill, Lornion,E. C., and mentioning this paper. All ehornista sell Sett's Emulsion.</p>
        <p>fell chart of the goal by a trifle. What a sigh of relief there was from the hrnni c:owd. The whiitle sounded " ceAse " and all 91,4 e ovor. Widnes were defeated and Warrington avenged.</p>
        <p>The gam, itself was of the sort the spectator likes, for thins wai not uninteresting moment in it. The teams were Ho ev.ealy matched that it wa.r impossible to tell which would b. the next to score. On the whole, however. the Ilmne lot were the beat, especially in the hack division. Bare and Ducv,worth were a better pair than Murphy and Bow°, 'huh the latter played an excellent game. Dock.worth has fairly won his place. and his display in the last two matches has been really first-class. SlrllP, of the op,nirgi be rnVe on Saturday were worthy of Bate in his palrnirst days—and that 10 high praise. Warring-on were much superior at clatra where Jonea and I,hervvo I comp!el:cly overplayed Nevitt and Risran. Same ,Af the former's tackling and breaking up of combination reminded me very much of Barber. What a grand wing the two togetber would make ! Harris was the best of the four wing men and played the sort of game one expects from him—dashing and determined. There was no choice brtwAen the two sets of forwards, the who'e sixtoon worksd like slaveS, and at full back too there WAq not much alvantage on either side. Batey wag the he.t, but he was very muCta off form. Ashurst dr serves further trial. He can pis,' a far better game than he dij on Saturday, when al,i ordeal he had to face would have tried the most osperl- enced of full bachs.</p>
        <p>There was nothinz very notable in the other Lan' caehire Senior Comp tition matches 1-7esond the defe3t of Leigh at Leigh hy Salford til,o fudv atoned for the crushing defeat they sbstai,ed at Mather-lane lag season. In wai a " take-d-,wn " for the Leighitee wb° have long' fancied thrills-Ives invincible at home. St• Helens alms, covered themselves with glory by complete ly r,tning last year's champions .t Wheater's Fields Lower Brourtitoa. The Cheshil e Club were combined against Comberland, who jor.tified the good opinion formed of thorn last year by nefeatinz the Cestriane bY a try to nil at Whitehavon.</p>
        <p>The Lineashire Lii tab's up to data i 3 as below. Warrinfftnn's pasitinn ha ihtiv improved, but five plints out of fivo mst7hP3 is no advance on 13st year's form, and is by no means satisfaczory.</p>
