      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>LATCHFORD BAPTISTS. A. SU.JOESsFUL BAZ</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD BAPTISTS. A. SU.JOESsFUL BAZ</p>
        <p>With tee objet of raising a sera of bitvreen £2OO awl £3OO tor dei:raying in. cos, of rep.irs the cnurch and enabling the. officers to carry out. certain suggested improvements. the friends con- nected with the Baptist Chapel at Latchford con- ducted a bazaar in the schoolroom last Wednesday.. Thursday, and again on Friday, with successful re- sults. The limited capacity of the schoolroom was taxed to the utmost but nevertheless it was made to suit the exigencies of the occasion. The sL-alls had been tastefully arranged round the room awi they were festooned with decoriptive material of prioarose and green. Th 9 refreshment stall was special 4 attractive, evergreens and plants having been introduced to heighten the effect, here were eight stalls altogether and they were in charge of ladies of the church and congregation. Stall one took the form of an advertisetnent stall and was in charge of young men. Numerous fancy articles were offered for sale at stall No. 2, whilst its near neighbour No. 3 contained dairy produce. Refresh- ments were dispensed at the fourth stall, whilst the fifth and sixth were devoted to plain and fancy goods respectively. Children's gifts were to be bought at the seventh stall, and the last was the flower stall. In 11.,..dition, a s'aooting gallery was provided, whilst in the chapel an autograph quilt bearing 520 signatures was on view. This quilt was the work of Mrs. Samuel Albinson, and the general arrangement and eft.ct was much admired. Another quilt containing 6,000 pieces was displayed and this was the work of Mrs. William Wilkinson,l of Antrobus.</p>
        <p>There was an encouraging attendance at the opening ceremony on Wednesday alternoon. Amongst those present were Mr. R. A. N tylor and Miss Naylor,the Rev. J F Matthews and airs Matthews, Rev Joon Yoege, Rev E T and Mrs Reed Mr and Mrs Joseph Albinsoneelr and Mrs James Strange, Mr and Mrs Jepson (Runcorn), Mr W 1' anel Mrs Wilkinson, Mr Isaac , Wilkiesen, Mr and Mrs Adams, Mr Davidson, Mr E Lloyd Jones, Mrs Rathboree, and Mrs flultne (Eerlestown). After a hymn had been sung and prayer offered by the Rev E T Recur, Mr Naylor was called upon te preside at the opening proceedings. He asked the pastor of the church to make a statement respecting the bazaar. The Rev. J. F. MATTHEWS prefaced his remarks by reading a lit of the gifts and promisee he had receiveo. Toe autograph quilt, he pointed out, which had been in the hands of Mrs Samuel Albinson had realisel £l5 lls 6J. (Applause.) Toe donations iecludtd £lO !rom I their treasurer, Mr Aibiusou ; £lO tram Me Davidson ; £5 from Sir J T Brunner ; £5 from Mr Monks; £5 from I Miss Naylor, and numerous etnaller amounts bringing up the total to about £6O. (Applause.) One of the first objects of the barter was to provide funds for cer- tain repairs in connection with the church building. Their first thought was to put their hands into their pockets and pay for them, but it was afterwards decided to hold a bazaar and make in addition certain much- needed improvement's. Tne ceiling over the choir gal- lery needed attention and they had been ob:iged to buy a new cylinder in connection with the heating apparatus. Then in winter they were troubled with abominable draughts, and in order to remedy this, it might be necessary to provide an entirely new vestry, and tbis would entail considerable expense. They were also hoping that after all expenses had been paid they would have a substantial balance left, which they hoped to put to good use in connection with their church work. He had been very much gratified with the kindness as well as the hearty co-operation shown during the time they had been endeavouring to get the things together for that baztar. He thought he could say with great truth that for what. they had received, and what they were about to receive, they were trc ty thankful. (Applause). Mr. It A. NAYLOR who was cordially received said he did not know why the Baptists, had arranged the bazaar that week for he noticed from the newspapres that the Baptist Union was meetiag in 14:,eds and the Lord Mayor of Leeds bad extended a very hearty welcome to something like fifteen hundred delegates and although he was not a Nonconformist himself and he did not know very much about the Baptist Denomination he made the encouraging statement that he believed the Baptist community was doing much for the advance- ment of the moral andospiritual progress of the people. (Hear, hear.) *The Lord Mayor of Leeds, he helieved, was a member of the Church,of England. If they wanted to get a little.bit of broad mindedness they must go outside the Baptist denomination. (Laughter.) Their pastor at Latchford had already informed them that many outside their own communion had been pleased to devote their energies, time, and means in supplying some of the lovely articles they sew arranged on the stalls. The Baptist denomination had done a great work, and he was rather I surprised to see that in connection with it there was a membership of 355,000, which showed something of the importance of the denomination in the United Kingdom. He had often been very pleased to read biographies of prominent Baptists, but if there was one he admired more than any fetter, it was Dr. Carey. (Hear, hear.) Towards the close of last cen- tury, many of them who were acquainted with the his- tory of the denomination, would know that he went out to India. He began life under conditions not altogether promising ante yet he studied hard and succeeded in becoming a profound scholar. His learning was so great that when he went to India he translated the Scriptures into several of the languages of that country. He was a man who did a great work in his day and generation, and if there was one man of wham Baptists ought to be really proud, and whose memory they ought to hold in reverence it was Dr. Carey. He was unlike many . christian ministers ; he was elo:hed with humility—(laughter)—and it would be perhaps better if some of the parsons of the present day would occasionally put on humility even if they could not wear it constantly. Although Dr Carey was a man so proficient a-s a scholar his humility did not seem to leave him. Those who had rad of his career would bas convinced that he was a matt blessed to a very great extent with the spirit of the World's Redeerner,and that was humility. He was not going to trespass further upon their consideration, but he trusted that their expectations would be fully realised. (Applause.) In felicitous terms be asked his sister to declare the bazaar open.</p>
        <p>_ Mies NAYLOR !NM she had very great pleasure in being there for many reasons. First of all she knew so many connected with the church and another reson was that she held a former pastor, the Rev. Joseph Wilkinson, in very great respect. She thought they had a worthy successor to Mr. Wilkinson in the Rev. J. F. Matthews, but in olden times—if she could use that expression— they thought very much of Mr. Wilkinson and she was always pleased te help him in his work. She was glad to be able to help them a little in the work they were now engaged in. She thought they were doing a very great deal of good,and those who were trying to do good de,erved all the help that could be given to there. (Elear,'hear.) She thought they might enc)urage others to do a little more. She had very great pleksure in declaring the bazaar open and hoped it would be very successful.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. T. WILKINSON said he had very great pleasure in proposing a vuts ot thank.; to Miss Naylor for open- ing the bazaar and to Mr. Naylor for presiding over the proceedings. It was not ti.ie first time that they had had a Mr. Naybr in tilt chair, for in the adjoi ping chapel tkey had had the pleasure on several Occasions of having his honoured father presiding over their tea meetings in by gone days. Ht, was sure they esteemed him and they neid him in thatiself-sarne respect in which M:33 Naylor regarded his. (Mr. Wiilrmson's) father.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Jogs YONGS son anded the vote of thanks. He said be was rather at a loss to understand why that pleasant duty should have 13,.en placed in 143 Manus that afternoon. He h-ad somewhat of an idea that it should b.long to Mr. Alhinson or some ether worthy member 01 their own community, hut be supposed, not for the first time, they thought that as an old frietid of theirs he might be expected ti do anything they wanted. (Lau4titer.; It gave him a very great deal of gratifi- cation arid pleasure to be theme that afternoon and to see, notwithstanding the inclemeat condition+ outside, se many friemis gathered together to help them in the work. It was always phasing to mtet Mr.. and Mies Naykr. They were always kindly and genial and will- ing to do anything they' could to make people happy. (Hear, hear.) He had been very much struck with that la,tely,and they seemed to be happiest when ttry were making other people hippy. (Hear, hear.) They laid themselves out in a beautitul way to make those whom they could inflarenc.. and help,be'rer and happier. (Final-, hear.) He should just like. to call his friend Mr. Naylor to account by the way for the idea be tweined to have that ministers wets not aler,ays clothed with humility. (Laughter.) Be was dii- posed to say that if they were not always clothed with humility they would ric.t be able to put it k'on sometimes. They could not put on humility as he suggetted. occisionaliv. It must b 3 an Ina; welling feature and a continually found expression. Nei who put on liem,lity u,u ally wore monk humility which was very distasteful. He hoped on la fl-ction that Mr Naylor would nut come to the conclusion that all ministers were wanting in that very superlative Christian grace It those who were there were wanting it ho hoped they would go home and take their friend's words to heart and seek to wear it more conetantly in the future. (Laughter.) He was sure that a udie. cc would w ishto render thanks to Mr and Miss Naylor for what they had so cheerfully and pleasantly cline. (Applause.) The vote el thanks was carried with acclamation and Miss Naylor baying responded the sale was proceeded with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>THURSDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>The Echoolraom wan agnin well filled n Thursday afternoon when the tAziar was re-vtned Amons; the visitinz friends 0re,...r.t were : Councillor and Mrs. William Fierpoint, Mrs, F. Monks, Miss Monks Ms. Armitage, Mrs. H. Roof rte, Mrs. Walker Mrs Longland, and Mrs. Venn.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. F. biArTukws arnouneed the result of the</p>
        <p>previous day's takings. Includine the subscriptions re 1 ceived the amount realided was £l3O. (Applause.) He thought that was very fair for the first uay's prcceeci- (Hear, hear.) The speaker proceeLicel to xplain fondly toe objects of the bazaar aud saiu tile friends con- nected with toe cilurcu were exceedingly grateful for the sympathy aad support they ,ha.o received. (Applause.) _</p>
        <p>• iiir.,DaviosoN, who presided, then called upon COuri- oilier Pierpoint to speaa, ri-maraing that they all appre- ciated his work amongst them anti his support exteuded to them as a denomination. ( epplause.) Councillor PIERPOINT sAid he came Were as a member of the Chuicu of &amp;valid in order to show how MuCa be appreeiated the work of his Isloucoeforinirt friends in Latchturd. Fle hoped his meld was sufficiently broad as to embrace almost all sympathies and almost all opinions upon religious matters. tie claimed for hunselt puttee t liberty mai he was quite wiliine tuat every man should worship in his own way, taking that liberty which he claimed he had a right to poasess. (Applause.) To speak at a bazaar was hardly in his way lor he felt rather strange in addressing an assemblage of people who were there furtheriug the interests they had so much at heart. It it bad bteti in cmnection with a cricket club,a football club or any club which developed tue muscles rather thee the head perhaps he should have felt much more at home in speaking than he aid now. (Laughter.) They must take the will for the deed. If any treacle or deeds ot his should add to the swocess of the bazaar he should cousiuer his time had been well sent. It was now 50 years ago since he first came to Latchford. Ile thouget he was theu a " rough uncouth buy but he was imbued with a good healtny conscitutieu and a fair appetite for hard work. Tnere was then no place of worship iii Latchford, but tuey had a schoolroom in Wash-lane, to wnich the Rectur frequently came aid ex- pounded Buuyares "Pitgriins Progress" and other litera- ture ot that kindibut they had no regular Sunday servicos. he remembered that at that time there were some very earnest members of the Baptist body who wended their way from Latentor d to a building set apart for worship at Stockton Heath which was called at teat time the old barn, and lie also recollected tue public baptisnas wlecn took place at Ackers Pits. lie had heard nothing of that kind for a great number of years, and he took it that they lied some facilities Of their own in conn::•ctiou with that buileing for conducting these baptisms in a manner more consistent with tue ideee of modern worship. ee had the pleasure of knowing a man whose name ssas still Levered by a greet many of the older mernners Of that congregation that was the Rev. Joseph Wiikinson. ((lear, hear.) The congrega- tion afterwards moved from the old portion of Stockton heath, and located themelvea in that corner ot Latch- ford. At that time tue building was better situated as it abutted on the inam road. By tue c instruction of the Ship Canal, very much to his regri:t and no di:mot to those connected witn the congregation, the church seemed to be cut eff aud placed in a le.tl corner. But a brotherly spiiit was present amongst the members, and they supported each other in a mariner which befit- ted a Christian community. Good work was still being dune there, and he wi,lieJ tli,m all the, pro:iperity it was posb.blo for a congregatiou to hive. he tweed they would go from strength to strength, end be a living force in the religious life of that corner ut Late:afore. (Applause). Mr. DAVIDSON then card upon Mrs P:erpoint to de- clare the bazaar open, an referred to ll goria Samaii- tan work sue ciii mu toe district. Mrs. PIERPOINT said the Cbairinon had made her feel 'neatly, ly nervous ; it was a very hard Whig to sit and feel comiortatrie vroilst hearing so many things said about hr. It she &amp;titer ved oue halt of what bad been said of her sue would not have lived in vaiu. She would like to remind wen] of Shakespeare's words that life was a stage and men aud women the actors. Sue would ask them to det teeir par: at that s ele and clear away all the pretty things offered for sale. See bad very great pleasure in declariug the hazier open. Mr. Geoaart WELLS pr,rposed a vote Of thanks to Councillor and Mrs Pierpuint, and referred is c.rmpli mentary terms to the good work they did in the dis- trict.</p>
        <p>Mr. ADAMS seo)uded and Mr ALBINSON supported the resolutiou.which was cartit.d wthsce.amatiun.</p>
        <p>PIERPOINT I:Lor,ng responded, bu_inesi c meuced.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>The haz.ar was (nailed cu. Friday by Mrs. John Fair- hurst, there being a large attendahee. Alter a b) mm had been sung, Too Rev, J. F. Marrukws said he had been lookiog forward to that closing atternum of their tiazatr tor several se.trions ; because in asking trifir two ti n.ls, °he to open the bf.z.ar and the other to preside, in h-fneuriiig them they were also hont.uring thtfm,elves. He thought their friend Mr. MN:ism), whom he tfai the Vetaute of salting to take the chair ought to he the hiiehtest and happiest mar who attended the Latchf,rd Baptist Cnurch. beou blessed with sous God 114 d been pleased to giro one ot whom was a j:iniur 11-i.tcon of their churef., t'id a pleacher of no mean ability, slid the presideu, • ' young man's Bible thiss ; another c. as 01,- secretary their Sunday tense] ; the third was the vief—president of their Chris- ian Endearaur t'ocie,y ; and the fourth was a member of tbe Bible ellbs and a consistent mem- ber of the Church. Of the daughters ill need say nothing. They were their own commendation. Be bad the gteatebt pleasure in asking Mr. Joseph Albin-</p>
