      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>NEWTON TIRBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>NEWTON TIRBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held in the Town Hall, Elrlestowa, on Monday evening. Mr. C. B. F. Borron, J.P., C.C., presided and there were also prescnt Messrs. W Collingwood, J W Ern:nett, N Siideley, W Gilmour, J Simpson, Dr Pattersm, W Thompson, J H Forster, E G Evans,T Ormond and W W Shirley (clerk), A BOWES (surveyor), D H Lloyd (gas engineel) and B Brierly (nuisance inspector). btEL. STONE.- . _</p>
        <p>The Chairman atated that he had received a communi- cation from Mr. Stone regretting that he was unable to attend the meeting owing to him absence in Scotland. THE VOTES OF CONDOLENCES.</p>
        <p>The Chairman read a letter from Mr. William Thomp- son which had been received by the Clerk stating twit he had to thank him on behalf ( f the family for his kind letter conveying the expression of sympathy and c3ndolence of the members of the Council on the death of their father. He asked that the Clerk would please convey to the Chairman and members their sincere thanks. Mr. Edward G. B. Worsley, of Winwick, writing on behalf of his mother and family stated that he desired sincerely to thank the members of the Council for their kind message of sympathy and condolence in their re- cent sad loss. He was at all times most cordially re- ceived by every member of the Council and it was a pleasure to know that his services were appreciated. TUE PROPOSED WATERWORKS</p>
        <p>MR. EMMETT HAS A----FEW QUESTIONS TO ASK. MR. GILMOUR AGAIN! A SCENE.</p>
        <p>The Chairman moved the confirmatiosa of the minutes of the previous meeting. Mr. Emmett said before the minutes wero confirmed he thought he would Ise in order in asking one or two questions about the water scheme. In the first place he understood that the rreliminary expenses cf sinking a borehole, Szc., had cost £1,200, and he vrshed to know whether that amount was includA in the esti- mates for the proposed waterworks. The sete,me which had been approved by the Council had been div ided into three parts. The first part w is to cost £7,000, the second £B,OOO, and the third £9,000, making a t)tal of £22,000. He wished to know if these included the boilers and boiler.houses. Then he also wanted to know whether the firdt part of the sch •me was complete in itself. From the report which nad been placel in the bands of the members it did no: appear to be so. He wanted to be satisfied whether it was a complete scheme or whether it required the second part to make it so. He would be glad if the Chairman would answer these questions. The Chairman said the queltione were a little out of order. _ .</p>
        <p>The Chairman : I move the confirmation of the rninutee.</p>
        <p>Dr. Patterson : I s?c mfl the proposition. The resolution was ca.rnod. TECHNICAL IN:TRUCTION GRANT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Pattorson moved the confirmation of a minote of the Technical L!struction Committee that the offer of th- Lancashre County Connell of the sum of -.E161 in aid of technical instruction for tbe years 1899-1900 be recommended to the Council for acceptance, The minute was passed.</p>
        <p>GAS AND WA TER COMMITTEE. THE QUALITY OF THE GAS.</p>
        <p>The Gas and Water Committee's minutes stated that the Gas Engineer submitted a plan for converting the disused purifying-house into a ph;:tometer room and offiee at an estimated cost,of £4B. It was resolved that estimates be obtained for same, including the boarding of the whole of tile floor and partitioning a certain por- tion according to the plan. The gas engineer's and gas manager's report upon street lighting by incandescent lights was read and considered, and it was resolved that 100 special street lanterns, to replace the existing lanterns for the incandescent lights, be purchased, and that as the burners wear out they be replaced by the latest system of incandescent burner. Teat 25 street lamp columns and 25 special street lanterns with incans desc,at burners be also purchased, to be fixed in positions to be hereafter determined upon. The report of the sub-committee of the system obtaining in Manchester, Liverpool and Bolton with reference to the examination and collection of moneys from the automatic gas meters was road and considered, after which it was resolved that the Gas and Water Com- mittee be recommended to engage three competent m ,te: inspectors at wages of 26s per week for tha first or chief inspector, and 23s per week each for the other two inspectors, with a full suit of uaiform every year and an overcoat and maekintosh every two years. Each inspector to be gearaisteed at the expense of the Council in a sum of £25 eac'. The two inspectors engaged at 231 per week to be sabordinete to the chief inspector. The chief inspector and the two subordinate inspectors to be primarily responsible to the collector at the Town flan. It was ordered that the Clerk write to Mr. Pardey and suggest that as the Council are about to lay a gas main from Crow-lane to Wargrave-road (terms of wayleave for which had been verbally agreed upon) it would be an opportune time for the opening out of a road from the two points named. At a sob.equent meeting a letter, was read from Mr.Pardey stating, in reply to the Clerk's letter, that there was no inteutiou on the part of Lord Newton to opea out a public road from Crow-lane (im- mediately opposite to Water-street) to Wargrave-road and that he was unable to accede to the request of the Council to do so.</p>
        <p>It a :,•pacial meeting held on the 2nd inst. it was re- silved that the tender of Messrs:D Parson, of Brierley Hill, for fourteen and eight inch cast iron main pipes, and that of Mr J Ritchie, of Middlesboro fir six, four, and three inch main pipes, be recommended to the Council for acceptance at the prices named in the resp Active tenders. It was also resolved that tenders be obtained for the navvy work required for laying gas pipes from the Gasworks to the Vulcan Foundry, and to Messrs. McCorquodale's works. It was further resolved that Messrs Builough's tender for a station governor at £143 14s be recommended for acceptance. Plans submitted for the erection of engine and boiler house, and chimney for the water works were approved, and the Council were recommended to invite tenders for tin same.</p>
        <p>The Chairman moved the confirmation of the minutes. Mr. Evans seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ecnmett said he was sorry to make complaints against the Gas Department but he believed they were now making the worst gas since he came to Earlestown. He did not know whose fault it was but there was hardly any illuminating power at all. Tile Chairman promised to bring the matter before the notice of the Gas Works Extension Committee.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilmour : We will have the electric light before long. Proceeding to revert to the waterworks Mr. Gil- mour said he believed it would prove to be a white ele- phant. ___ ____ . .</p>
        <p>- The minutes were passed, Mr. Gilmnur being the only dissontient.</p>
        <p>HIGHWAYS ND SANITARY COMMITTEE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <p>Mr. Emmett said he must apelogise for asking the questions now. He happened to be absent at the last meeting when the scheme was approved, but he could notheote for the connrmation of the proceedings unlees he was satiseed that they were in the interes's oi the public. The Chairman remarked that the question before the Council at the present time was whether the minutes were a correct record of the preceedinge. He was sorry that Mr. Emmett was not present when the question Was before the Council and when the scheme was peassd. Be (the Chairman), explained the scheme at that time. Seeing that Mr. Emmett had taken coesiderable interest in the subject, if the Council would forbear with him he would be pleased to answer the various points raised. With reference to the first ques- tion respecting the expenditure of £1,200. That sum had , been spent and the money had been raised ' by a rate upou the township. Therecore it was not in- cluded in the estimates for the proposed works. Dealing with the question raised regarding boilers and boiler houses those were included in the estimate. Speaking from memory be shculd say that the first part af the scheme was a scheme in itself. This portion of the schene was to supply water for the district, and the second part ot the scheme was to act as a safeguard. It would eneble them to provide tanks, duplicate engines, purn: s, &amp;c., and place them in a position to meet any emergency. Mr. Emmett remarked that if the first part of the scheme was complete in itsAf he did not see why it was necessary to sink a second borehole, which, he under• stood, was part of the second portion of the scheme. The Chairman stated that as he had explained on a previeus occasion the firt borehole which had been sunk only supplied the quantity of water at pres.nt being consumed in the district. The second borehole was to act as a safety ealve, and prevent them being without water in case anything happened in connection with the first one* Duplicate pumps would be provided and they would then be in a position to avoid stoppages which took place when supplies were taken from boreholes only. Mr. Emmett remarked that he had read the engineers report once or twice, and he was still trea,ble to see that scheme No. 1 did net run into scheme No. 2. If No. 1 scheme was complete in itself he would like to know whether the cost of the second borehole was included in scheme No. 1 or scheme No. 2. The Chairman pointed out that it was included in No. 1, and wa3 referred to as an additional borehole. Mr. Emmett said supposine they did not got the water they anticipated from the second borehole he supposed they would immediately go on with the vconcl portion of the scheme which would bring the estimate up to £13,000. The Council seemed to have committed itself to a scheme which was incomplete, and they were then bound to go forward and spend money on she s-cand portion of the scheme, and if that wee found insufficient they would have to go on' with the thiri section, which would bring up the total coat to something like £24,000 including the preliminary expenses. He wag of opinion that this scheme would cos% the ratepxyars £30,090. He would like to be satisfied aboet the matter before he voted for the money to be spent. The Chairman : There is no vofing in the matter new. Mr. Emmett: I am afraid the ratepayers don't under- stand it. Mr. Gilmour: Mr. Chairman, you have made a little slip in your explanation. The Chairman : I cannot allow you to speak. Mr. Gilmour : You have allowed Mr. Emmett. The Chairman : 1 asked the permission of the Coun- cil because Mr. Emmett was not present at the last meeting. Mr. Gilmour: I must have an explanation. The Chairman, replying to Mr. Emmett said the second section of the scheme went with the first. One was a security ageing the other. Mr. Emmett said if the first and second section,' were found insufficient they would be bound to go on with the third section. The Chairman explained that the third section had nothing to do with the pumping gf water. It was for the provision of a reservoir for storing purposes. Mr. Emmett : I can quite understand that. The Chairman : Senn; experts say that is not neces- sary ; but that point will come up when it is con- sidered necessary to make it. The subject could then be discussed. Mr. Emmett : If you are going to have a Govern- ment inquiry I ePseme that you will ask for power to birrosv the whole amount. The Chairman: That is quite true; but the Council will be able to say hew it shall he spent. Mr. Gilmour again rose to speak. The Chairman tild him that he was not entitled to speak as he was preseet during the whole of the previ- ons diftcussion. Mr. Giltn-,ur said at the previous meeting he asked whether the sum of £l2OO which had already been spent was included in the estimate. The Chairman said it was but be had now told Mr. Emmett that it was to nem,. out of the rates. He would ask for details of that £1,200. It Was a fair qu(stlon. The Chairman : We are not going into further ex- planation. All these particulars were printed before the last meeting of the Council and it was then for you to raise objections about the £1,200 already spent or any other sum. Mr. Gilmour ! I did raise the question and you haven't told the truth. Cries of " Order, order." Mr. Collingwood : I rise to a point of order. (Hear, bear.) Mr. Gilmour : You have no right to sprak. Mr. Collingwocd : I think we ought to put a stop to these insinuations. It is disgraceful for a man to talk like that. I will not sit here and tear it. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Gilmour: You must sit down. The Chairman tells me one thing at one meeting and contradicts him- self at the next. He has treated me with kindness but only answers me when he chooses. The Chairman : I thank Mr. Collingwood and the tear:Oen of the Council for supporting the chair, but I have determined that will not take the slightest notice of insinuations that may come from Mr. Gilmour. He uses words that I believe he does not really intend to use. Therefore I shall not take the slightest notice of him. _</p>
        <p>Mr Gilmour : I shall preso for an explanation if I stand here until domesday. It is a serious matter for the ratepayers if this £1,200 has to come out of the rates. Mr. Emmett : I am very much 'surprised that Mr. Gilmour shon!ci have got up in this Council and charged the Chairman with not speaking the truth. I have con- fidence in the Chairman and I believe he has spoken the truth in the statement he has made. If Mr. Gilmour has not properly understood the matter I think it is only due to the Chairman that be should apologise. *Mr. Ormand rose to speak. Mr. Gilmour : I protest. M. Ormand : One member of the Council is only entitled to address the chair once on one question. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Gilmour is under a misapprehen- sion cf one kind or other, I have followed very closely all the arguments for and against the water scheme, and I know that the Chairman did not state that the esti- mate incluced the cost of No. 1 borehole. ;Hear. hear.) Be never gave utterance to any such thing. Mr. 15ilmour: After that explanation I withdraw the statement I made. I was under the imprersion that the Chairman said the BUM mentioned was included in the estimsto, dtbst la told oto</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <p>The minutes of the Highways and Sanitary Committee contained the following:—That tho Melieal Practitioners of the district be respectfully requested, as an act of courtesy to the Council, to give them the advantage of more definite particulars when certifying the cause of death to the district registrar of deaths as to the dura- tion of the disease, especially in: the fatal cases of non- notiftable infectious disease ; and that the district regis- trar of deaths be requested to furnish the Medical Officer of Health with a notice of the death of all infectious cases immediately upon re- ceipt of the certificate of such death. The Sewage Disposal Committee reported that they had to recommend the Committee to adopt the bacteria- logicsl system of sewage disposal, and for that purpose to incur an expenditure (estimated) of £lBO, for the conversion of the tanks at the central and west outfall sewerage works to the proposed system. And that a sewer be made to drain the Mill-lane portion of the township into the central outfall works, at an estimated cost of £240. And also that the Local Goverment Board he informed of the decision of the committee to adopt the bacteriological system of sewage disposal,and asked for their sanction to the lean for private streets. After a discussion it was resolved that the foregoing report be approved and adopted, subject to the surveyor conferr- ing with Mr. Naylor, of the Lancaehire County Council, as to the proposed scheme of Sewage Disposal. THE QUESTION OF A LAW CLERK.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General Purposes Com-raittee a letter was read from Mr. H. J. Longton, solicitor, of Earlestown, asking the Council to favourably consider his amine ae the successor is the late Mr. J. E. Worsley, eolicitpr to !he Council. It was ordered that the Clerk write to Mr. Longton and inform him that the Council do not propose to appoint anyone to the position formerly held by Mr. Worsley but to retain a free hand as to in- structing a solicitor as occasion may arise. It was also resolved that as Messrs. Peace and Ellie, of Wigan, solicitors, have in hand an important matter on behalf of the Council, apt Clerk to the Council is hereby ordered to ask them to be good enough to act on their behalf in the matters of "The Cluncil v. Lyon "and " 'The Council v. Brown" (refusal to pay tolls for street hawking), and 0 make such arrangements as may he necessary (if such arrangements are not already com- pleted) for the carrying on of the appeal already lodged by the Council against the decision of the Warrington County Cenrt Judge in these cases. The minutes were passed on the proposition of the Chairman and the Council shortly afterwards rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>-,-.0,--- WHE RE THE RUNCORN FOOTBALLERS</head>
        <p>-,-.0,--- WHE RE THE RUNCORN FOOTBALLERS</p>
        <p>COME FROM.</p>
        <p>BY THE "SCORCHER."</p>
        <p>Last Saturday, as by far the greatest portion of the " Lianets " were doing service for Cheshire, at Wbitehaven, I bethought myself that a stroll down to Irwell-lane would bring me face to face with the material from which the full fledged " Red tail "is hatched, as Runcorn "A " were there entertaining another local fifteen—Runcorn Recreation. For junior locals the exhibition was of a high order,and I was convinced of the fact that the present team doing service for Runcorn by no means exhausts all the "good players" of whom Runcorn can proudly boast. There were some lads playing in that loeal game, who will make as fine a lot of players as ever put heads together in a few years. C. Butterworth will make as fine a threfequarter as his brother "Jimmy," and his inclusion in the first fifteen is likely to occur at any moment. Indeed the fact that there is such a good man waiting to take his place on that coveted three-quarter line, should make those "four" who now occupy it really proud of their position. As a half-back, something after Jolley' e style, W Gayter stands out, and shows pro- mising qualities. Amongst the forwards there are a few good pushers, half-a-dozen of whom might account for themselves creditably any day. Then again the Reareation also supply the great" First " when required; without taking into account the stingir,g youngsters that the Runcorn Hor- nets could place at their disposal, and both those teams have amongst them really tal- ented manipulaters of the oval. Able, whose prowess as a quarter miler is well-known, also asaisted the "A "team, and if he would really go in for the game would make a marvel, something after the Crompten style, but as he plays at present he is far too effeminate for Northern Union, and therefore out of the question. The game was a spirited one, but considerably spoilt through too much feeling being displayed. It resulted in a win for the " A" team by 6 points to 3—but it was only natural that " class would tell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>NEXT SATURDAY'S GAME.</head>
        <p>NEXT SATURDAY'S GAME.</p>
        <p>Tyldesley will give the Irwell-lane —enclosure a visit next Saturday. They should of course be beaten, but whether they will remains to be seen. They are one of the teams I ara afraid of, and their forwards will want some holding in check, as Jolley, Butterworth, and Houghton can well testify. The way to victory lies with the Runcorn neck, and where they lead there will be a capable following up. But if they are not watchful next Saturday, something distasteful is going to happen. Tyldes- ley is no easy snap, and the more the " Green-uns " recognise that fact, the more secure shall I feel of victory.</p>
        <p>ASK :or CUNNINGHAM' HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATICRS.—Advt</p>
        <p>CLEARANCE SALE OF CYCLES, New and Second- 'iand.-3. S. G.sai.ams.sl to 55, Bridge-street, Warring- ton, A dv3</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER TM&amp; CLOCK in BEST BOOTS Ix LARCASIIIRE.-Advt.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN moved the adoption of the reports and said he could speak with a full knowledge of the very valuable work which the nurses performed in the town. They were very hard worked, and if it were possibla to raise sufficient funds to add two others to the staff it would be money exceedingly well spent, as there was plenty of work to co. (Hear, hear.) He heard from many sources of the great appreciation of the work, particularly amongst the women of the towe, and he hoped that now it had begun it would b 3 continued and would not be interfered with owing to a lack of funds. With regard to the finances, it would be seen that the subscription list was rather less than it was last year, but be thought that arose from various causes which would not be repeated. A very handiJme subscription had been promised through Mr. John White from the grand-daughter of the late Lord Winmarleigh, and it would be much appreciated coming from such a source. (Applause.) But there was another pleasing feature, and it was this, namely, the very handsome subscription from the members of the Band of Hope Union. They made a sort of street col- lection a few weeks ago, which resulted in the hand- some sum of £3B being realized. (Applause.) That was a source of income they did not posseas last year. Then there was another source of income that he hoped would gradually increase, and that was from the congrega- tional cellections. If there was any work which should appeal to the sympathies of the Christian community of that town it was this work of mercy, and he hoped that as the years went on instead of having eight or ten congregations making collections in aid of the funds that it would be an exception for any congregation not to subscribe. (Flear,hear.) Still the amount received was £3l 12s. 51., and it was very acceptable. They began the present year with a balance in hand of £27 Bs. 6i. (Applause.) The Rev. CANON WILLIS seconded, and said he wished to add his testimony to the excellent work which the Association was doing. They were all indebted to Mr. Monks for his interest in it, and the help he gave it. He was sure that the nurses had diligently carried out their duties during the past year.</p>
        <p>The Rey: E. C. E.-CARLETON, in supporting the motion, alluded to the appreciation of the public in the work of the Association, but that appreciation was not more than they had a right to expect. It was a Christian-like work, and he also felt how indebted they were to Mr. Monks for his services. It was a work which in the future be would look back upon with satisfaction. (Hear, hear.) The Rev W H Dattarmone contended that the time had come when it might be very well to underline the words to which the chairman had referred about the necessary increase of the nurses. Anyone who had followed their work would be aware that the time had come when they must either over-work themselves or curtail what they were now doing by refusing to take cases, and that would be very undesirable in the early history of the Society,and would seriously injure the cause in the esti- mation of the people of the town. But it was a fact that they had now more work for the nurses than they had time and strength to accomplish, and it had also to be borne in mind that up to the present they had been working their superintendent not as a superintendent but as an ordinary nurse. The duty of the superintendent nurse was to supervise the work of the other nurses, to keep in touch with all their work and to be the inspiring spirit and not to take up a number of cases as Miss Whitfield had to do at the present time. (Hear, hear.) It was quite impossible for her under the pre sent circumstances to supervise the work of the town a&amp; she ought to do. It was like appointing a chief con- stable and expecting him to do ordinary policeman's work. Everything pointed to the necessity of the appointment shortly of two new nurses to undertake more cages and set the seperintendent free to do her real work—that of superintendent, for which she was appointed. (Applause.) There was one part of the report to which he should like to draw the attention of the meeting. Last year the balance was £ll9 12s. 9d. ; this year it was £27 Ss. 61. ; so that it could not be said that they were in a very healthy and satisfactory con- dition. They ought to get a larger subscription list and a greater number of smaller subscribers. (Hear, bear.) The Rev, E. T. REED explained that the street collec- tion was not entirely taken up be the Band of Hope Union. Three societies joined in the lort which the B.`nd of El lee initiated—the Bind of Hope Union, the Christian Endeavour society and the British Women's Temperanee A.sociation. He wished to add his personal testimony to the great work the nurses were doing in the town. JApplauae.)</p>
        <p>The Rev: -JouN YONIn thought the time had come, or was near at hand, when they would need an inerease of the staff. He also thought most distinctly that Miss Whitfield should be set free from the work of the ordin- ary nurses in order that .he might give more complete oversight to the work going on throughout the town and district. No one except those acquainted with the Home could have any idea of the amount of labou devolving upon her in her office of superintendent, and these who had seen it would testify that everyone was greatly indebted to her for the admirable manner in which all the aeeounts were kept and the details filled up, so that everything was carried on in the most admirable manner. (Hear, hear and applause.) The question of finance was one he hoped the committee would take into serious consideration, how to increase the funds and to bring the matter before the general constituency of the town, in their congregations, and in fact, in all parts of the town and district. (Applause.) The CIIAIRMAN hoped they would not be tempted to go beyond what they had a reasonable chance of getting and have to be put to the necessity of running round with the hat to make up their deficiencies. An object like this ought to be supported cheerfully, and they ought not to do anything in the way of extension until</p>
        <p>INQUEST ANn VERDICT. Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, held an in- quest in the Parade Room at the Police Station on Tuesday evening, on the body of Thomas Patrick Lally, aged two yeare,who died in the Infirmary on Monday from burns.—Bridget Lally,wife of Thomas Lally, rubber worker, 62, Sc. James-street, Latch- ford, said deceased was her son and was two years of age last birthday. On Monday morning at 9-45 she had occasion to leave the house to make a pur- chase at a shop. She left deceased and a younger child in the house. She was not away above five eninutes,the shop being next door but one,but when she came back she found deceased in flames in the kitchen. The baby was on the sofa. She ex- tinguished the flames and took him to the Infirmary.—The Coroner: They were rather young children to leave. Would not a neighbour have come in to look after them? Witness: I could have left them with the woman next door if I had had the presence of mind to do so.- The Coroner asked how she thought the boy could have got on fire.—Witness said she thought he must have got hold of a piece of paper and put it in the fire. The fire was very low. She found some charred paper* on the floor.—Nurse Isherwood said deceased was admitted to the Infirmary at about 10 o'clock on Monday morning suffering from severe burns all over the body. There was no chance of his re- covery and he died at 11.45.—Detective Barlow in answer to the Coroner said deceased's parents ware very respectable people: He had known them for a number of years.—The foreman of the jury said he thought it necessary under such circumstances to leave someone to take care of the children.—A Juryman: They do not always think of that when they leave them.—The Coroner said they should take into consideration the condition of life in which the parents were living.—The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WARRINGTON DISTRICT NURSING ASSOCIATION. A SATISFACTORY YEAR'S WORK.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON DISTRICT NURSING ASSOCIATION. A SATISFACTORY YEAR'S WORK.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Monks, J.P., C C., presided at the annual meeting of the Warrington District Nursing Asso- ciation, held in the Lecture Room, at the Museum, on Tuesday afternoon. Amongst those present were the Revs Canon Willis, E CE Carleton, F E Powell, V( 11 Drummond, John Yonge, E T Reed, and Mr John White, Dr Guest Gornall, Mr C Madeley, Mr J D Fairclough, (hon. secretary), and a large nuraber of ladies.</p>
        <p>THE REPORTS.</p>
        <p>The Committee in their second annual report stated that they had much pleasure in recording the second, year's work of the association. So far everything undertaken had been greatly appreciated, and muchl good had been done amongst the very poor. Since the last annual meeting Miss Wortabet had resigned, and an additional nurse had been engaged. These two posts were at present most satisfactorily filled by Misses I Leshaw and Harvey. To meet the requirements of this addition to the staff, the home in Arpley-street had been re-arranged and made thoroughly comfortable. The heme had been visited by an inspector from head quarters, and her report was most satisfactory. On July 4th, Miss Whitfield received a silver superintend- ent's badge of the Queen Victoria's Jubilee institute, and Misses Leshaw and Harvey the 'bronze badge and brassard from the hands of the Princess Louise at Ken- sington Palace. Miss Jacocke already possessed these tokens of proficiency from the institute. The Treasurer's report showed the Association on a good financial basis, and the Cemmittee hoped that the Association would centinue to meet with the ready help it had received from the townspeople during the past, The Committee desired to thank all those who had in any way centrihu- ted to the funds. It only remained for the Committee to thank the Superintendent and her staff for the able way in which they had conducted their work, and express confluence in them ie every reeeeee Miss WHITFIELD (the superintendent) in her report stated that it was one of progress and success. A com- parison of the statistics of this year with those of last year would show that the association was neither stationary nor retrogressive. Not only had its useful- ness been increased by the adeition of another nurse to the staff, but its benefits were becoming more widely known and its methods better understood. Very little inquiry from those who knew by experience the advant- age derived from the visits of the district nurses would serve to prove with what appreciation the efforts of the subseribers and supporters to provide skilled nursing for them in their time of eicknese were regarded, and such inquiry would at the same time effectually dispel any doubt as to the claim of the association to continued and increased support. A pleasing evidence of progress was the increasing demand made for the services of th 6 nurses by the patients and their friends. A very large proportion of the cases reported as being sent in by the medical men were so sent in at the request of the patients of their friends, or of those interested in their welfare. The number of housewives nursed was very striking,lss out of a total of 291 patient!, and it was perhaps these who more especially appreciated the boon of being at- tended by a district nurse. In many cases the poor women were left to the care of children much too young to understand the importance of anything but play, and their gratitude for the ministrations of the nuree,where- by they were made elean and comfortable, and were fed at least once a day, often found expression in the cry ; "I'd have been lost but for you nurse." Gifts of all kinds for the use of the patients, but especially of old linen, old sheets, blankets, garments. appliances, &amp;c., are always most welcome and will be gratefully received by the Superintendent, at 41, Arpley-street. The following is a summary of eases dealt with : July Ist, 1898, to June 30th, 1899 : 391 cases nursed 9,854 visits paid. Result of cases nursed : Convalescent. or relieved, 178 ; transferred to hospital or infirmary,l7: died, 63; removed from books for ether causes, 11 ; still on books, June 30th, 1899, 22; total, 291. By whom the cases were sent in : Medical men, 239 ; clergy,ministers, and visitors, 26 ; patients' friends, 18 ; found by nurses, 6 ; employers, 2 ; total, 291. The balance sheet accompanying the reports showed that the total income for the year was £282 2s 3d which with a balance brought forward from the previous year of £ll9 12s 9i made a total of £4Ol 15s 01. After meet- ing the expenses of the year there was a balance carried t 3 the next account Of £27 8s 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <p>111 E EXAMINER FRIDAY, OCTOBER 6, 1899 INTERESTING WEDDING AT PADGATE. BENNET-IT-BENNETT.</p>
        <p>they saw their way to a sufficient income for the pur pose. As to the necessity of two additional nurses they • were all ogreei, as the present ones were overworked. He had been afraid of a breakdown m3re than oace. He oped that when the new committee got to work they viould be enabled to leave Miss Whitfield to do the real wcrk of superintendent. (Hear, hear and applause.) le report was then agreed to. APPOINTMENT OF OFFICERS AND C IMMITTEE. Tilf. following officers and committee were re-appointed en the proposition of Miss Riminer :—President, Lord Lilford vice-presidents, the Mayor, Rev Canon Willis, and Mr k Monks ; secretaries, Mrs John Fairclough, and Mr J Dutron Fairclough ; treasurer, Mr F Monks; and committee rf Management,Mesdames A.rmitage,Carleton, Drummond, Edlesten, Fairclough, John Fairclough, Fox Garnett, Harison, Massy, Monks and Yonge, and the Rev E C E Carleton, Rev W El Drummond, Dr Fox, Mr J Fairclough, Mr Fanshawe, Mr John Roberts, Dr Sherratt, Mr G lhompson, Mr John White, Rev Father Wilson, Rev John Yonge. and Dr Joseph. The Rev. E. T. REED made a suggestion that in future the committee shoule. b 3 elected by ballot. It might be that some would object to certain individuals, but did not care ti do so openly, VOTH OF TISANES TO THE NURSING STAFF. The CHAIRMAN, in proposing a vote of thanks to Miss Whitfield and the staff, spoke of the admirable way in which they had performed their duties. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>There was a village weiding at Paclfate on Wed- nesday, and although there was none of that rustic simplicity about it which Luke Fildee has so graphically depicted in his celebrated picture "The Village Wedding," yet it was one in which the whole village seemed more or less interested. Pad- gate, by the way, is rapidly becoming something more than a mere village. In place of green fields with here and there a farmstead or a dwelling- house, there are now long rows of houses with a plentiful sprinkling of shops which give it the ap- pearance of a small town, or perhaps what the American would be more disposed to call a city. With Padgate the name of Bennett has been for many years closely associated, and one of the distinguishing characterie Lica of those who have borne it has been their staunch NOD - conformity and loyalty to the Wesleyan Church. On Wednesday, the unique event was witnessed of members of two branches of the Bennett family being united in the bonde of matri- mony. The contracting parties were Mr. James Bennett, M.R.C.S., L.R.C.P. (London), of Linton Giange, son of Mr. John Bennett, of the Westlands, Padgate, and Miss Mary Bennett. fourth daughter of the late NIA% Thomas Bennett, of Pear Tree House Padgate. Both were connected with the choir of the Wesleyan Chapel and on Sunday evening, at the close of the service. Mr. E. L. Pearce, on behalf of the choir presented Dr. and Miss Mary Bennett, on the occasion of their marriage, with a case of silver spoons and sugar tongs. In doing so Mr. Pearce expressed the congratulations of the choir, who, he said, wished them much happiness. lie hoped that Miss Bennett would not sever her con- neetion with the choir and that Dr. Bennett would continue to act as cur:mut:Ler. miss Dcnnetc inl.lln absence of the doctor, suitably responded. This was but one evidence of the esteem and respect in which both Dr. and Miss Mary Bennett are held, and on Wednesday it found expression in various ways. Flags were to be seen protruding from windows of many houses in the neighbourhood, and a lino of flags had been drawn across the street opposite the Wesleyan Chapel. At the homes of the contracting parties bunting: was displayed and altogether Pad- gate presented a festive appearance. The time fixed for the ceremony was twelve o'clock, but the congregation commenced to assemble in the Wesleyan church before that hour, and when the ceremony was commenced,the build- ing was crowded. The Communion table contained a choice collection of flowers. During the arrival of the guests, sir. Finch, the organist, played .several suitable selections on the organ including "Andante" (C E Melville), "Offertoire" (E 6) by A Leprevost, and the "Grand Festival Overture" (W H Richmond). Shortly after the appointed time, the ceremony commenced with the singing of "The Voice that Breathed o'er Eden."</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. T. REED seconded, and inquired whether Miss Whitfield attended the meeting of the committee. The CHAIRMAN stated that she did not. The Rev. J. YONGE thought it desirable that she should attend the meetings. Her presence would be a great help very often, (Hear, hear.) The vote of thanks was heartily carried. Mrs. GOSLETT, a lecturer on hygiene to the Queen's Jubilee nurses and to several colleges then delivered an address. She said the district nurses were deing a great and grand work all over the country, and it had been aptly described as the grandest work of the close of the present century. It illustrated eloquently one of the great features of their day, a universal kindliness, and which was peculiar to their own times, and a feel- ing of a sense of responsibility they had for their fellow creatures. They Ws that the, anuit help weir brothers. They were not their brother's keeper, but they were their brother's brother. From the most selfish point of view it was wise to help the poor. If they were to keep disease out of their own homes they must see that the alleys and the slums of the town were k-pt free from the germs by which disease was spread. They felt, therefore, that every effert should be made towards in- ducing people to live better, purer, sod more sanitary lives. They had district nurses in thousands, but they wanted more. The nurses' influence upon the people they visited was astonishing. It was at humaniz- ing iufluence, and wrought incalculable good. In their trouble and sorrow people would listen to advice regard- ing their meth eds of living, advice upon sanitation and ventilation, and that advice the nurse was in a singu- larly suitable position to give. She believed in a town like Warrington they must know something of typhoid fever which was a disgrace to civilisation and a disgrace to the community in which it existed, because it could only come from conditions of unwholesomeness. It was an absautely preventible disease. There was a great lack of knowledge amongst the people in these matters. There were actually people who still talked of typhoid and typhus as if they were the same thing ; and there were ethers who did not know that consumption was infee- tioes, and who did not disinfect their rooms. If there were any there who said that the people would not act even if they were taught in these matters she should flatly contradict them because her experience was otherwise. District nursing could not pauperize. With the nursing institution there were no "deserving sick." If people were sick they attended them. (Hear, hear.) They helped them, but they did not spoil them. The speaker proceeded to advocate an increase in the nurs- ing staff at 'Warrington. One nurse to every ten thou- sand of the population was a very serious conditiou of things. What they would do, would be to kill their nurses. Ne woman could do such work. The nurses had very difficult duties to perform, and deserved to be cheered and enceuraged in their work which WAS of such a charaeter that it shortened their lives.</p>
        <p>On the motion of the CHLIRMAN: a: hearty vote 01 thanks was passed to Mrs. Goslett for her address.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was also passed to the Chairman' on the motion of the Rev. E. C. E. CARLETON, seconded by the Rev. F. E. POWELL.—rhe Chairman havinf acknow- ledged the compliment the proceedings terminated.</p>
        <p>CHILD BURNT TO DEATH AT WARRINGTON.-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>AN OLD WOMAN MISSINeI FROM BURTO,IIIOOD.</p>
        <p>A police notice has been issued stating that Mary Derbyshire, 70 years of age, of Lumber-lane, Bur- tonwood, is missing from home. She is of medium height, full featured, wears a soft bonnet, grey and white shawl, black stockings. She left home on Friday last, saying she was going for a walk. She took a little dog with her, but the dog returned later in the day, and the old lady has not since been seen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>FIRE AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>FIRE AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Abent half-past eight on Sunday -Morning, P.C. Lennie received information that a dwelling van was on fire in the back yard of No. 58, Market- street, Earlestown. He informed Inspector Morgan and together they went to the place and found that the travelling dwelling van belonging to Mrs. Ognen was on fire. Thinking someone might be inside they burst the door open, but found that the occupant was out. The fire was extinguished with buckets of water, but not before one side of the van was destroyed, the damage being estimated at .£l5 and was not insured. Mrs. Ogden said she had only left the van a few minutes, leaving a small fire, and when she returned she found the police extinguishing the flames which had got a good hold of the van.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>NEWTON DIVISION BOWLING ASSOCIATION. _</head>
        <p>NEWTON DIVISION BOWLING ASSOCIATION. _</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening there was a gathering at the Pied Bull Hotel, Newton, in connection with the presentation of the Newton Division Bowling Association Challenge Cop to the Haydock Bawling Club who have this year proved the winners. The chairman of the association, Mr. Frnest Grace pre- sided supported by Colonel R. Pilkington, James Ryder, Messrs C.B.F. Borron, JP., J. Jackson J.P., and G. Clarke. The Chairman very briefly in- troduced Colonel Pilkington to the members, and said they were very much indebted to the gallant. member for coming that night. Colonel Pilkingtou then rose for the purpose of presenting the cup to Mr. J. Jackson as representative of the Haydoek club. He said it afforded him very great pleasure indeed to visit them. In fact it was a pleasure to be among them at any time. (Hear, hear.) He spoke in eulogistic terms of the various kinds of sports and pastimes of the British public, saying how very necessary and useful they were in develop- ing both mind and body. He further particularised the fact that the game of bowls was very popular amongst colliers. He was pleased to present the cup to Haydoek,though he must not be prejudiced in any way, because there were other clubs in the division and it would not do for him to favour one more than another. If Hardock won it a second time, he supposed he would have to find another cup, and that he would very gladly do. He then handed the cup to Mr. Jackson, who responded in suitable term'. The Chairman afterwards proposed a vote of thanks to the Hon. member, which Mr. Jackson seconded, and it was heartily accorded.—Colonel Pilkington briefly acknowledged and proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman, which the Rev. J. Ryder seconded, and it was unanimously carried.— A zapital knife and fork tea was afterwards dis- patched, and the remainder of the evening was spent barmaiosaly.;</p>
        <p>This is the first Nonconformist marriage, we be- lieve, which has taken place in this district without the presence of the registrar. The new Act is so cumbered with restrictions and bound up in red tapeiem that it has not been generally adopted by Nonconformist bodies in this district. The officiat- ing ministers on this occasion were: Rev. H. G. Roberts, M.A., Rev. J. Grange Bennett (brother of the bridegroom), and the Rev. T. F. Nicholson (brother-in-law of the bride). The bride was accompanied to the communion Jails by her brother, Mr. W. J. Bennett, of Law CfoB3, Poulton-le-Fylde, who gave her away. She was charmingly attired in a dress of white benga- line poplin trimmed with yoke an 4 fichu of chiffon and orange blossom. She wore a wreath of orange ' blossoms and veil, and her ornaments included a gold self-closing bracelet, set with torquoise and pearls, the gift of the bridegroom. She carried a beautiful shower bouquet composed of lilies of the valley, stephanotis, roses and fern. The bridesmaids I were Miss H. Bennett ',sister of the bride), Mies Emily Bennett, Miss Florence Bennett (sisters of the bridegroom.) They wore dresses of white alpaca, trimmed with Irish lace, over pale-green glac4 silk :and chiffon. They also wore black velvet picture hats with white plumes, and carried shower bouquets of pink roses, maiden hair and smilax. Each received as a present from the bride- groom, a gold swallow brooch set with pearls. Two little pages were also in attendance in the persons of Master Clifford Graugo Bennett (nephew) and Miss Marie Bennett (niece). Miss Marie wore a white lace slip over pink China silk and a picture hat of white felt and plumes, whilst Master Clifford was neatly attired in a black velvet Lord Fauntleroy suit, and displayed a gold pin, the gift of the bridegroom. The duties of best man were satisfactorily discharged by Mr. Frederic S. Bennett, of London, brother of the bridegroom. He was assisted by two grolmsmen, namely, Mr. Alfred Bannett,(Poulton-le-Fylde) and Mr. Leonard Bennett. The hymn "0 Father all creating" was sung by the congregation, and at the close of the ceremony Mr. Finch gave an effective rendering of Mandelesoba's " Wedding MATO," A reception was bald at rear Tree House, at which about 80 invited guests were present. Later in the day supper was served to a large number of relatives. The wedding party was photographed on the lawn by Mr. T. Birtles. In the afternoon Dr. and Mrs. Bennett departed for Lynton and Ilfracombe, where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride's travelling dress was a dark green smooth cloth coat and skirt, with green velvet blouse, shot with silver, and a green felt hat to match and natural feather boa. Appended will be found a list of the wedding presents :</p>
        <p>Mother of bride, slyer tea service a nd hot water jug Bridegroom to bride, self-closing bracelet, set with tur- quoise and pearls Bride to bridegroom. surgery furniture Father of bridegroo o, cheque Mother of bridegroom, pair of bronzes, tea and dinner ser- viees, and honsehold requisites Miss H Bennett, brass fire suite, and silver serviette rings Mr Alfred Bennett, dessert service Mr P Houghton Bennett, marble timepiece Bev T F and Mrs Nicholson, china tea service Mr and Mrs? Wright and family, worked bed spread Mr and Mrs W J Bennett (Foulton-le-Pylde). cheque HT and Mrs fa Hutchinson, case dessert knives and forks Tom. Bert, Frank and Margie Nicholson, Doulton hot- water jug Muriel, Dees and Marie Bennett, silver and ivory bread fork</p>
        <p>Mr and Mrs NY Grange Bennett (Garstang) salad bowl Rev I Grange and Mrs Bennett (Bolton), drawing-room overraantel</p>
        <p>Mr and Mrs Joseph Bennett, silver revolving entree dish Miss Brally Bennett. pictures,pair drawing-room chairs and bamboo table Miss Florence Bennett. duet stool Mr F Stanley Bennett, picture " The Doctor" Mr Leonard Bennett, case of cutlery Winifred and Hilda Bennett, ivory and silver knife rests t Ol fforol Grange Bennett butter kaife _ _ .</p>
        <p>F _lrene Riggill-Bennett Worcester bon-bon dish</p>
        <p>311 es Luaii(iforneastie), Worcester jug Mrs Niche son. Woreetter shell and fruit dish</p>
        <p>Mr leseph Bennett sen, cheque Mr and Mrs Dupr6 (Jersey), plant stand Mrs and +dies Badley (Lincoln), silver cream jug Dr T ld Fox (Batley), cut glass sent spray, toilette bottles and water-3elour painting Mr Sidney Martin (Liverpool, brass lamp Mr and Mrs James Mason, iron and copper fire suite and drawing-room chair Mr John Houghton, set of bowls Mr and Mrs F R Plerpoint travelling rug</p>
        <p>Mr and Mrs Shaw Green and Family. pair silver muffineers Bev H G Roberts, M.A., Bible and gong Dr and Ildra Peacock°, silver bon-on dish Miss Andrews, hearth-rug Mr and Mrs Bell (Nottingham), silver egg stand Mrs Carter, silver tea spoons Mr W Roberts. wedding slippers Mrs 0 Harbottle, Deniton tea pot Mr Thomas Honghton. biscuit jar Miss May Houghton, sliver jam stand Miss Bea Houghton, silver toast reek Mr and Mrs W Harris-Wright inlaid writing cabinet Mrs Ford, knitted bed-lace and d'o vleys Mrs Thornton. drawn linen tray cloth Mrs Harris, occasional chair . .</p>
        <p>Mr and Mrs W Newton, revolving newspaper rack Miss Amy Lacy (London), case of silver salts Mr and Mrs Frank Harris, copper and wrought iron cake stand</p>
        <p>Mrs Worthington. glass and silver biscuit jar Mr wad Mrs J Gandy, case of silver salts</p>
        <p>The Misses Gandy, painted satin table centre and d'eyleys The Misses Mason, breakfast cruet</p>
        <p>Mrs James Warburton, table Rhea Mr Bert Rlggall, marmalade jar</p>
        <p>Idles Lee, breed 'fork</p>
        <p>Ml5B Mainwarlog, fruit dish</p>
        <p>Mr and Mrs W Ifinnett, aheque The Misses Bennett (Lyndhurst), picture Miss London, water-colour painting Mrs Fry (Didaeury), Indian cushion Mr It If Daintith, complete set of househol I brashes Mies Ooklestou (Southport) calendar for 1900 . . _</p>
        <p>Mr and Mrs Swinton, trinicst sat</p>
        <p>-- -- The Misses Hammond (Poulton-le-Fylde) drawn-linen too sloth. _</p>
