        <p>Mr and Mrs Harry Houghton, brass tire irons. Mr and Mrs 3 W Houghton, case silver serviette rings Mr and Mrs T Birchall, silver cake basket</p>
        <p>Mrs Royston and family, brass and copper spirit kettle Mr and Mrs R Richardson, ease of silver salts</p>
        <p>Miss and Mr J B RichardsOn, silver fern pot Mr John Jackson, nissnialad• jar</p>
        <p>The Chairman observed that the nest business down for consideration was a motien by Mr E Gatley that he would make a statement as to the expenditure of the Council acting as the Burial Board for the district and would move a resolution if deemed advisable. He found that it was not quite in order but the mattcr could be dealt with by declaring the subject a " matter ot urgency," when upon any tea members voting for it it could be dealt with.</p>
        <p>After some considerable time had been spent in devis- ing a means for admitting the subject to discussion, on the motion of Mr. G. Hewitt, seconded by Mr. J. Wilson the standing orders were suspended and the matter opened. Mr. Gatley stated that ha would rather content him- self under the circumstances by asking the Secretary (Mr E Marshall) to read the following documents 18th March, 1896 "—My lor 14 and gentlemen.—Yoer lordships will be aware that on the 28th March, 1895, ltbe Runcorn Urban Dierict Council took over the powers and liberties of the Runcorn Burial Board. This Council had recently had oceasion to negotiate some loans for the purpose of re-paying existing loans. While this was on hand they took the opportunity of ascertain- ing how the sinking fund stood, and it seems to them that the late Burial Board have not made sufficient pro- visions for the repayment of debt. lam directed by my Council to furnish you with the folowing particu- lars. It would appear that the following sums were borrowed on capital expenditure. I give these figures with considerable reserve for the following reasons. No capital account has been kept by the Secretary to the Burial Board, nor does the registrar of mortgages distingnieb between original borrowings and sums barrowei for the purpose of paying 'off existing loans --15th December, 1858, £500; 29th December, 1858, £3,050 ; 29:h December, 1858, £950; 3rd October, 1859, £l.OOO ; 14th June, 1860, £250 ; 30th March, 1884, £2,000; total £8.250. Against this I may state that only the sum of £6,000 has been sanc- tioned by the Vestry. I understand that the period of repayment is 50 years. The debit at present amounts to £4,780. As only about 13 years have to run of this period the debit ought to have been muck smaller if proper provision had been made for payments to the Sinking Fund, The Secretary of the late Burial Board explains to me that some of the money set apart to the Sinking Fund was used for capital expenditure instead of obtaining fresh loans for such purposes. My Council direct me to make application to your Lordship to sanc- tion an extension of time during which this debt may be paid, as it is felt that it would be a hardship on the ratepayers to ask them to pay off during the short period of 13 years the debt which ought to have been extin- guished many years ago. One reason why I would strongly urge this upon your lordships is that the trade of the town is in a deplorable depressed condition. Ycu may judge that this is so when I say that with a popu- lation of 19,000 there are 700 empty bNises. My Coun- cil will be pleased to hear that you can see your way to grant this application. I have the honour to be my lords and gentlemen,your obedient servant,E.MAR4HALL, secretary. The Lords Commissioners of Her Majesty's Treasury, Whitehall, S W;" _ _</p>
        <p>:'Town Hall, Runcorn, 3rd October, 1699.—Mr. Coun- cillor Gatley.—Dear sir,—l beg to furnish you with particulars as to the Burial Boards Loans as requeited. I enclose a copy of the letter addressed to the Local Government Board on the 12th of March, 1896. This will explain in general terms, why the precepts on the Overseers of the Poor are larger now than formerly. I may say that a letter came from the Local Government Board in reply to to one I enclose to the effect that they had no power to grant an extension of time beyond the statutery limit of 50 years The amount put into sinking fund /salt of the Poor Rate by the Barial Board ought to have been £165 per annum. Instead of that amount a sum was said which averaged only £96 a year, a deficiency of about £2480. This sum of £2480 has to be found by the Overseers in addition to the annual sum of £165 during the next 14 years, £330 has now to be provided whereas the amount was only £96 a year previously te this Council taking over the powers of the Burial Board. Had the Council not increasel the amount to be paid into sinking fund in all probability the District Auditor would have discovered the discrepancy. Had he not done so, however, the Council would have run the risk of being called upon by Ithe Local Government Board to make a precept on the overseers at the end of the 50 years that is about 10 years from this date, to provide a sum of £2,480 a risk which evidently the Council did not care to take. I hall be glad to furnish you with any further particulars you may require. Yours faithfully. E. Mensimx." Town Hall, Runcorn, October 3, 1899.—Dearlsir,—In accordance with your request, I send you the following list of works beyond maintenance which have been done at the cemetery from January 1896 un to date. Taking down and rebuilding spire. &amp;c.. £37 ; inserting 80 gullies £4O ; heating both chapel and church, £44; painting house rails, and church and chapel £43 174 6e; total. £164 17s 6d. Yours obediently, James Wilding. Mr Councillor Gatley. Upon Mr. Marshall complying Mr. Gatley stated he did not wish to supplement that statement except by asking Mr. Imison one question, with the permission of the chair. Had he (Mr. Imison) ever voted against any of that expenditure ? Mr. Imison : No.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gatley continuing said if he moved any motion it would be that that statement be printed with the min- utes of the Ceuleil.</p>
        <p>W arrington.—Printed and Published by Join( W.u.scEg Co.. at The Examiner General Printing Works, iSewsl37- street. and published by them at the same place and also at The Examiner Offices, Victoria-read, WIDNES; 42,GForge- street, Sr. HELENS, and 3, Market-street, HARLEsToSIN lit the County of Lancaster and also at High-street, Huscosst in the County of Chester.-00Tonza 6, 1899,</p>
      </div>
