        <p>Mr and Mrs C fl Stovin (Alford), sliver cream jug Mr and Mrs J White, silver sugar sitter Mr W Houghton, ease silver nautnears and mustard pot Miss Jackson, silver anger basin and tongs Mrs John Smith, copper crumb brush and tray Mr and Mrs Rzar-I, case of nut cracks Mr and Mrs Percy Grange, silver and glass flower stand Miss Raglestield, framed embroidered text Mrs Adamson, cushion Mr and Mrs Bradford, silver sugar tongs Councillor and Mrs A Bennett, silver mounted toilet bottles Mrs Dean, set of jugs Mrs London, set of d'oyleys Dolly London, hair-pin box Mr and Mrs Hughes (Clrwea), a nor and cream stand The Misses Br entnall (Nottingham), handkerchief case Mr and Mrs Hutchins, pearl-handled bread fork Mr and Mrs H Hatton, revolving book case The Misses A B and N Birtles, marmalade jar Mrs Taylor (Charlton), cake knife Mr and Mrs James Houghton, eider down quilt Members of thi. Wesleyan Church Choir, case of silver tea spoons and sugar tongs Mrs Glover, pair of ornaments Maid at Pear Tree House, set of glass dislass Mies Patchett (London), Swiss clock Mr Wilfred Dupre (Jersey), lounge chair Mrs R Bennett, cushions and toilet tidies Dr Robinson (Altrinobans), hall brush rack Dr Alnscow (Wigan), silver and Wedgewood butter dish Mr and Mrs Gill, sliver cake basket The Misses and Messrs Warburton (Rewsey), pair o f ornaments Annie (Maid at Springfield), pair flower vases</p>
        <p>RUNCORN ITRBA.N DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of the above Council was held at the Town Hall, Runcorn, on Thursday morning, when there were present :—Meslns. F Wiswall (chair- man),D Bisbrown, S Taylor, M Sweetman, E Imison, S Walton, W Handley, J W Lightburn, George Hewitt, R Posnett, Thomas Jones. J Wilson, E Gatley, J Grice, UH Kirkham, E Marshall (secretary), PA Lake (law clerk), Joseph Wilding (surveyor), and Dr McDou- gall (medical officer.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>FUEL FOR THE WATIRWORKS.</head>
        <p>FUEL FOR THE WATIRWORKS.</p>
        <p>The Water Committee recommended that the tender ear Simpson, Davies &amp; Sons, to supply 600 tons of Bridgewater Trustees Worsley Tip Slack, to be delivered in such quantities as may be required within eight mor,ths at the Top Locks, Runcorn, or the Runcorn Railway Station in the event of the Bridgewater Canal being frczen, be accepted, and that the seal of the Coun- cil be affixed to the contract.</p>
        <p>The foregoing minutes were adopted on the pro- position of Mr. James Pritchard.</p>
        <p>TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The above committee made the following recommend- ations :—That a further sum of £lO be eirpendA in the purchase of science apparatus for the day school ; also that casts be purchased for the Arl School, estimated cost RS, and that the annual report of the committee be approved and adopted.</p>
        <p>Mr. J.Wilson seconded and thought it was only fair to put that side of the question with the facts of the case betore the public. They were certainly as clear as any of the others that had been placed before them. Be said that withofe, wishing to use any hard termq, Mr. S Taylor stated'he had been spoken to by a num- ber of people who had had the bare facts before them as stated by Mr. Imison, and it was only lair that tbe. ratepayers should also have the other side. They knew that one thing was certain that since toe Council had taken over the Burial Board there was an increased expenditure, for the money that the Burial Board were lax about was being paid up. He was laying the blame upon nobody, but as a member of the Burial Board which used to do its work in a routine manner month by month, he was willing to take his share of the blame.</p>
        <p>Mr. Handley adhered to his original observation that there was nothing to answer in all that Mr. Imison bad said. He had simply eupplied the very best answer to the question himself. In a lesgthy criticism Mr. Handley pointed out that Mr. Imison h ad first of all accused them of extravagance and then told them be knew perfectly well that there bad been extraordinary expenditures. Then he went on to say that there had been a very serious increase in ordinary expenditure, and that in the old days the cemetery looked well, but that now it looked better. Thus he (Mr. Handley) took those 3 reasons to be the best answer to Mr. Imigora whose chief grwvance seemed to be that in the old days the cemetery was managed well, while since it had come under the control of the Council the mismanage- ment had begun. Mr. Imison objected to the use of the expression mis-management by Mr. Handley,as he had not implied that.</p>
        <p>Mr. Inaison pointed out that the whole of his argu- ment had been aimed at showing the ratepayers the reason for the increase in the Poor Rate. At the close of his reply he moved an amendment that the figures supplied by himself be added to the state- memt supplied by Mr. Gatley. This was seconded and eventually carried by the Chairrnak's casting vote. The meeting then closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-06_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>VENTILATION OF THE SCHOOL BOARD ROOM.</head>
        <p>THE VENTILATION OF THE SCHOOL BOARD ROOM.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee recommended that the ventil- ation of the School Board Room be improved in accord- ance with the suggestion made by the Surveyor at an estimated cost of £6; the tender of Mr. John Sefton for the erection of a strong room in accordance with the Surveyor's plan and specification be accepted, and that tho tender of Messrs. Dowson, Taylor and Co., for the supply of two armoured sliding doors for the strong room, delivered at the Town Hall Runcorn, be accepted. Mr. Imison proposed that the minutes of the Finance Committee be received and adopted. Mr. T. Jones seconded,and the resolution was unanim- ously carried. . CHAIRMAN'S COMMUNICATIONS.</p>
        <p>Circulars were received from the Local Government Board with regard to the Baths and Washbouses Act, 1899, and the Small Dwellings Acquisition Act, 1899, by the Chairman who submitted them to the Council ftr comment.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. A. Lake (law clerk) observed that with regard to the Baths and Washhouse Act, 1899, the Council having no baths, the Act would not at present be appli- cable to them. With regard to the Small Dwellings Acquisition Act it was another matter. Having explained its workings be said the Council could have no power under its provisions unless they passed a resolution. It was open to any Urban District Council to adopt it within their own district. The object of the Act was to enable householders to borrow money from the Council so as to be able to purchase their own dwellings. The Council was to borrow the money in the first instance, but could only charge I per cent, more for the leans to the ratepayers who borrowed, so thst the figure would be a very much more advantageous one than borrowing. money privately.</p>
        <p>_ On the motion of Mr. G. Hewitt, monde.' by Mr. Grice, it was decided to refer the matter to the General Purposes Committee to report.</p>
        <p>TUE CEMETERY COMMITTEE ON ITS DIGNITY .-MR. IMISON'S</p>
        <p>CRITICISMS.</p>
        <p>2 45 p.m. The remaining inhabitants of Newcastle held a meet- ing to-day, at which they decided not to attempt a de- fence of the town in case of a Boer advance in force against it. People are crowding the trains for Lady- smith, every coal truck being called) into requisition to convey the fugitives away from the town. PINTERMARITSBURO, WZDNIESDAY, 3 40 P.M. The Orange Free State is commandeering British sub - jects with the avowed intention of sending there to defend the Basuto border. Several murders and rob- beries have been committed on the Rand. Intense indig- nation everywhere prevails regarding the Boer bar- barity to passengers through the Transvaal, especially to those coming to Natal. Woman and children were denied water for a spice of thirty hours. Rifles were fired over the opm trucks conveying passengers by drunken Boers, and delicate ladies were assailed with obscene abuse and threats. At almost every stition the field cornet expressed his longing to shoot everyone that spoke English. NO OFFICIAL CONFIRMATION. No official confirmation can be got of the reported invasion of Natal.</p>
        <p>EXPLOSION AT BRUNNER, MOND'S CHEMICAL WORKS.</p>
        <p>_ At half past nine yesterday morning an extraordinary accident happened at the chemical works of Messrs Brunner, Mond, and Co., Winningt-m, near Northwich. Joseph Isherwood, shiftman, of Warrington-road, was deputed to clean out an empty sewer in the works. He entered with a lamp, and immediately there was an explesion of gas. He was burned en the face and arms, the injuries covering a large area. He was removed to the Victoria Infirmary. LATEST LOCAL WEATHER FORECAST. The following forecast of the weather for the ensuing twenty-four hours In the north-west of Eagland was issued at the Meteorological Office at three o'clock yea- day afternoon :</p>
      </div>
