        <p>In Lubberiand they give t ern half-a-crown a thy for sleeping This is good news for th? loafing Brigade, and for People who don't like working between meals. Idling and thif ving generally go together. Spurgeon used to say of his Boys, "Better up to their eyes in mud and mu chief thin j in the Loif- ing Brigade." An idle man would hardly hold up his basin if it rained por ridge; f:e's like Micawber "always waiting for something to turn up," and if it doesQ't turn up he blainPs Smith, Brown, Jones, Rocinson, his Wife, Bai rial, Providence, anything and everybody rather than himself. A holy thrashing with a bit of venom in it would prove a capital physia to these Idlebones. Page Woodcock of Lincoln, is far from being an idler. He's busy always, urging the Blitish public to once test his famous Wind Pills, the World-famed cure fcr Indigestic n, Wind on the Stomach, Liver Complaints, Sick Healache, CoAiveness, Nervous Debility, Palpitation of the Heart, Biliousness,&amp;c., knowing that if tested all praise will be given. A Lady said to the Proprietor only a few days ago," I have used your Wind Pills for 11. years I suffer from a form of Indigestion and Wind, which, giving me a revere rill n near the heart, makes me imagine I suffer from Heart Disease. Nothing relieves me so quickly as your Wird Pills. I would not be without them on any recount." The lady's name is Elia theth Hylton, 7, Clarkt,on's Row, Lincoln. Page Woodcock's Wind Pills being purely Vege- table,Tasteless, and Mild and Tonic in their action, may be taken with perfect safety by the most deli- cate of either sex. Children may aafely take them in reduced doses. Page Woodcock's Wind Pills are sold by all Medicine Vendors at is 41 and 2.3 91 ; post free Lot price by Page Woodcock, Lincoln.</p>
